592186212749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212750 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212751 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212752 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212753 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212754 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212749 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212749 1077 "selection" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212749 1080 #uuid "d322b9cb-fda8-5393-8035-30d5adc0552a" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212750 1081 "2a + a\\sqrt{a}" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212750 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212750 1084 #uuid "8f64a18a-2211-5b14-a4b7-94763314bc19" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212751 1081 "\\frac{8a^{2} - 4a^{2}\\sqrt{a}}{4 - a}" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212751 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212751 1084 #uuid "8fdfb028-be29-5489-bd84-ae65a7ab2961" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212752 1081 "\\frac{8a^{2} - 4a^{3}}{2 - 4a}" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212752 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212752 1084 #uuid "00d8d442-73e3-5938-aa94-a6e1547ea010" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212753 1081 "\\frac{8a^{2}\\sqrt{a} - 4a^{3}}{2 - 4a}" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212753 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212753 1084 #uuid "3b19ba57-07b5-536a-b491-f5faf0bda140" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212754 1081 "\\frac{4a^{2} - 4a^{2}\\sqrt{a}}{a - 4}" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212754 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212754 1084 #uuid "41ffda20-fa97-59a5-82a0-1ddc04598b99" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212755 1069 "e-18849" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212755 1071 "A health clinic administers $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ of a unit of one vaccine and $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ of a unit of another vaccine per patient. Let $a$ be the number of patients receiving the first vaccine and $b$ the number receiving the second. If the clinic used $36$ units of vaccine in total, which equation models this situation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212755 1072 #uuid "bce2dcaa-4a7c-54e8-a378-aa8b7a68b8ee" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212755 1074 "We are told that $a$ represents the number of patients receiving the first vaccine, and $b$ represents the number receiving the second.\n\n- Each patient receiving the first vaccine uses $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ of a unit, so the total amount used from that vaccine is $\\dfrac{2}{5}a$.\n- Each patient receiving the second vaccine uses $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ of a unit, so the total amount used from that vaccine is $\\dfrac{3}{10}b$.\n\nTo find the total amount of vaccine used, we add these two expressions together:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}a + \\dfrac{3}{10}b\n$$\n\nThis total must equal the clinic’s total usage of $36$ units, so we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}a + \\dfrac{3}{10}b = 36\n$$\n\nThis is the equation that models the situation." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212756 1069 "e-18850" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212756 1071 "A smoothie shop uses $0.75$ pounds of strawberries and $1.2$ pounds of bananas in each batch. The equation $0.75s + 1.2b = 24$ models the total pounds of fruit used in $s$ batches with strawberries and $b$ batches with bananas. If $s = 8$, how many banana batches were made?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212756 1072 #uuid "b9213af5-b63e-5754-843e-3c4da04736ef" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212756 1074 "We are told that $s = 8$. We will substitute this value into the equation and solve for $b$.\n\nFirst, we start with the original equation:\n\n$$\n0.75s + 1.2b = 24\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $s = 8$ and solve the resulting equation for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.75(8) + 1.2b &= 24 \\\\\n6 + 1.2b &= 24 \\\\\n1.2b &= 24 - 6 \\\\\n1.2b &= 18 \\\\\nb &= \\frac{18}{1.2} \\\\\nb &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $15$ banana batches were made." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212757 1069 "e-18855" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212757 1071 "Solve the equation $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}x - 1\\right)\\left(2x + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)(x - 7) = 0$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212757 1072 #uuid "2a1030f3-4ecd-58f9-acae-aeb306b2708d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212757 1074 "We apply the zero product rule, which tells us that if the product of several expressions is zero, then at least one of the expressions must be zero.\n\nSo, we set each factor equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}x - 1 = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad 2x + \\dfrac{1}{4} = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad x - 7 = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve each of these equations:\n\n- For the $1$st factor:\n$\\dfrac{3}{2}x - 1 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{3}{2}x = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = \\dfrac{2}{3}$\n- For the $2$nd factor:\n$2x + \\dfrac{1}{4} = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad 2x = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = -\\dfrac{1}{8}$\n- For the $3$rd factor:\n$x - 7 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 7$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are the following:\n\n$$\nx = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-\\dfrac{1}{8}},\\ \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}},\\ \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212758 1069 "e-18856" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212758 1071 "Solve the equation $x^2(x + \\sqrt{6}) = 0$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212758 1072 #uuid "6c7a1bf0-51ee-5253-83e5-9ed2c8cf230b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212758 1074 "We apply the zero product rule, which tells us that if the product of several expressions is zero, then at least one of the expressions must be zero.\n\nSo, we set each factor equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad x + \\sqrt{6} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve each of these equations:\n\n- For the $1$st factor:\n$x^2 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 0$\n- For the $2$nd factor:\n$x + \\sqrt{6} = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = -\\sqrt{6}$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are the following:\n\n$$\nx = -\\sqrt{6},\\ 0\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212759 1069 "e-18857" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212759 1071 "Find a cubic equation with a leading coefficient of $3$ that has solutions $x = -1$, $x = \\dfrac{4}{3}$, and $x = 6$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212759 1072 #uuid "586178ab-497c-52ba-b4df-b84a77fccf64" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212759 1074 "The given solutions $x = -1$,\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nand $x = 6$ are the solutions to the following equations:\n\n- $x + 1 = 0$\n- $x - \\dfrac{4}{3} = 0$\n- $x - 6 = 0$\n\nSo, according to the zero product rule, they are also the solutions of the product\n\n$$\n(x + 1)\\left(x - \\dfrac{4}{3}\\right)(x - 6) = 0\n$$\n\nTo eliminate fractions, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(x + 1)(x - \\frac{4}{3})(x - 6) &= 3 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n(3x - 4)(x + 1)(x - 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x - 4)(x + 1)(x - 6) &= (3x - 4)((x + 1)(x - 6)) \\\\\n&= (3x - 4)(x^{2} - 6x + x - 6) \\\\\n&= (3x - 4)(x^{2} - 5x - 6) \\\\\n&= 3x(x^{2} - 5x - 6) - 4(x^{2} - 5x - 6) \\\\\n&= 3x^{3} - 15x^{2} - 18x - 4x^{2} + 20x + 24 \\\\\n&= 3x^{3} - 19x^{2} + 2x + 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a cubic equation that has the required solutions is\n\n$$\n3x^3 - 19x^2 + 2x + 24 = 0\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212760 1069 "e-18859" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212760 1071 "The following equation is true for all values of $x$.\n\n$mx^2 + 5x + 4 = 2x^2 + 5x + 4$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $m$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212760 1072 #uuid "343c42bc-2a8b-5979-9a21-accb89835444" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212760 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x$, the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the quadratic terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $m$. On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $2$.\n\nSo, we set\n$m = 2$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212761 1069 "e-18860" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212761 1071 "The equation\n\n$ax^2 + bx + 12 = 7x^2 + 2x + 12$\nis true for all values of $x$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212761 1072 #uuid "0538ef73-5254-5d99-b709-b08c9ff89c11" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212761 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x$, the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a$, and on the right-hand side it is $7$. So, we have\n$a = 7$.\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b$, and on the right-hand side it is $2$. So, we have\n$b = 2$.\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 7 + 2 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212762 1069 "e-18861" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212762 1071 "The equation\n$x^2 + 9x + 18 = (x + 3)(x + k)$\nis true for all values of $x$, where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212762 1072 #uuid "802b17c9-3c7d-58b9-b7ee-8632f4f1cd13" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212762 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x$, the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the factored form on the right-hand side must expand to match the left-hand side.\n\nLet's expand the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)(x + k) &= x^{2} + kx + 3x + 3k \\\\\n&= x^{2} + (k + 3)x + 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the expanded form to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 9x + 18 = x^2 + (k + 3)x + 3k\n$$\n\nEquating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 &= 3k \\\\\n6 &= k \\\\\nk &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $k = 6$ produces the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk + 3 &= 6 + 3 \\\\\n&= 9✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side. So, our solution is valid." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212763 1069 "e-18865" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212763 1071 "Factor the following expression:\n$(2y - 5)(y - 4) - (2y - 5)(3y + 2)$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212763 1072 #uuid "c9b07a13-41bd-5d96-90dd-22b20553b0f4" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212763 1074 "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(2y - 5)(y - 4) - (2y - 5)(3y + 2)$.\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(2y - 5)$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(2y - 5)\\left[(y - 4) - (3y + 2)\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n=(2y - 5)(y - 4 - 3y - 2); = (2y - 5)(-2y - 6)\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212764 1069 "e-18866" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212764 1071 "Factor the following expression:\n$(2y - 1)(y + 4) + 3(2y - 1)^2$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212764 1072 #uuid "c144a2bc-d426-5b66-893a-2a76e458bd4b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212764 1074 "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(2y - 1)(y + 4) + 3(2y - 1)^2$.\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(2y - 1)$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(2y - 1)\\left[(y + 4) + 3(2y - 1)\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n=(2y - 1)(y + 4 + 6y - 3); = (2y - 1)(7y + 1)\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212765 1069 "e-18867" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212765 1071 "Factor the following expression:\n$(4y+1)(8y-7) - (7-8y)(6y-5)$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212765 1072 #uuid "2e8d2947-97fd-59f5-a375-192e72326c8c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212765 1074 "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(4y+1)(8y-7) - (7-8y)(6y-5)$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first term in this expression contains a factor of $(8y-7)$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of $(7-8y)$.\n\nThese factors differ only by the sign:\n\n$$\n7-8y = -(8y-7)\n$$\n\nSo, we first make the factors the same by factoring out $-1$ in the second term:\n$(4y+1)(8y-7) + (8y-7)(6y-5)$\n\nNow, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(8y-7)\\left[(4y+1) + (6y-5)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n(4y+1) + (6y-5) = 10y - 4\n$$\n\nSo the factored form is\n$(8y-7)(10y-4)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212766 1069 "e-18868" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212766 1071 "Consider the expression\n$(y - 1)^3(y + 2) + (y + 4)(y - 1)^2$.\n\nThis expression can be written in the form\n$(y - 1)^2\\cdot g(y)$.\nFind the polynomial $g(y)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212766 1072 #uuid "bcace49f-f7ed-5478-9afc-7037d3839b05" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212766 1074 "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(y - 1)^3(y + 2) + (y + 4)(y - 1)^2$.\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(y - 1)^2$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(y - 1)^2\\left[(y - 1)(y + 2) + (y + 4)\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n=(y - 1)^{2}[(y - 1)(y + 2) + (y + 4)]; = (y - 1)^{2}[y^{2} + y - 2 + y + 4]; = (y - 1)^{2} \\cdot (y^{2} + 2y + 2)_(⏟)_(g(y))\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(y) = y^2 + 2y + 2\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212767 1069 "e-18871" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212767 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{64}{x + 3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{80}{x - 10}\\right)}$, where $x \\neq -3$ and $x \\neq 10$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212767 1072 #uuid "15f06a29-96d2-5fb2-af1b-d79dc087b251" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212767 1074 "First, we write the fraction using division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{64}{x + 3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{80}{x - 10}\\right)} = \\dfrac{64}{x + 3} \\div \\dfrac{80}{x - 10}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that division is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{x + 3} \\div \\dfrac{80}{x - 10} = \\dfrac{64}{x + 3} \\cdot \\dfrac{x - 10}{80}\n$$\n\nNow we multiply the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{64}{x + 3} \\cdot \\frac{x - 10}{80} &= (64 \\cdot (x - 10))/((x + 3) \\cdot 80) \\\\\n&= (64(x - 10))/(80(x + 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(64(x - 10))/(80(x + 3)) &= (16 \\cdot 4(x - 10))/(16 \\cdot 5(x + 3)) \\\\\n&= (4(x - 10))/(5(x + 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212768 1069 "e-18872" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212768 1071 "Simplify\n$\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{7}{x - 6} - \\dfrac{4}{x + 1}}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212768 1072 #uuid "2b4d2c61-d61d-56cb-a43f-9b4820a38bbd" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212768 1074 "We begin by rewriting the denominator as a single fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{x - 6} - \\frac{4}{x + 1} &= \\frac{7}{x - 6} \\cdot \\frac{x + 1}{x + 1} - \\frac{4}{x + 1} \\cdot \\frac{x - 6}{x - 6} \\\\\n&= (7(x + 1))/((x - 6)(x + 1)) - (4(x - 6))/((x - 6)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (7(x + 1) - 4(x - 6))/((x - 6)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (7x + 7 - 4x + 24)/((x - 6)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (3x + 31)/((x - 6)(x + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{7}{x - 6} - \\dfrac{4}{x + 1}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3x + 31}{(x - 6)(x + 1)}\\right)}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the fraction using division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3x + 31}{(x - 6)(x + 1)}\\right)} = 1 \\div \\dfrac{3x + 31}{(x - 6)(x + 1)}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that division is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 \\div (3x + 31)/((x - 6)(x + 1)) &= 1 \\cdot ((x - 6)(x + 1))/(3x + 31) \\\\\n&= ((x - 6)(x + 1))/(3x + 31)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212769 1069 "e-18874" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212769 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{4x - 8}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{x - 2}{7}\\right)}$, where $x \\neq 2$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212769 1072 #uuid "be87f21b-0f2e-57a1-9232-02dfbc944b09" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212769 1074 "First, we write the fraction using division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{4x - 8}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{x - 2}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{4x - 8}{9} \\div \\dfrac{x - 2}{7}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that dividing by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying by its reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x - 8}{9} \\div \\dfrac{x - 2}{7} = \\dfrac{4x - 8}{9} \\cdot \\dfrac{7}{x - 2}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n4x - 8 = 4(x - 2)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we multiply the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4x - 8}{9} \\cdot \\frac{7}{x - 2} &= (4(x - 2))/(9) \\cdot \\frac{7}{x - 2} \\\\\n&= (4(x - 2) \\cdot 7)/(9(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= (28(x - 2))/(9(x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(28(x - 2))/(9(x - 2)) &= (28(x - 2))/(9(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{28}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212770 1069 "e-18875" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212770 1071 "Simplify the following expression:\n$\\dfrac{5-q}{3p+4q}-\\dfrac{6p+8pq}{18p^2+24pq}$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212770 1072 #uuid "268f0827-8645-5ef5-887d-81b62a628159" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212770 1074 "Notice that we can factor the polynomial in the second denominator as\n\n$$\n18p^2+24pq = 6p(3p+4q)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5-q}{3p+4q}-\\dfrac{6p+8pq}{18p^2+24pq} = \\dfrac{5-q}{3p+4q}-\\dfrac{6p+8pq}{6p(3p+4q)}\n$$\n\nNow, we can put each rational expression over a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the first fraction by $6p$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5 - q}{3p + 4q} - (6p + 8pq)/(6p(3p + 4q)) &= \\frac{6p}{6p} \\cdot \\frac{5 - q}{3p + 4q} - (6p + 8pq)/(6p(3p + 4q)) \\\\\n&= (6p(5 - q))/(6p(3p + 4q)) - (6p + 8pq)/(6p(3p + 4q))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6p(5 - q))/(6p(3p + 4q)) - (6p + 8pq)/(6p(3p + 4q)) &= (6p(5 - q) - (6p + 8pq))/(6p(3p + 4q)) \\\\\n&= (30p - 6pq - 6p - 8pq)/(6p(3p + 4q)) \\\\\n&= (24p - 14pq)/(6p(3p + 4q)) \\\\\n&= (2p(12 - 7q))/(6p(3p + 4q)) \\\\\n&= (12 - 7q)/(3(3p + 4q))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212771 1069 "e-18876" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212771 1071 "Simplify the following expression:\n$\\dfrac{5}{2x^2-11x+5}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5}$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212771 1072 #uuid "1ae8c973-aef8-5913-b319-d424adcab806" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212771 1074 "First, we factor the denominator in the first term, $2x^2-11x+5$.\n\nTo factor this quadratic expression, we need to find two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 5=10\n$$\n\nand add to $-11$. These are $-10$ and $-1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 11x + 5 &= 2x^{2}- 10x - x_(⏟)_(-11x) + 5 \\\\\n&= (2x^{2} - 10x) + (-x + 5) \\\\\n&= 2x(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) \\\\\n&= (x - 5)(2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2x^2-11x+5}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5} = \\dfrac{5}{(x-5)(2x-1)}+\\dfrac{7x}{x-5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can put each rational expression over a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the second fraction by $(2x-1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{7x}{x - 5} &= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + \\frac{2x - 1}{2x - 1} \\cdot \\frac{7x}{x - 5} \\\\\n&= (5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) + (7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) &= (5 + 7x(2x - 1))/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (5 + 14x^{2} - 7x)/((x - 5)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (14x^{2} - 7x + 5)/((x - 5)(2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212772 1069 "e-18878" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212772 1071 "Simplify the following expression:\n$\\dfrac{2y-5}{4-x}-\\dfrac{3}{x^2-4x}$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212772 1072 #uuid "5460d77d-e90c-5440-88b2-956485e8ea86" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212772 1074 "Notice that we can factor the polynomial in the second denominator as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4x &= x(x - 4) \\\\\n&=-x(4 - x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2y - 5}{4 - x} - \\frac{3}{x^{2} - 4x} &= \\frac{2y - 5}{4 - x} - (3)/(-x(4 - x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2y - 5}{4 - x} + (3)/(x(4 - x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can put each rational expression over a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the first fraction by $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2y - 5}{4 - x} + (3)/(x(4 - x)) &= \\frac{x}{x} \\cdot \\frac{2y - 5}{4 - x} + (3)/(x(4 - x)) \\\\\n&= (x(2y - 5))/(x(4 - x)) + (3)/(x(4 - x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x(2y - 5))/(x(4 - x)) + (3)/(x(4 - x)) &= (x(2y - 5) + 3)/(x(4 - x)) \\\\\n&= (2xy - 5x + 3)/(x(4 - x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212773 1069 "e-18879" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212773 1071 "Simplify the expression $b^{3/4}\\left(b^{5/4} c + b^{1/4} c^2 \\right)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212773 1072 #uuid "d53ca797-3be2-56cc-a930-c92e9efff51e" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212773 1074 "First, we use the distributive law, as shown below:\n\n$$\nb^{3/4}\\left(b^{5/4} c + b^{1/4} c^2 \\right) = b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{5/4} c + b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{1/4} c^2\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the terms by combining the rules of exponents with algebraic expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb^{3/4} \\cdot b^{5/4}c + b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{1/4}c^{2} &= (b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{5/4})c + (b^{3/4} \\cdot b^{1/4})c^{2} \\\\\n&= b^{(3/4 + 5/4)}c + b^{(3/4 + 1/4)}c^{2} \\\\\n&= b^{8/4}c + b^{4/4}c^{2} \\\\\n&= b^{2}c + bc^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212774 1069 "e-18880" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212774 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{p^7 q + p q^3}{p^4}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212774 1072 #uuid "9248fd6c-1873-5688-9fbc-2fba0e732a3a" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212774 1074 "First, we rewrite the denominator using negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p^7 q + p q^3}{p^4} = \\left(p^7 q + p q^3 \\right) p^{-4}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the distributive law, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\left(p^7 q + p q^3 \\right) p^{-4} = p^7 q \\cdot p^{-4} + p q^3 \\cdot p^{-4}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the terms by combining the rules of exponents with algebraic expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{7}q \\cdot p^{-4} + pq^{3} \\cdot p^{-4} &= (p^{7} \\cdot p^{-4})q + (p \\cdot p^{-4})q^{3} \\\\\n&= p^{7 - 4}q + p^{1 - 4}q^{3} \\\\\n&= p^{3}q + p^{-3}q^{3} \\\\\n&= p^{3}q + \\frac{q^{3}}{p^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212775 1069 "e-18881" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212775 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{v^{15/8} u^2 + v^{5/4} u}{v^{7/8}}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212775 1072 #uuid "117e74c6-ff7e-5660-aba6-9a1eeda3f671" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212775 1074 "First, we rewrite the denominator using negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{v^{15/8} u^2 + v^{5/4} u}{v^{7/8}} = \\left(v^{15/8} u^2 + v^{5/4} u \\right) v^{-7/8}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the distributive law, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\left(v^{15/8} u^2 + v^{5/4} u \\right) v^{-7/8} = v^{15/8} u^2 \\cdot v^{-7/8} + v^{5/4} u \\cdot v^{-7/8}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the terms by combining the rules of exponents with algebraic expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv^{15/8}u^{2} \\cdot v^{-7/8} + v^{5/4}u \\cdot v^{-7/8} &= (v^{15/8} \\cdot v^{-7/8})u^{2} + (v^{5/4} \\cdot v^{-7/8})u \\\\\n&= v^{15/8 - 7/8}u^{2} + v^{10/8 - 7/8}u \\\\\n&= v^{8/8}u^{2} + v^{3/8}u \\\\\n&= vu^{2} + v^{3/8}u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write fractional exponents as radicals:\n\n$$\nv u^2 + v^{3/8} u = v u^2 + u \\sqrt[8]{v^3}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212776 1069 "e-18882" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212776 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^6}+\\sqrt{x^3y^2}}{\\sqrt{x^6}}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212776 1072 #uuid "267faf02-e034-5c4e-bd11-0d344c32058f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212776 1074 "First, we write radicals as fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{x^{6}} + \\sqrt{x^{3}y^{2}}}{\\sqrt{x^{6}}} &= (x^{6/2} + (x^{3}y^{2})^{1/2})/(x^{6/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{3} + x^{3 \\cdot 1/2}y^{2 \\cdot 1/2}}{x^{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{3} + x^{3/2}y^{1}}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the denominator using negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3+x^{3/2}y}{x^3}=\\left(x^3+x^{3/2}y\\right)x^{-3}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the distributive law, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^3+x^{3/2}y\\right)x^{-3}=x^3\\cdot x^{-3}+x^{3/2}y\\cdot x^{-3}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the terms by combining the rules of exponents with algebraic expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} \\cdot x^{-3} + x^{3/2}y \\cdot x^{-3} &= (x^{3} \\cdot x^{-3}) + (x^{3/2} \\cdot x^{-3})y \\\\\n&= x^{3 - 3} + x^{3/2 - 3}y \\\\\n&= x^{0} + x^{-3/2}y \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{y}{x^{3/2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write fractional exponents as radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + \\frac{y}{x^{3/2}} &= 1 + \\frac{y}{x^{3/2}} \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212777 1069 "e-18887" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212777 1071 "![](images/e7/e7ce5a43d79071b00cafaccf5d19b1d195b0a366b13ad98c4da66745e702e2a1.png)\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is inscribed in a rectangle as depicted on the diagram above. Find the area of $\\triangle ABC$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212777 1072 #uuid "1acdf719-57a3-503f-bc7c-56c2214cb0ee" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212777 1074 "![](images/60/609738e75d7d263268af65470237126cd34eb808e63c497fda68814f3cb82598.png)\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$A_{◻}|= width \\cdot height; = 9 \\cdot 6; = 54cm^{2}$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 9 \\cdot 3; = 13.5cm^{2}$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 3; = 9cm^{2}$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 6; = 9cm^{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 54 - 13.5 - 9 - 9 = 22.5 \\: \\text{cm}^2\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212778 1069 "e-18888" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212778 1071 "Find the area of the triangle with vertices $(-4,3)$, $(2,5)$, and $(-1,-2)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212778 1072 #uuid "b8bf4b67-5934-5121-aa36-0b2668abe4a6" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212778 1074 "First, note that we can inscribe our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/034a8b7157f2b08f41d5531325ecce2205413dc02410852900046fe593e017e1.png)\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$A_{◻}|= width \\cdot height; = 6 \\cdot 7; = 42$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 2; = 6$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 7; = 10.5$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5; = 7.5$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 42 - 6 - 10.5 - 7.5 = 18\n$$\n\nsquare units." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212779 1069 "e-18890" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212779 1071 "In $\\triangle DEF$, where $\\angle E$ is the right angle, we have that $\\tan D = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}$. What is the value of $\\cot F$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212779 1072 #uuid "2dfd288e-c64d-505f-9b7e-5aa8263dc4c5" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212779 1074 "![](images/06/06dc96cacfc5be1638716edea794c18c7a4b70d9e643f901b70ece01a6ba33b9.png)\n\nConsider the right triangle $\\triangle DEF$ with the right angle $\\angle E$ shown above.\n\nRecall that in such a triangle,\n\n- the tangent of the acute angle $\\angle D$ is given by\n$\\tan D = \\dfrac{\\text{opposite to D}}{\\text{adjacent to D}}$,\n- the cotangent of the acute angle $\\angle F$ is given by\n$\\cot F = \\dfrac{\\text{adjacent to F}}{\\text{opposite to F}}$.\n\nNotice that the leg that is adjacent to $\\angle F$ is the same as the leg that is opposite to $\\angle D{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{adjacent to F} = \\text{opposite to D}\n$$\n\nAlso, the leg that is opposite to $\\angle F$ is the same as the leg that is adjacent to $\\angle D{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{opposite to F} = \\text{adjacent to D}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cot F &= \\frac{adjacent to F}{opposite to F} \\\\\n&= \\frac{opposite to D}{adjacent to D} \\\\\n&= \\tan D \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212780 1069 "e-18891" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212780 1071 "Suppose in $\\triangle PRQ$, we have that $\\angle R = 90^\\circ$. Given that $\\cos P = \\dfrac{8}{17}$, what is the value of $\\cos Q$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212780 1072 #uuid "67dbe4ad-939f-5201-9a7a-fe9182dbbe31" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212780 1074 "Recall that the cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is given by the ratio of the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse.\nSo,\n\n$$\nP\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\cos P = \\dfrac{8}{17}\n$$\n\nSo, the ratio of the adjacent to $\\angle P$ leg and the hypotenuse must be\n$8: 17$.\n\nA sketch of $\\triangle PRQ$ is shown below (drawn not to scale), where $x$ denotes some positive constant.\n\n![](images/d8/d825a08a9d6defcbf24feb6dbf4d4fce53a01548d431d0370d91040ccf292e3a.png)\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we have that\n\n$$\nPR^2 + RQ^2 = PQ^2\n$$\n\nSolving this for $RQ$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nRQ &= \\sqrt{PQ^{2} - PR^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(17x)^{2} - (8x)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{289x^{2} - 64x^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{225x^{2}} \\\\\n&= 15x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add this to our sketch.\n\n![](images/1f/1fad5080c548bd399b9088cc66f6da72ed251ad1e9050b0b4aae50a50b98a89b.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos Q &= \\frac{adjacent to [math]Q[/math]}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15x}{17x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212781 1069 "e-18892" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212781 1071 "Suppose in $\\triangle ABC$, we have that $\\angle B = 90^\\circ$. Given that $\\tan A = \\sqrt{7}$, what is the value of $\\tan C$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212781 1072 #uuid "45f6ae93-8bbc-5234-b370-aab302bbf811" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212781 1074 "Recall that the tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is given by the ratio of the opposite leg to the adjacent leg.\nSo,\n\n$$\nA\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\tan A = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the ratio of the opposite to $\\angle A$ leg and the adjacent to $\\angle A$ leg must be\n$\\sqrt{7}: 1$.\n\nA sketch of $\\triangle ABC$ is shown below (drawn not to scale), where $x$ denotes some positive constant.\n\n![](images/16/1665f38e5bae34fd6b23ef48e2b23c4caa613919fe045ac2f0d01f2b98f4735a.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan C &= \\frac{opposite to [math]C[/math]}{adjacent to [math]C[/math]} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212782 1069 "e-18897" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212782 1071 "| Trays of Cookies | Families |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $3$ | $9$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $11$ | $10$ |\n| $15$ | $1$ |\n| $16$ | $4$ |\n\nFamilies were asked how many trays of cookies they baked during a holiday weekend. The table above summarizes the responses. What is the mean number of trays of cookies baked per family?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212782 1072 #uuid "6571f318-f905-50c2-972f-84b230b47954" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212782 1074 "To calculate the mean of a dataset given in a frequency table, we compute the sum of the products of each value $x$ and its corresponding frequency $f_x$, and then divide that sum by the total number of data points.\n\nWe begin by extending the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency:\n\n| Cookies ($x$) | Families ($f_x$) | $x \\cdot f_x$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $9$ | $3 \\cdot 9 = 27\\:\\:\\qquad$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $4 \\cdot 6 = 24\\:\\:\\qquad$ |\n| $11$ | $10$ | $11 \\cdot 10 = 110\\qquad$ |\n| $15$ | $1$ | $15 \\cdot 1 = 15\\:\\:\\qquad$ |\n| $16$ | $4$ | $16 \\cdot 4 = 64\\:\\:\\qquad$ |\n\nWe now calculate the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points, given by the total sum of the frequencies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{27 + 24 + 110 + 15 + 64}{9 + 6 + 10 + 1 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{240}{30} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean number of trays of cookies baked per family is $8$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212783 1069 "e-18898" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212783 1071 "A curve is defined by $y = f(x)$, where $f(x) = x^2 - 5x + 14$. If $y = g(x)$ is a vertical translation of $y = f(x)$ by $6$ units down, what is the function $g(x)$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212783 1072 #uuid "5cf3de46-fd95-5b0c-8c3b-09ca33dd0e09" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212783 1074 "We're given the curve\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 5x + 14\n$$\n\nand we wish to translate this curve by $6$ units down.\n\nFor each point on the curve $y = f(x)$, we must subtract $6$ from each $y$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on the new curve $y = g(x)$. So, the $y$-coordinates of the points on the new curve are given by\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{new}} = y - 6\n$$\n\nSubstituting here the equation of the first curve gives\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{new}} = (x^2 - 5x + 14) - 6\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\ny = (x^2 - 5x + 14) - 6\n$$\n\nFinally, collecting the like terms, we have\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 5x + 8\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2 - 5x + 8\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212784 1069 "e-18899" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212784 1071 "A line is defined by $y = f(x)$, where $f(x) = 4x + 2$. If $y = g(x)$ is a horizontal translation of $y = f(x)$ by $1$ unit right, what is the function $g(x)$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212784 1072 #uuid "20e7c0fe-1dd1-5f05-b02d-404287dd3a3c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212784 1074 "We're given the line\n\n$$\ny = 4x + 2\n$$\n\nand we wish to translate this line by $1$ unit right.\n\nFor each point on the line $y = f(x)$, we must add $1$ to each $x$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on the new line $y = g(x)$. So, the $x$-coordinates of the points on the new line are given by\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{new}} = x + 1\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\nx = x_{\\textrm{new}} - 1\n$$\n\nTo get the equation of the new line, we substitute this into the equation of the first line. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4x + 2 \\\\\ny &= 4(x_{new} - 1) + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\ny = 4(x - 1) + 2\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying this expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4x - 4 + 2 \\\\\ny &= 4x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = 4x - 2\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212785 1069 "e-18900" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212785 1071 "The line $3x + 2y = 1$ is translated by $4$ units left. What is the equation of the new line?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212785 1072 #uuid "b5bf22e7-d5b8-55ac-91f8-a685b116e6c0" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212785 1074 "We're given the line\n\n$$\n3x + 2y = 1\n$$\n\nand we wish to translate this line by $4$ units left.\n\nFor each point on the line, we must subtract $4$ from each $x$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on the new line. So, the $x$-coordinates of the points on the new line are given by\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{new}} = x - 4\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\nx = x_{\\textrm{new}} + 4\n$$\n\nTo get the equation of the new line, we substitute this into the equation of the first line. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n3(x_{new} + 4) + 2y &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\n3(x + 4) + 2y = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing this equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 12 + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n3x + 2y &= -11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212786 1069 "e-18901" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212786 1071 "A curve is defined by $y = f(x)$, where $f(x) = -x^2 + x + 2$. If $y = g(x)$ is a horizontal translation of $y = f(x)$ by $2$ units left, what is the function $g(x)$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212786 1072 #uuid "543c27fa-8922-558d-ae47-cac767c61236" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212786 1074 "We're given the curve\n\n$$\ny = -x^2 + x + 2\n$$\n\nand we wish to translate this curve by $2$ units left.\n\nFor each point on the curve $y = f(x)$, we must subtract $2$ from each $x$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on the new curve $y = g(x)$. So, the $x$-coordinates of the points on the new curve are given by\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{new}} = x - 2\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\nx = x_{\\textrm{new}} + 2\n$$\n\nTo get the equation of the new curve, we substitute this into the equation of the first curve. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x^{2} + x + 2 \\\\\ny &= - (x_{new} + 2)^{2} + (x_{new} + 2) + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\ny = -(x + 2)^2 + (x + 2) + 2\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= - (x + 2)^{2} + (x + 2) + 2 \\\\\ny &= -x^{2} - 4x - 4 + x + 2 + 2 \\\\\ny &= -x^{2} - 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2 - 3x\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212787 1069 "e-18902" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212787 1071 "If $\\dfrac{6p}{3} = 10$, then find the value of $6p - 4$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212787 1072 #uuid "6c524edd-0704-5280-8a6b-f5ccd5c09754" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212787 1074 "Notice that $6p - 4$ contains $6p$.\n\nSo, we isolate the term $6p$ from the first equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6p}{3} &= 10 \\\\\n3 \\cdot \\frac{6p}{3} &= 3 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n6p &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $6p = 30$ into $6p - 4$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6p - 4 &= (30) - 4 \\\\\n&= 26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212788 1069 "e-18903" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212788 1071 "If $12 + 4x = 28$, what is the value of $3 + x$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212788 1072 #uuid "900cbf92-21b9-5e6a-b623-796c8475eca2" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212788 1074 "Notice that the target expression $3 + x$ can be obtained by multiplying the expression $12 + 4x$ by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot (12 + 4x) = 3 + x\n$$\n\nSince we are already told that\n\n$$\n12 + 4x = 28\n$$\n\nwe can multiply both sides of the equation by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 + 4x &= 28 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (12 + 4x) &= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 28 \\\\\n3 + x &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $3 + x$ is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{7}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212789 1069 "e-18904" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212789 1071 "If $\\dfrac{3x}{2} + 5 = 11$ and $x\\neq 0$, what is the value of $\\dfrac{2}{3x}$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212789 1072 #uuid "93f35819-2a33-5a34-acd7-552e07d25264" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212789 1074 "Notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3x}\n$$\n\nis the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x}{2}\n$$\n\nfrom the first equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x}{2} + 5 &= 11 \\\\\n\\frac{3x}{2} + 5 - 5 &= 11 - 5 \\\\\n\\frac{3x}{2} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it is sufficient to take the reciprocal on both sides of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x}{2} &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{3x}{2} &= \\frac{6}{1} \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3x} &= \\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212790 1069 "e-18907" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212790 1071 "A clothing store offers a $15\\%$ discount on all jackets. The price of a jacket varies between $\\[math]80$ and $\\[/math]140$. If $S$ represents the saving that a customer can receive on a single jacket purchase, find an inequality that must be satisfied by $S$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212790 1072 #uuid "91aebcfd-da07-5951-bd0c-a4910698cd72" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212790 1074 "The price of the jackets varies between $\\[math]80$ and $\\[/math]140$.\n\nIf the $15\\%$ discount is applied, then:\n\n- The minimum saving a customer can receive is given by\n$15\\% \\cdot \\[math]80 = \\[/math]12$.\n- The maximum saving a customer can receive is given by\n$15\\% \\cdot \\[math]140 = \\[/math]21$.\n\nTherefore, if $S$ represents the total saving a customer can receive, we have\n\n$$\n12 \\leq S \\leq 21\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212791 1069 "e-18908" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212791 1071 "Find the value of $\\sin\\left(8,235^\\circ\\right)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212791 1072 #uuid "f47101e8-00d7-5873-b5f7-8833af2f4e8c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212791 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $8,235^\\circ$ and lies in the range $[0,360^\\circ)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $360^\\circ$. So, we divide $8,235$ by $360$ with remainder:\n\n$$\n8,235 \\div 360 = 22\\ \\text{R} \\ 315\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8,235= 22\\cdot 360 + 315\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n8, 235^{∘} = 22 \\cdot 360^{∘} + 315^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(8,235^\\circ\\right) = \\sin \\left(315^\\circ\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $315^\\circ$ corresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(315^\\circ\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(8,235^\\circ\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212792 1069 "e-18909" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212792 1071 "What is the value of $\\cos\\left(7,965^\\circ\\right)$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212792 1072 #uuid "c344d19a-d54a-5f49-899f-53598c94d63c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212792 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $7,965^\\circ$ and lies in the range $[0,360^\\circ)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $360^\\circ$.\n\nSo, we divide $7,965$ by $360$ with remainder:\n\n$$\n7,965 \\div 360 = 22 \\ \\text{R} \\ 45\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n7,965^\\circ = 22 \\cdot 360^\\circ + 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(7,965^\\circ\\right) = \\cos\\left(45^\\circ\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/1f/1f53eb01ebae188bfbe48a454e3d840566024fe669c8e158b7b247d2054fbee9.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $45^\\circ$ corresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(45^\\circ\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(7,965^\\circ\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212793 1069 "e-18910" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212793 1071 "Find the value of $\\tan\\left(7,140^\\circ\\right)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212793 1072 #uuid "03fab5be-9e34-56f9-a5f7-7a5eb70daf85" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212793 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $7,140^\\circ$ and lies in the range $[0^\\circ,360^\\circ)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $360^\\circ$.\n\nSo, we divide $7,140$ by $360$ with remainder:\n\n$$\n7,140 \\div 360 = 19 \\ \\text{R} \\ 300\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n7,140^\\circ = 19 \\cdot 360^\\circ + 300^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan(7,140^\\circ) = \\tan(300^\\circ)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $300^\\circ$ corresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos \\left(300^\\circ \\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin \\left(300^\\circ \\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan(300^\\circ) = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\tan(7,140^\\circ) = -\\sqrt{3}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212794 1069 "e-18920" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212794 1071 "A linear function $g(x)$ gives the remaining mobile data, in gigabytes, on a monthly plan after $x$ days of continuous usage. There are $48$ GB remaining after $2$ days, and $30$ GB remaining after $5$ days. Which equation defines $g(x)$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212794 1072 #uuid "aaf7aedd-ad76-5b8d-bb6a-3925f8d41006" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212794 1074 "The linear function modeling this situation is\n\n$$\ng(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nWe know two points on the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{1}, y_{1}) &= (2, 48) \\\\\n(x_{2}, y_{2}) &= (5, 30)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the given two points, we find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30 - 48}{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-18}{3} \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= mx + b \\\\\n&=-6x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $b$, we substitute one of the points, say $(2, 48)$, into this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48 &= -6 \\cdot 2 + b \\\\\n48 &= -12 + b \\\\\nb &= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear equation that defines $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\ng(x) = -6x + 60\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212795 1069 "e-18921" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212795 1071 "A theme park charges $\\[math]65$ per person for the first $30$ people in a group and $\\[/math]50$ for each additional person. Which function $C$ gives the total cost, in dollars, for a group of $g$ people, where $g \\geq 30$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212795 1072 #uuid "4fb71375-cea2-5364-b809-c844c462a1a9" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212795 1074 "The total cost consists of two parts.\n\n- For the first $30$ people, the park charges\n\n$$\n65 \\times 30 = 1,950\\,\\,\\textrm{dollars}\n$$\n\n- For the additional persons beyond $30$, the park charges\n\n$$\n50(g - 30)\\,\\,\\textrm{dollars}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total cost $C$, in dollars, for a group of $g$ people, where\n\n$$\ng\\geq 30\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(g) &= 1, 950 + 50(g - 30) \\\\\n&= 1, 950 + 50g - 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 50g + 450\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212796 1069 "e-18923" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212796 1071 "Find the value of $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{127\\pi}{6}\\right)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212796 1072 #uuid "f19a3370-14ad-58d2-81cb-88f2203ed34d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212796 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{127\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $127$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 6 = 12\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n127 \\div 12 = 10 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n127 = 10 \\cdot 12 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{127π}{6} &= ((10 \\cdot 12 + 7)π)/(6) \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot \\frac{12π}{6} + \\frac{7π}{6} \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{127\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{127\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212797 1069 "e-18924" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212797 1071 "Find the value of $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{159\\pi}{4}\\right)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212797 1072 #uuid "e6549dd5-7dbf-52fc-9c62-18346301b451" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212797 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{159\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $159$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n159 \\div 8 = 19 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n159 = 19 \\cdot 8 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{159π}{4} &= ((19 \\cdot 8 + 7)π)/(4) \\\\\n&= 19 \\cdot \\frac{8π}{4} + \\frac{7π}{4} \\\\\n&= 19 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{159\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{159\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212798 1069 "e-18925" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212798 1071 "Find the value of $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\\right)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212798 1072 #uuid "9868564c-4ed9-5aca-812f-5b19e777fe57" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212798 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $215$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n215 \\div 8 = 26 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n215 = 26 \\cdot 8 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{215π}{4} &= ((26 \\cdot 8 + 7)π)/(4) \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot \\frac{8π}{4} + \\frac{7π}{4} \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} = -1\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212799 1069 "e-18926" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212799 1071 "| $x$ | $r(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $6$ | $22$ |\n| $9$ | $31$ |\n| $12$ | $40$ |\n\nSome values of the linear function $r$ are shown in the table above. Which of the following defines $r(x)$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212799 1072 #uuid "604f7244-01c2-597e-9590-b7ab3965d816" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212799 1074 "Since $r$ is a linear function, we know it must be of the form\n\n$$\nr(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $m$ is the slope, and\n- $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using two points. For $x = 6$ and $x = 9$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (r(x_{2}) - r(x_{1}))/(x_{2} - x_{1}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{31 - 22}{9 - 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{3} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(x) &= mx + b \\\\\n&= 3x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept using one point. For $x = 6$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(6) &= 3(6) + b \\\\\n22 &= 18 + b \\\\\nb &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear equation that defines $r(x)$ is\n\n$$\nr(x) = 3x + 4\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212800 1069 "e-18928" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212800 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{1}{3-\\sqrt a}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212800 1072 #uuid "659e7fd3-3502-5a47-a54c-3d0c33150d73" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212800 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $3+\\sqrt a$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $3+\\sqrt a$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3 - \\sqrt{a}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3 - \\sqrt{a}} \\cdot \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{a}}{3 + \\sqrt{a}} &=  \\\\\n(3 + \\sqrt{a})/((3 - \\sqrt{a})(3 + \\sqrt{a})) &=  \\\\\n(3 + \\sqrt{a})/(3^{2} - (\\sqrt{a})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{a}}{9 - a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212801 1069 "e-18929" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212801 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212801 1072 #uuid "dfb7701e-d11b-52a0-97fb-8363b1ff0901" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212801 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x+1}-1$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x+1}-1$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {(\\sqrt{x+1})^2-1^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {x+1-1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {x} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{x+1}-1 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212802 1069 "e-18930" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212802 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212802 1072 #uuid "4bcf9ec5-b5a1-5ab2-bb9f-f22280e072ae" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212802 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x})(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{(\\sqrt{2})^2-(\\sqrt{x})^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{2-x} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212803 1069 "e-18943" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212803 1071 "![](images/b4/b4bd31f57d640c1da5f25b7d7fbf7d3e9c6933d48a1e652d7ad1e3cc9b3200e6.png)\n\nA teacher recorded students' test scores in a test. The histogram above shows how many students achieved each score. What was the median score?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212803 1072 #uuid "c7c4bdd6-3492-501a-8fde-fa28ff6cfe37" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212803 1074 "To find the median from a histogram, we first convert the histogram into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram, the horizontal axis shows the values in the dataset, and the vertical axis tells us how many times each value appears.\n\nIn our case, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n| Score | Students | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $76$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $77$ | $5$ | $8$ |\n| $78$ | $7$ | $15$ |\n| $79$ | $3$ | $18$ |\n| $80$ | $2$ | $20$ |\n\nThus, the total number of values is\n\n$$\nN = 3 + 5 + 7 + 3 + 2 = 20\n$$\n\nSince this is an even number, the median is the average of the values in the following positions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N}{2} = \\dfrac{20}{2} = 10\\textrm{th} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad 10 + 1 = 11\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nLooking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $8$ positions in total.\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $15$ positions in total.\n\nSo, the $10$th and $11$th positions both fall in the third row and correspond to the value $78$. Therefore, the median is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian &= \\frac{78 + 78}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{156}{2} \\\\\n&= 78points\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212804 1069 "e-18944" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212804 1071 "![](images/63/6394109eea97e2924e01e245146c2273c7adc8095eac262a3b7281798a90ccdc.png)\n\nThe histogram above shows how many values from a dataset fall into each group. Which of the following values could be the median of the dataset?\n\n1. $17.5$\n2. $20$\n3. $22$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212804 1072 #uuid "d5f030f5-3419-5c1b-b6fc-748292db0297" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212804 1074 "To estimate the median from a histogram, we first convert the histogram into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram with groups, the horizontal axis shows the value groups, and the vertical axis tells us how many values fall into each group.\n\nIn our case, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n| Group | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $12-15$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $15-18$ | $6$ | $10$ |\n| $18-21$ | $3$ | $13$ |\n| $21-24$ | $5$ | $18$ |\n| $24-27$ | $2$ | $20$ |\n\nThere are $20$ total values in the dataset. Since this is an even number, the median is the average of the values in positions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{2} = 10 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad 10 + 1 = 11\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $10$th and $11$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $10$ positions in total.\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $13$ positions in total.\n\nSo, we have that\n\n- the $10$th value falls in the second row corresponding to the group $15-18$, while\n- the $11$th value falls in the third row corresponding to the group $18-21$.\n\nThe median could fall in either of these groups. Of the given options:\n\n- Option I, $17.5$, *falls* within the group $15-18$, so *could be* the median.\n- Option II, $20$, *falls* within the group $18-21$, so *could be* the median.\n- Option III, $22$, *doesn't fall* within either group $15-18$ or $18-21$, so *cannot be* the median.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212805 1069 "e-18945" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212805 1071 "Gina is a science blogger. She received the following number of likes for each of her six posts over the past $24$ hours:\n\n$8, \\; 16, \\; 32, \\; 14, \\; 20, \\; 6$\n\nMaria, another science writer, received a mean of $14$ likes per post over the past $24$ hours.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Gina's posts received a mean of $14$ likes each over the past $24$ hours.\n2. Maria's mean number of likes per day is smaller than Gina's.\n3. A comparison of the means suggests that Gina's posts are more popular than Maria's." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212805 1072 #uuid "5e0912b8-6ac9-5ed9-8985-a54f5407c62f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212805 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The mean number of likes Gina's posts received is\n$\\dfrac{8 + 16 + 32 + 14 + 20 + 6}{6} = \\dfrac{96}{6} = 16$.\nTherefore, Gina's posts received an average of $16$ likes each over the past $24$ hours.\n- Statement II is true. Since $14 < 16$, we conclude that Maria's mean number of likes per post is smaller than Gina's.\n- Statement III is true. Maria's mean is smaller than Gina's, suggesting that Gina's posts are more popular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212806 1069 "e-18946" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212806 1071 "Pam and Barbara both drive to work every day. Over the past week, Pam spent the following amount of money each day on gas:\n$\\[math]13, \\quad \\[/math]15, \\quad \\[math]18, \\quad \\[/math]20, \\quad \\[math]20, \\quad \\[/math]21$\n\nIf Barbara spent a median of $\\[math]25$ per day on gas, which of the following statement are true?\n\n1. Pam's median daily fuel expense is $\\[/math]19$.\n2. Barbara's median daily fuel expense is greater than Pam's.\n3. A comparison of the medians suggests that Pam spends more money on gas per day than Barbara." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212806 1072 #uuid "3a829958-51e0-51b6-b8e4-ded26ca4b4bc" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212806 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that Pam's data set is already arranged from smallest to greatest. Since the data set has an even number of data points ($6$), the median is the mean of the two middle numbers:\n$13, \\: 15, \\: {\\color{blue}18}, \\: {\\color{blue}20}, \\: 20, \\:21$\nThe median of the data set is\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}18}+{\\color{blue}20}}{2}=\\dfrac{38}{2}=19$.\nTherefore, Pam's median daily fuel expense is $\\[math]19$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, Barbara and Pam's median daily fuel expenses are $\\[/math]25$ and $\\[math]19$, respectively.\n- Statement III is false. Since Pam's median is smaller, there is no suggestion that Pam spends more on gas per day than Barbara.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212807 1069 "e-18947" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212807 1071 "Dr. Perelman and Dr. Conway compared blood glucose level measurements they each obtained from $10$ of their patients. Dr. Perelman's patients' modal blood glucose level was $182 \\, \\mathrm{mg}/\\mathrm{dL}$. The blood glucose levels (in $\\mathrm{mg}/\\mathrm{dL}$) of Dr. Conway's patients are as follows:\n\n$120,\\; 140, \\; 145,\\; 160,\\; 175, \\; 190, \\; 190,\\; 190, \\; 200,\\; 210$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The modal blood glucose level of Dr. Conway's patients is $190 \\, \\mathrm{mg}/\\mathrm{dL}$.\n2. The modal blood glucose level of Dr. Conway's patients is higher than that of Dr. Perelman's patients.\n3. A comparison of the modes suggests that Dr. Conway's patients have higher blood glucose levels." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212807 1072 #uuid "9f8fe0de-327b-5da5-818c-4b34de08804d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212807 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the data set from Dr. Conway's patients are already ordered from smallest to greatest and that $190\\, \\mathrm{mg}/\\mathrm{dL}$ is the most frequent value on the list:\n$120,\\; 140, \\; 145,\\; 160,\\; 175, \\; {\\color{blue}190}, \\; {\\color{blue}190},\\; {\\color{blue}190},\\; 200, \\; 210$\nSo, the modal value is $190\\, \\mathrm{mg}/\\mathrm{dL}$.\n- Statement II is true. Dr. Conway's patients' modal blood glucose level is $190\\, \\mathrm{mg}/\\mathrm{dL}$, while Dr. Perelman's patients' modal blood glucose level is $182 \\, \\mathrm{mg}/\\mathrm{dL}$.\n- Statement III is true. Dr. Conway's mode is larger, suggesting that his patients have higher blood glucose levels.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212808 1069 "e-18948" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212808 1071 "![](images/26/26ae20ee88d7f2b285f97021aaeb5972474c6d3c3580e1d8a57cf4b63ac01412.png)\n\nTwo fitness centers recorded the number of exercises each member completed per session during a certain period. The information for Center A is summarized in the dot plot above, where each dot represents the number of exercises completed by a certain member in one session. Center B had a mean number of exercises completed equal to $3.5$.\n\nA comparison of the means suggests which group completed more exercises per session." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212808 1072 #uuid "82bfdfc5-adeb-59a4-a982-f23e9489d976" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212808 1074 "We begin by constructing the frequency table from the given dot plot:\n\n| Exercises Completed | Number of Members |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ |\n| $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo calculate the mean of a dataset given in a frequency table, we compute the sum of the products of each value $x$ and its corresponding frequency $f_x$, and then divide that sum by the total number of data points.\n\nNext, we extend the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency:\n\n| Exercises Completed ($x$) | Number of Members ($f_x$) | $x \\cdot f_x$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2 \\cdot 2 = 4$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $3 \\cdot 2 = 6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $4 \\cdot 3 = 12$ |\n| $6$ | $3$ | $6 \\cdot 3 = 18$ |\n\nWe now calculate the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points, given by the total sum of the frequencies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean(A) &= \\frac{4 + 6 + 12 + 18}{2 + 2 + 3 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{40}{10} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3.5 < 4$, the mean for Center B is less than the mean for Center A.\n\nTherefore, a comparison of the means suggests members of Center A completed more exercises per session on average." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212809 1069 "e-18953" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212809 1071 "![](images/0e/0efa3cce371d0fb521dfc1f2de3f51d000c62f98e99be345701f9f894f6eb9b2.png)\n\nTeam B had a median sprint time of $11.5$ seconds. The dot plot above shows Team A's sprint times. A comparison of the medians suggests which team was faster overall?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212809 1072 #uuid "b7501c80-8922-5d06-906b-96f25fff7e70" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212809 1074 "The median is the middle value in an ordered data set. It separates the lower half from the upper half.\n\nTo find the median for Team A, notice that the number of data points ($18$) is even. This means the median is the average of the two middle values: the $9$th and $10$th positions.\n\n![](images/2b/2b05c6b0b0b0342a07f23060f748d3ab05727b0735309c087c572bce4187d626.png)\n\nFrom the cumulative counts, the $9$th and $10$th data points both fall at $11.4$, so the median is\n\n$$\n\\text{Median} = \\dfrac{11.4 + 11.4}{2} = {11.4}\n$$\n\nSince $11.4 < 11.5$, Team A’s median sprint time is smaller than Team B’s.\n\nTherefore, based on the median values, Team B was faster overall." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212810 1069 "e-18954" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212810 1071 "![](images/2f/2f85656e8aede19fe9883bb5a9cc3d68aa2afb7d8dafd442bbe2e0ad0211ac5b.png)\n\nTwo different classes took the same 100-point quiz. The median score in Class A was $67$ points. The histogram above shows the distribution of scores in Class B.\nBased on the median values, which class performed better on the quiz?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212810 1072 #uuid "035e8349-b59e-581a-b6af-b859738f1ae8" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212810 1074 "The median is the middle value in an ordered data set. It separates the lower half from the upper half.\n\nOn a histogram with groups, the horizontal axis shows the value groups, and the vertical axis tells us how many values fall into each group.\n\nTo find the median for Class B, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n| Score Range | Students | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $50-60$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $60-70$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| $70-80$ | $6$ | $11$ |\n| $80-90$ | $5$ | $16$ |\n| $90-100$ | $2$ | $18$ |\n\nClass B has a total of $18$ students. Since $18$ is even, the median is the average of the $9$th and $10$th values in the ordered data set.\n\nBoth the $9$th and $10$th values fall in the group $70-80$.\n\nSo, the median score in Class B lies in the group ${70-80}$.\n\nSince every score in the group $70-80$ is greater than $67$, the median score in Class B is greater than the median score in Class A.\n\nBased on the median values, students in Class B performed better on the quiz." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212811 1069 "e-18955" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212811 1071 "| Grade | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $5$ |  |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |\n| $4$ | $6$ |  |\n\nA teacher graded the results of a math test using a $4$-point scale. The distribution of the grades is shown in the table above. Find the missing values in the cumulative frequency column." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212811 1072 #uuid "dd501542-5b47-5633-a19f-9722be76d496" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212811 1074 "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies.\n\nTo complete the cumulative frequency column, we start with the first value. Then, we walk down the table, adding each new frequency to the cumulative frequency in the previous row.\n\nFor the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n\n| Grade | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{blue}5}$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |\n| $4$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFor each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative frequency *from the previous row*. So, we have the following:\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $2$nd row in our table is equal to\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}+{\\color{red}5}=7\n$$\n\n| Grade | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $5$ | ${\\color{red}5}$ |\n| $2$ | ${\\color{blue}2}$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |\n| $4$ | $6$ |  |\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $3$rd row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{red}7}=14\n$$\n\n| Grade | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | ${\\color{red}7}$ |\n| $3$ | ${\\color{blue}7}$ | $14$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |  |\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $4$th row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6} + {\\color{red}14} = 20\n$$\n\n| Grade | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ | ${\\color{red}14}$ |\n| $4$ | ${\\color{blue}6}$ | $20$ |" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212812 1069 "e-18956" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212812 1071 "A group of students were asked how many snacks they purchased from the school vending machine last week. The frequency table below shows the distribution of responses. Fill in the missing values in the cumulative frequency table below.\n\n| Snacks Bought | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$ | $12$ |\n| $2$ | $\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$ | $18$ |\n| $3$ | $\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$ | $25$ |" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212812 1072 #uuid "24551c17-4983-5456-956c-d1982a8030c6" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212812 1074 "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nTherefore, to find the frequency for each row except the first one, we subtract the previous cumulative frequency from the current cumulative frequency.\n\n- The frequency for the $1$st row in our table is equal to $3$.\n- The frequency for the $2$nd row in our table is equal to $12-3=9$.\n- The frequency for the $3$rd row in our table is equal to $18-12=6$.\n- The frequency for the $4$th row in our table is equal to $25-18=7$.\n\nLet's add these values to our table.\n\n| Snacks Bought | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3}$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{9}=12-3$ | $12$ |\n| $2$ | $\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{6}=18-12$ | $18$ |\n| $3$ | $\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{7}=25-18$ | $25$ |" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212813 1069 "e-18961" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212813 1071 "![](images/9a/9a41acc78ae79220813710170b20ebfaa394369c169d613fe13929d71bca38cd.png)\n\nTwo companies tracked the number of website visits per user per day during a certain period. The information for Company A is summarized in the dot plot above, where each dot represents the number of visits for a certain user on a particular day. For Company B, the most frequently reported number of visits per day was $7$. A comparison of the modes suggests which company's website had more visits per day?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212813 1072 #uuid "cc00f11f-5950-5f07-bddd-0381703aa16d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212813 1074 "The mode is the value with the highest frequency in the data set.\n\nFrom the dot plot, we see that the highest frequency is $8$, corresponding to $9$ visits per day.\n\n![](images/fe/fe48f4cd84da39e9ab13977c6054db04e8bdce248e5a54b6220b3990d2f2be97.png)\n\nSo, the mode for Company A is $9$. And since $9> 7$, the mode for Company B is less than the mode for Company A.\n\nTherefore, based on the modes, Company A had more website visits per day." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212814 1069 "e-18962" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212814 1071 "![](images/7d/7d697fb80d0d6bede845c53b5ba70d7e59746c77ccffaae1a41a14708ae75208.png)\n\nThe histogram above shows how many values from a dataset fall into each group. What is the modal group of the dataset?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212814 1072 #uuid "b4cc2e18-e9a7-5d91-8b52-21be4497d9e0" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212814 1074 "When data is grouped into intervals, the modal group is the group with the highest frequency.\n\nFrom a histogram, we can directly compare the bar heights to see how often values from each group occurs.\n\n![](images/b9/b97aaee44cfe0156bef8a167861d2d248e7683e2fca55ba13bf273d21f83615f.png)\n\nIn our case, the tallest bar corresponds to the group $9-14$, which has a frequency of $12$, more than any other group.\n\nTherefore, the modal group of the dataset is $9-14$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212815 1069 "e-18964" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212815 1071 "The formula\n$B = 30x + 40$\ngives the brightness level $B$ (in lumens) of a display screen as a function of the slider position $x$, where $x = 0$ represents the lowest setting and $x = 1$ represents the maximum setting. For recommended viewing conditions, the slider should be set between $0.4$ and $0.9$. What inequality describes the acceptable brightness range during use?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212815 1072 #uuid "038aa2b2-3d66-5076-9ec6-ddd25211ea3a" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212815 1074 "We are asked to find the brightness range based on the formula and a recommended slider range.\n\nThe formula is\n\n$$\nB = 30x + 40\n$$\n\nThe slider should be set between $0.4$ and $0.9$. Thus, we compute:\n\n- At the minimum recommended slider level $x = 0.4$, the brightness is\n$B|= 30(0.4) + 40; = 12 + 40; = 52lumens$.\n- At the maximum recommended slider level $x = 0.9$, the brightness is\n$B|= 30(0.9) + 40; = 27 + 40; = 67lumens$.\n\nTherefore, the acceptable brightness range during use is\n\n$$\n52\\leq B\\leq 67\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212816 1069 "e-18965" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212816 1071 "A branding consultant charges a base fee of $b$ dollars and receives a royalty equal to $10\\%$ of the product revenue $x$ generated from a marketing campaign. For a particular contract, the consultant wants total compensation to be at least $1.4$ times and at most $1.8$ times the base fee. What inequality shows all possible values of $x$ that meet this goal?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212816 1072 #uuid "9c1a949d-6f2b-55c4-a04e-15a0d4ea14b7" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212816 1074 "The consultant earns $10\\%$ of the revenue. So, if the total revenue is $x$ dollars, they earn $0.10x$ dollars.\n\nAdditionally, there is a base fee of $b$ dollars. Therefore, the total earned, in dollars, is\n\n$$\nb + 0.10x\n$$\n\nSince the consultant wants total earnings to be at least $1.4$ times and at most $1.8$ times the base fee, we must have\n\n$$\n1.4b \\leq b + 0.10x \\leq 1.8b\n$$\n\nSubtracting $b$ throughout, we get\n\n$$\n0.4b \\le 0.10x \\mid \\le 0.8b\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $0.10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{0.4b}{0.10} \\le x & \\le \\frac{0.8b}{0.10} \\\\ 4b \\le x & \\le 8b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is\n\n$$\n4b \\leq x \\leq 8b\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212817 1069 "e-18966" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212817 1071 "An IT support team recorded how many tickets individual technicians resolved during a shift. The results are shown in the following frequency table.\n\n| Tickets Resolved | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $7$ | $6$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat are the mode(s) of this data set?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212817 1072 #uuid "8fa6285d-81c7-5259-9acd-2bb959677b49" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212817 1074 "The mode is the value with the highest frequency in the data set.\n\nFrom the frequency table, we see that the highest frequency is $6$, corresponding to resolving $4$ and $7$ tickets.\n\n| Tickets Resolved | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{blue}6$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}7$ | $\\color{blue}6$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the data set has two modes: $4$ and $7$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212818 1069 "e-18968" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212818 1071 "A music club recorded the number of songs each student memorized over a month. The incomplete data set is shown below.\n\n| Songs Memorized | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $x$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ |\n| $4$ | $7$ |\n| $5$ | $4$ |\n| $6$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the only mode of the data set is $2$, which of the following values could be the value of $x$?\n\n1. $7$\n2. $8$\n3. $6$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212818 1072 #uuid "45a022d2-526c-57cd-a760-8d107ad85676" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212818 1074 "The mode is the value with the greatest frequency.\n\nWe are told that $2$ is the only mode, so its frequency $x$ must be greater than the frequency of every other value in the table.\n\nThe largest known frequency is $7$ (for $4$ songs). So, we must have\n$x > 7$.\n\nNow, let's test each value:\n\n- We cannot have $x=7$ since $7 > 7$ is false. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- We can have $x=8$ since $8 > 7$ is true. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- We cannot have $x=6$ since $6 > 7$ is false. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is option **II.**" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212819 1069 "e-18970" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212819 1071 "![](images/5b/5bb82f56d8ede8d8322bf047a56fc8be6f3872a42bf056afd89880da9887b0e4.png)\n\nGiven that the area of a square inscribed in a circle is $162\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2$, what is the circle's radius?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212819 1072 #uuid "29bf6681-4d32-5a16-a1ee-c7fddb062e68" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212819 1074 "If $d$ is a diagonal of a square, then the area of the square is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{1}{2}d^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known area and solving for $d$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}d^{2} \\\\\n162 &= \\frac{1}{2}d^{2} \\\\\n324 &= d^{2} \\\\\nd &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagonal of the square inscribed in the circle equals the circle's diameter, and the diameter is twice the radius. Therefore, the radius is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{d}{2} = \\dfrac{18}{2} = 9\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212820 1069 "e-18971" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212820 1071 "A square is inscribed in a circle, and the same circle is inscribed in a larger square. If the area of the inner square is $3 \\,\\mathrm{m}^2$, what is the area of the outer square?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212820 1072 #uuid "04f88779-ffc8-5459-baa1-6693d939dc27" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212820 1074 "The diagram below shows the described situation.\n\n![](images/cd/cd3234614a0f7d6bec1245ea5d00a4c92dad9e84ef7e310d21a35fccda2b2a3d.png)\n\nNotice that the diagonal of the inner square equals the diameter of the circle.\n\nNow, if $d$ is a diagonal of a square, then the area of the square is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{1}{2}d^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known area of the inner square, and solving for $d$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{I} &= \\frac{1}{2}d^{2} \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{1}{2}d^{2} \\\\\n6 &= d^{2} \\\\\nd &= \\sqrt{6}m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe side of the outer square equals the diameter of the inscribed circle. Therefore, the area of the outer square is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_O = d^2 = \\left(\\sqrt{6}\\right)^2 = 6 \\: \\textrm{m}^2\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212821 1069 "e-18972" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212821 1071 "A fitness coach records how many flights of stairs each participant climbs in a building. The cumulative frequency table below summarizes the data. Fill in the missing values in the table and determine the median number of stairs climbed.\n\n| Flights Climbed | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{3}}$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$ |" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212821 1072 #uuid "a0a3168e-8bdd-594c-9dd2-af5a52b3f9bd" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212821 1074 "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add the missing value in the cumulative frequency table.\n\n| Flights Climbed | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3}$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{11}$ |\n\nThus, the total number of values in the dataset is $11$ (the final cumulative frequency). Since our dataset has an odd number of data points, the median is the middle value in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian position &= \\frac{11 + 1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{2} \\\\\n&= 6th\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let’s determine where the $6$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $5$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $11$ positions in total.\n\nSo, the $6$th position falls into the fourth row that corresponds to the value $8$ (stairs climbed).\n\n| Flights Climbed | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3}$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{11}$ |\n\nTherefore, the median number of stairs climbed is\n\n$$\n\\text{Median} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{8}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212822 1069 "e-18973" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212822 1071 "A group of volunteers reported how many hours they spent helping out during a weekend event. The cumulative frequency table below summarizes the data. Fill in the missing values in the table and determine the median number of volunteer hours.\n\n| Volunteer Hours | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{2}}$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{7}}$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $10$ |\n| $9$ | $10$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{20}}$ |" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212822 1072 #uuid "6d32860b-611a-5d8d-837f-5b657eed9f79" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212822 1074 "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add the missing value in the cumulative frequency table.\n\n| Volunteer Hours | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{7}$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $10$ |\n| $9$ | $10$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{20}$ |\n\nThus, the total number of values in the dataset is $20$ (the final cumulative frequency). Since our dataset has an even number of data points, the median is the average of the two middle values in the following positions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{2} = 10\\textrm{th} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 10+1 = 11\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nNow, let’s determine where the $10$th and $11$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $10$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $20$ positions in total.\n\nSo, we have that\n\n- The $10$th value falls in the third row corresponding to the value $5$ (volunteer hours), and\n- The $11$th value falls in the fourth row corresponding to the value $9$ (volunteer hours).\n\n| Volunteer Hours | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{7}$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $10$ |\n| $9$ | $10$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{20}$ |\n\nTherefore, the median is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian &= \\frac{5 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{2} \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvolunteer hours." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212823 1069 "e-18975" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212823 1071 "The incomplete frequency table below shows how many days guests stayed at a mountain lodge.\n\n| Days | Guests |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ |\n| $14$ | $\\,? \\,$ |\n\nIf the mean number of days stayed was $12$, how many guests stayed for $14$ days?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212823 1072 #uuid "21399760-40a9-510c-b748-39e3f4481d6a" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212823 1074 "To find the missing frequency, we can find an expression for the mean in terms of the missing frequency.\n\nWe first extend the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency:\n\n| Days ($x$) | Guests ($f_x$) | $x \\cdot f_x$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1 \\cdot 2 = 2\\:\\:\\qquad$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2 \\cdot 2 = 4\\:\\:\\qquad$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ | $6 \\cdot 2 = 12\\qquad$ |\n| $14$ | $f_{14}$ | $14 \\cdot f_{14}\\qquad\\:\\:$ |\n\nNow, we find an expression for the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{2 + 4 + 12 + 14f_{14}}{2 + 2 + 2 + f_{14}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18 + 14f_{14}}{6 + f_{14}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that the mean of the dataset is $12$. So, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18 + 14f_{14}}{6 + f_{14}} = 12\n$$\n\nThen, we eliminate the denominator by multiplying both sides by $(6 + f_{14})$, before solving for $f_{14}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 + 14f_{14} &= 12(6 + f_{14}) \\\\\n18 + 14f_{14} &= 72 + 12f_{14} \\\\\n14f_{14} - 12f_{14} &= 72 - 18 \\\\\n2f_{14} &= 54 \\\\\nf_{14} &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a total of $27$ guests stayed for $14$ days." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212824 1069 "e-18977" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212824 1071 "Convert $33\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}$ into square feet per second." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212824 1072 #uuid "5f08e3b7-59ef-5268-a3a9-9dc3e681863b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212824 1074 "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we must apply two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{yd}^2$ to $\\textrm{ft}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{min}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{yd}^2$ to $\\textrm{ft}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{min}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{yd}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $33\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}$.\n- There are $3$ feet per yard. We can write this as follows:\n$3\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{yd}}$\nTo determine the number of square feet in one square yard, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(3\\frac{ft}{yd})^{2}|= 3^{2}\\frac{ft^{2}}{yd^{2}}; = 9\\frac{ft^{2}}{yd^{2}}$\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ minutes per second. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{60}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n33\\frac{yd^{2}}{min} &= 33\\frac{yd^{2}}{min} \\cdot 9\\frac{ft^{2}}{yd^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\frac{min}{s} \\\\\n&= (33 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\frac{1}{60})\\frac{yd^{2}}{min} \\cdot \\frac{ft^{2}}{yd^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{min}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{297}{60}\\frac{ft^{2}}{s} \\\\\n&= 4.95\\frac{ft^{2}}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n33\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n4.95\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212825 1069 "e-18978" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212825 1071 "A park maintenance robot is clearing leaves at a rate of $132{,}000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$. Express this rate in square meters per minute. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: $1\\,\\textrm{m} \\approx 3.28\\,\\textrm{ft}$*" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212825 1072 #uuid "ad8ca4df-81ef-5c68-87b1-57c34cfe0f8b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212825 1074 "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert it to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nWe'll need to multiply by two conversion factors to change both the area unit ($\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and the time unit ($\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{ft}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The robot's clearing rate is given as $132{,}000 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $3.28$ feet per meter. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3.28}$ meters per foot. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3.28} \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{ft}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square foot, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(\\frac{1}{3.28}\\frac{m}{ft})^{2}|= \\frac{1^{2}}{3.28^{2}}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}$\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ hours per minute. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{60} \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n132, 000\\frac{ft^{2}}{h} \\mid \\approx 132, 000\\frac{ft^{2}}{h} \\cdot \\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\frac{h}{min}; = (132, 000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10.7584} \\cdot \\frac{1}{60})\\frac{ft^{2}}{h} \\cdot \\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{h}{min}; = \\frac{132, 000}{10.7584 \\cdot 60}\\frac{m^{2}}{min}; = \\frac{132, 000}{645.504}\\frac{m^{2}}{min}; \\approx 204\\frac{m^{2}}{min}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the park maintenance robot is clearing leaves at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n204\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212826 1069 "e-18987" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212826 1071 "Factor the expression $y^2-\\dfrac{1}{10}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212826 1072 #uuid "33cc9ad9-0ba8-5f65-adb6-41b05eccfc3c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212826 1074 "Notice that\n\n$$\n10 = \\big(\\sqrt{10}\\big)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - \\frac{1}{10} &=  \\\\\ny^{2} - (1)/((\\sqrt{10})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= y^{2} - (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\ny^{2} - (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})^{2} = (y - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})(y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212827 1069 "e-18988" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212827 1071 "| Movies Watched | Number of Friends |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |\n| $4$ | $5$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n\nTwo groups of friends reported the number of movies they watched last month. The table above summarizes the responses for Group A. For Group B, the most frequently reported number of movies watched was $4$. A comparison of the modes suggests which group watched more movies last month?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212827 1072 #uuid "c7cbc919-8234-5ed5-a87f-abd4d4dff255" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212827 1074 "The mode is the value with the highest frequency in the data set.\n\nFrom the frequency table, we see that the highest frequency is $7$, corresponding to $2$ movies watched.\n\n| Movies Watched | Number of Friends |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |\n| $4$ | $5$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n\nSo, the mode for Group A is $2$. And since $2 < 4$, the mode for Group B is greater than the mode for Group A.\n\nTherefore, based on the modes, Group B watched more movies last month." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212828 1069 "e-18989" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212828 1071 "A farmer recorded the number of apples picked from each tree. The incomplete data set is shown below.\n\n| Apples Picked | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $x$ |\n| $4$ | $5$ |\n| $5$ | $7$ |\n| $6$ | $6$ |\n\nIf $3$ is a mode of the data set, which of the following values could be the value of $x$?\n\n1. $7$\n2. $8$\n3. $6$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212828 1072 #uuid "5cf57a6c-6add-5cf5-a74d-1cc7012f8f3c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212828 1074 "The mode is the value with the greatest frequency.\n\nWe are told that $3$ is a mode of the data set, so its frequency $x$ must be at least as large as the greatest known frequency in the table.\n\nFrom the table, the highest known frequency is $7$ (for $5$ apples). So, we must have\n\n$$\nx \\geq 7\n$$\n\nNow, let’s test each value:\n\n- We can have $x=7$ since $7 \\geq 7$ is true. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- We can have $x=8$ since $8 \\geq 7$ is true. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- We cannot have $x=6$ since $6 \\geq 7$ is false. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212829 1069 "e-18994" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212829 1071 "| Items PurchasedFrequencyCumulative Frequency$28$$1$$1$$32$$6$$7$ $35$$4$$11$$40$$9$$20$ |  | Items PurchasedFrequencyCumulative Frequency$30$$6$$6$$33$$2$$8$ $38$$15$$23$$42$$4$$27$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\nTwo grocery stores, store $A$ and store $B$, recorded how many items customers purchased in a day. The cumulative frequency tables above summarize the data from each store, where the data from store $A$ is on the left, and store $B$'s data is on the right. Which store has the smaller median?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212829 1072 #uuid "647e86c4-552a-59a6-803d-001caeafdc5e" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212829 1074 "Let's compute the medians for the datasets in turn.\n\n- The total number of values for store $A$ is $20$. Since our data set has an even number of data points, the median is the average of the two middle values in the positions\n$\\frac{20}{2} = 10and10 + 1 = 11$.\nNow, let’s determine where the $10$th and $11$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in the left table, we see the following:Up to the second row, the table covers $7$ positions in total.Up to the third row, the table covers $11$ positions in total.So, the $10$th and $11$th values both fall into the third row corresponding to the value $35$ (items purchased). Therefore, the median is\n$Median|= \\frac{35 + 35}{2}; = \\frac{70}{2}; = 35$.\n- The total number of values for store $B$ is $27$. Since our data set has an odd number of data points, the median is the value in position\n$\\frac{27 + 1}{2} = 14$.\nNow, let’s determine where the $14$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in the right table, we see the following:Up to the second row, the table covers $8$ positions.Up to the third row, the table covers $23$ positions.So, the $14$th value falls into the third row corresponding to the value $38$ (items purchased). Therefore, the median is\n$Median|= 38$.\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the median of store A is smaller than the median of store B." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212830 1069 "e-18997" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212830 1071 "Convert $\\left(6n\\right)^{2/3}$ into a radical expression." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212830 1072 #uuid "10559936-2df1-53ab-8152-47af5e13465b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212830 1074 "First, using the power law for exponents, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\left(6n\\right)^{2/3} = \\left((6n)^2\\right)^{1/3}\n$$\n\nIn general, the fractional exponent $x^{1/n}$ can also be written as the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}$.\n\nIn this particular case, $n=3$ and $x=(6n)^2$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left((6n)^2\\right)^{1/3} = \\sqrt[3]{(6n)^2} = \\sqrt[3]{36\\,n^2}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212831 1069 "e-18998" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212831 1071 "Write $\\sqrt[11]{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{a}\\right)^{2}}$ using a fractional exponent." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212831 1072 #uuid "68572603-fb9d-59c8-a913-c070bfff1a9d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212831 1074 "In general, the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/n}$.\n\nIn this particular case, $n=11$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\left(\\dfrac{5}{a}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[11]{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{a}\\right)^{2}}=\\left(\\left(\\dfrac{5}{a}\\right)^{2}\\right)^{1/11}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\left(\\dfrac{5}{a}\\right)^{2}\\right)^{1/11}=\\left(\\dfrac{5}{a}\\right)^{2/11}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212832 1069 "e-18999" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212832 1071 "![](images/e6/e60d31e183d6b7036f67ca31262123dd956cfd9c5c8f33b8e9c5b4c0e39a39f7.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the square is inscribed in the circle, and the smaller circle is inscribed in the square. If the area of the outer circle is $18\\pi\\,\\mathrm{in}^2$, what is the circumference of the inner circle?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212832 1072 #uuid "f1ee1034-2284-55f9-a603-ee69d0716241" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212832 1074 "Since the area of a circle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\n, where $r$ is the radius, we can find the radius of the outer circle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{O} &= πr^{2} \\\\\n18π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n18 &= r^{2} \\\\\nr &= 3\\sqrt{2}in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagonal of the square $d$ is twice the radius of the outer circle. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nd=2r= 6\\sqrt{2} \\,\\mathrm{in}\n$$\n\nIf $d$ and $s$ are the lengths of the diagonal and the side of the square, respectively, then $2s^2 = d^2$. So, we can find the side of the square in the following way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2s^{2} &= d^{2} \\\\\n2s^{2} &= (6\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\\ns^{2} &= 36 \\\\\ns &= 6in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the side $s$ of the square is equal to the diameter of the inner circle.\nTherefore, the circumference of the inner circle is\n\n$$\nc_I = \\pi s = 6\\pi\\,\\mathrm{in}\\,\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212833 1069 "e-19000" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212833 1071 "![](images/d3/d349cde2d1540f10d43bc8a211cf101ff2eb0ca9cb6d84aaa816cae34af5de71.png)\n\nDetermine the length of the side of a square inscribed in a circle, given that the circle's area is $4\\pi$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212833 1072 #uuid "af26defc-833e-58e6-a6f2-8d633ac7ae2f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212833 1074 "If $r$ is the circle's radius, then the circle's area is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known area and solving for $r$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n4π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n4 &= r^{2} \\\\\nr &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe diagonal of the square inscribed in the circle equals the circle's diameter, and the diameter is twice the radius. Therefore, the diagonal is\n\n$$\nd = 2r = 2(2) = 4\n$$\n\nFinally, if $s$ is the length of the side of the square, then $2s^2 = d^2$. Substituting $d=4$ and solving for $s$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2s^{2} &= d^{2} \\\\\n2s^{2} &= 4^{2} \\\\\n2s^{2} &= 16 \\\\\ns^{2} &= 8 \\\\\ns &= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212834 1069 "e-19001" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212834 1071 "Convert $(8y)^{-3/2}$ into a radical." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212834 1072 #uuid "77f5bc98-bb3b-53d5-a024-c061adae6bc0" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212834 1074 "Remember that in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have\n\n$$\n(8y)^{-3/2} = \\dfrac{1}{(8y)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nNext, using the power law for exponents, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(8y)^{3/2}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left((8y)^3\\right)^{1/2}}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\left((8y)^3\\right)^{1/2}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(8y)^3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n(8y)^{-3/2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(8y)^3}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{512y^3}}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212835 1069 "e-19002" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212835 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[8]{(4r)^3}}$ using power notation." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212835 1072 #uuid "8352f65d-4a8c-5440-b7fb-593447775f8a" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212835 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[8]{(4r)^3}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\left((4r)^3\\right)^{1/8}}=\\dfrac{1}{(4r)^{3/8}}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the reciprocal and convert the exponent to a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(4r)^{3/8}}=(4r)^{-3/8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[8]{(4r)^3}}=(4r)^{-3/8}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212836 1069 "e-19003" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212836 1071 "![](images/a2/a261047506119ebea9fad10ac40811f380bc0e58839460ae1419874d26a9e3af.png)\n\nThe side of an equilateral triangle is $27$ feet. The three vertices of the triangle lie on a circle, as depicted above. What is the radius of the circle?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212836 1072 #uuid "ce70a1e9-6020-58ec-8a76-8bea48e76720" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212836 1074 "To find the radius of the circle, we draw radii from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c053b3b368ffd379091b1b72c4701c3b77c0438394235a0abede8676043086c.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The length of the longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$x = \\dfrac{AC}{2} = \\dfrac{27}{2} = 13.5$.\n- The length of the hypotenuse is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\ntimes the length of the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n13.5 &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot 13.5 \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 9\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $9 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212837 1069 "e-19004" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212837 1071 "![](images/94/94594802c73e052e9d8f333f13801a04bc6dc0f1e9fdd3d112ca2f0620e4d0ec.png)\n\nAn equilateral triangle is inscribed in a circle, as shown above. What is the area of the triangle if the radius of the circle is $6$ meters?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212837 1072 #uuid "b6e3351d-db99-558b-9936-1cf57b538081" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212837 1074 "To find the area of the triangle, we draw radii from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a42f60fce0184d40831450e286c3670dc0113b98807c4ed0530c07aa508f0ed5.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- Let the longer leg, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, be $x$. Notice that it is half of our equilateral triangle's side length.\n- The hypotenuse is a radius of the circle and has a length $r = 6\\:\\text{m}$.\n\nNext, in the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\ntimes the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equilateral triangle's side length is\n\n$$\ns = 2x = 2 \\cdot 3 \\sqrt{3} = 6 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{m}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}s^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(6\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot 108 \\\\\n&= 27\\sqrt{3}m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212838 1069 "e-19005" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212838 1071 "| Cups of Water Drunk | Number of Campers |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $3$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ |\n| $7$ | $1$ |\n\nTwo groups of campers reported the number of cups of water they drank during a hike. The table above summarizes the responses for Group A. For the campers in Group B, their average number of cups of water was $2$.\n\nA comparison of the means suggests which group drank more cups of water on average?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212838 1072 #uuid "25fee0a5-9700-5457-95b2-faed6eed4f8f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212838 1074 "To calculate the mean of a dataset given in a frequency table, we compute the sum of the products of each value $x$ and its corresponding frequency $f_x$, and then divide that sum by the total number of data points.\n\nWe begin by extending the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency:\n\n| Cups of Water ($x$) | Number of Campers ($f_x$) | $x \\cdot f_x$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $1 \\cdot 5 = 5$ |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $3 \\cdot 3 = 9$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $5 \\cdot 3 = 15$ |\n| $7$ | $1$ | $7 \\cdot 1 = 7$ |\n\nWe now calculate the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points, given by the total sum of the frequencies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean(A) &= \\frac{5 + 9 + 15 + 7}{3 + 3 + 3 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{12} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 < 3$, the mean for Group B is less than the mean for Group A.\n\nTherefore, a comparison of the means suggests Group A drank more cups on average." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212839 1069 "e-19006" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212839 1071 "![](images/09/09a4bef406aaf6f1f1cb44166896fa38f61a6dade2be5b256d3782c8cf9d7915.png)\n\nThe equilateral triangle $\\triangle BFJ$ is inscribed in a circle with center $O$, as shown above.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\overline{OJ}$ is the radius of the circle.\n- $\\triangle{BOJ}$ is an isosceles triangle.\n- $\\angle{FOB}$ has a measure of $120^\\circ$.\n- $\\triangle{WOJ}$ is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle.\n- $\\angle{OJW}$ has a measure of $30^\\circ$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212839 1072 #uuid "0fb45d85-e1ab-5dc3-9a14-5466ff9aa4df" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212839 1074 "The radii, drawn from the center of the circle to each of the triangle’s vertices, form three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a14f144ba47d209869f29a9c2de4956af6f3b73d03b0d58ffae22d46fc362fc.png)\n\nWe have the altitude $\\overline{OW}$ drawn in one of these isosceles triangles from the center of the circle to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, a possible answer could be as follows:\n\n- $\\overline{OJ}$ is the radius of the circle.\n- $\\triangle{BOJ}$ is an isosceles triangle.\n- $\\angle{FOB}$ has a measure of $120^\\circ$.\n- $\\triangle{WOJ}$ is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle.\n- $\\angle{OJW}$ has a measure of $30^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, all the statements are true." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212840 1069 "e-19007" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212840 1071 "Factor the following expression:\n$(z-12)(z+3) + 4(12-z)^2$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212840 1072 #uuid "f24b62ac-2dee-547f-86c7-4f0ac3f1057e" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212840 1074 "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(z-12)(z+3) + 4(12-z)^2$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first term in this expression contains a factor of $(z-12)$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of $(12-z)$.\n\nThese factors differ only by the sign:\n\n$$\n12-z = -(z-12)\n$$\n\nHowever, the squaring eliminates the minus sign:\n\n$$\n(12-z)^2 = (z-12)^2\n$$\n\nSo, we can simply rewrite the second term:\n$(z-12)(z+3) + 4(z-12)^2$\n\nNow, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-12)\\left[(z+3) + 4(z-12)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z - 12)[(z + 3) + 4(z - 12)] &=  \\\\\n(z - 12)[z + 3 + 4z - 48] &=  \\\\\n&= (z - 12)(5z - 45)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212841 1069 "e-19008" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212841 1071 "Consider the expression\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(7-2a)^2$.\n\nThis expression can be written in the form\n$(2a-7)^2\\cdot g(a)$.\nFind the polynomial $g(a)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212841 1072 #uuid "8163c2ef-1fdf-532b-9600-22860d3ad041" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212841 1074 "We're asked to factor the expression\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(7-2a)^2$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first term in this expression contains a factor of $(2a-7)^2$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of $(7-2a)^2$.\n\nThese factors differ only by the sign:\n\n$$\n7-2a = -(2a-7)\n$$\n\nHowever, the squaring eliminates the minus sign:\n\n$$\n(7-2a)^2 = (2a-7)^2\n$$\n\nSo, we can simply rewrite the second term:\n$(2a-7)^3(a+2) + (3a+4)(2a-7)^2$\n\nNow, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(2a-7)^2 \\left[(2a-7)(a+2) + (3a+4)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2a - 7)^{2}[(2a - 7)(a + 2) + (3a + 4)] &=  \\\\\n(2a - 7)^{2}[2a^{2} + 4a - 7a - 14 + 3a + 4] &=  \\\\\n&= (2a - 7)^{2}(2a^{2} - 10)_(⏟)_(g(a))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ng(a) = 2a^2 - 10\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212842 1069 "e-19009" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212842 1071 "A regional road race has $19{,}600$ registered runners. A random sample of runners was surveyed, and each participant was asked whether they plan to purchase official race merchandise. Of those surveyed, $47\\%$ responded that they plan to purchase merchandise.\n\nBased on this survey, which of the following is the best estimate of the total number of runners who plan to purchase merchandise? Round your answer to the nearest $500$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212842 1072 #uuid "13a0c310-c1db-54d2-98d5-b8fb36685015" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212842 1074 "We are told that there are $19{,}600$ runners in total, and the sample suggests that $47\\%$ plan to purchase merchandise. To estimate the total number who plan to purchase, we compute $47\\%$ of $19{,}600$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n19,600 \\cdot 47% &= 19,600 \\cdot 0.47 \\\\\n&= 9,212\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must round to the nearest $500$, which is $9{,}000$.\n\nTherefore, the best estimate is $9{,}000$ runners." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212843 1069 "e-19010" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212843 1071 "A coach wants to estimate the typical finish time of participants in a 10K race series. Over several years, $200$ races have been held. The coach randomly selects $7$ races and records the winning times (in minutes): $52$, $48$, $55$, $50$, $60$, $53$, and $58$.\n\nBased on the **median** of the sample, which of the following ranges contains the best estimate for the total combined finish time of all $200$ races?\n\n1. $10{,}000$ to $10{,}500$\n2. $10{,}500$ to $11{,}000$\n3. $11{,}000$ to $11{,}500$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212843 1072 #uuid "ce57ce00-9a02-5331-ac96-832cdc3dee07" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212843 1074 "First, we list the sample times in order from least to greatest:\n$48,\\ 50,\\ 52,\\ 53,\\ 55,\\ 58,\\ 60$\n\nSince there are $7$ values, the median is the middle value, which is the $4$th number in the list:\n\n$$\n\\text{Median} = 53\n$$\n\nThis suggests a typical race finish time of $53$ minutes.\n\nTo estimate the total combined finish time over all $200$ races, we multiply the median by the total number of races:\n\n$$\n53 \\cdot 200 = 10{,}600\n$$\n\nThe number $10{,}600$ lies between $10{,}500$ and $11{,}000$. Therefore, the best estimate falls in the range $10{,}500$ to $11{,}000$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212844 1069 "e-19011" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212844 1071 "A corporation randomly sampled $500$ employees about next year’s health insurance. The results were categorized by work arrangement and are shown below.\n\n|  | Plan to choose<br>Health Plan A | Plan to choose<br>Health Plan B |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Remote | $110$ | $90$ |\n| On-site | $140$ | $160$ |\n\nThe corporation has $34{,}000$ employees in total. Based on the table, what is the best estimate of the number of employees who plan to choose Health Plan A?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212844 1072 #uuid "90fc821d-ef4b-5ac4-a469-66a85a27feb1" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212844 1074 "First, we total the number in the sample who plan to choose Plan A:\n\n$$\n110 + 140 = 250\n$$\n\nOut of the $500$ people surveyed, the proportion who favor Plan A is\n\n$$\n\\frac{250}{500} = 0.5 = 50\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $50\\%$ of those sampled intend to choose Plan A.\n\nTo estimate how many of the $34{,}000$ employees favor Plan A, we scale the proportion by the population:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n34,000 \\cdot 50% &= 34,000 \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 17,000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best estimate is that $17{,}000$ employees plan to choose Health Plan A." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212845 1069 "e-19012" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212845 1071 "At a beach event, $2{,}900$ people attended. The organizer randomly surveyed $145$ of them and found that $29$ bought sunglasses. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?\n\n1. Exactly $580$ attendees must have bought sunglasses.\n2. Exactly $290$ attendees must have bought sunglasses.\n3. The best estimate for the number of attendees who bought sunglasses is $580$.\n4. The best estimate for the number of attendees who bought sunglasses is $290$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212845 1072 #uuid "8f4404f7-6398-596c-9351-713c3dc6920e" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212845 1074 "From the random sample, the proportion who bought sunglasses is\n\n$$\n\\frac{29}{145} = 0.20 = 20\\%\n$$\n\nSo, $20\\%$ of those sampled bought sunglasses.\n\nTo estimate how many of the $2{,}900$ attendees bought sunglasses, we scale by this proportion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2,900 \\cdot 20% &= 2,900 \\cdot 0.20 \\\\\n&= 580\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause this is based on a sample, we report it as a *best estimate,* not an exact count.\n\nTherefore, the most appropriate conclusion is, \"The best estimate for the number of attendees who bought sunglasses is $580$.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212846 1069 "e-19013" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212846 1071 "An environmental group randomly surveyed $500$ hikers who reported supporting wilderness protection policies and showed them a new promotional video about forest fire prevention. Of those surveyed, $62\\%$ said they disliked the video. Which of the following inferences can appropriately be drawn from this survey result?\n\n1. Most hikers who support wilderness protection will dislike the video.\n2. At least $62\\%$ of all residents will dislike the video.\n3. At least $62\\%$ of all hikers will dislike the video.\n4. Most hikers who oppose wilderness protection will like the video." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212846 1072 #uuid "5571bf40-e1c2-538b-874e-53caed7b47a0" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212846 1074 "Because the sample is *random* and drawn only from hikers who support wilderness protection, any inference we make should describe the population of hikers who support wilderness protection, not all hikers or all residents.\n\nWe are told that $62\\%$ of the surveyed hikers disliked the video.\nThis supports the statement that most hikers who support wilderness protection dislike the forest fire prevention video.\n\nNote that the other inferences are not supported by the data:\n\n- The sampling frame excludes hikers who do not support wilderness protection, so we cannot generalize to all hikers.\n- Because the result is based on a sample, the correct language is “approximately” (or “best estimate”), not an exact claim.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion best supported by the data is that \"Most hikers who support wilderness protection will dislike the video.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212847 1069 "e-19014" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212847 1071 "A nonprofit surveyed $600$ volunteers who were selected at random from its membership list and asked, “Do you plan to volunteer at least once in the next three months?” Of those surveyed, $35\\%$ responded “no”.\n\nBased on this result, which of the following conclusions are appropriate?\n\n1. Of all volunteers in the nonprofit, exactly $35\\%$ do not plan to volunteer at least once in the next three months.\n2. If another $600$ volunteers selected at random from the nonprofit were surveyed, the proportion who say “no” is likely to be close to $35\\%$.\n3. If $600$ volunteers selected at random from a different nonprofit were surveyed, the proportion who say “no” is likely to be close to $35\\%$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212847 1072 #uuid "6af5e56d-8aca-5713-8bcc-7599b1efe859" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212847 1074 "Because the $600$ volunteers were chosen *at random* from the nonprofit, the $35\\%$ is a *sample proportion* that serves as a *best estimate* for the population proportion in this nonprofit. The exact value for the whole population is unknown, but another random sample from the *same nonprofit* would be expected to give a result close to $35\\%$ (though not necessarily exactly $35\\%$).\n\n- Statement I is not an appropriate conclusion. The true proportion in the nonprofit may differ slightly from $35\\%$ due to sampling variability.\n- Statement II is an appropriate conclusion. Repeating the sampling process with the same method and sample size from the same nonprofit would likely produce a proportion close to $35\\%$.\n- Statement III is not an appropriate conclusion. The inference applies only to this nonprofit, not to a different nonprofit that could have a different population proportion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “II only.”" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212848 1069 "e-19015" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212848 1071 "![](images/03/03a47d8c9911e6f074106854e4051a0e22134e7cf449aa9182164c6e76e3e204.png)\n\nThe circle with center $O$ is inscribed into the equilateral triangle $\\triangle XYZ$, as shown above. Fill in the blanks below.\n\n- $\\overline{OM}$ is the radius of the circle.\n- $\\triangle{XOY}$ is an isosceles triangle.\n- $\\angle{XOZ}$ has a measure of $120^\\circ$.\n- $\\triangle{XOM}$ is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle.\n- $\\angle{MYO}$ has a measure of $30^\\circ$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212848 1072 #uuid "5c7afb26-d5c2-5f3c-b456-ef955d660ceb" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212848 1074 "The line segments, drawn from the center of the circle to each of the triangle’s vertices, form three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/11/113afec6ef8de814a94699a7cab651d40fcbd77fd7705e776266dad2dbf62109.png)\n\nWe have the altitude $\\overline{OM}$ drawn in one of these isosceles triangles from the center of the circle to the point where the circle touches the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, a possible answer could be as follows:\n\n- $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\overline{OM}}$ is the radius of the circle.\n- $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\triangle{XOY}}$ is an isosceles triangle.\n- $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\angle{XOZ}}$ has a measure of $120^\\circ$.\n- $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\triangle{XOM}}$ is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle.\n- $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\angle{MYO}}$ has a measure of $30^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, all the statements are true." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212849 1069 "e-19016" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212849 1071 "A hospital wants to estimate the average number of hours nurses work per week.\n\nWhich of the following sampling methods is most likely to produce unbiased results?\n\n1. Randomly selecting nurses from the hospital’s complete employee database.\n2. Randomly selecting nurses from the list of those assigned to the emergency department.\n3. Randomly selecting nurses from the list of those who attended a recent training workshop." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212849 1072 #uuid "30153ac6-b133-5412-b23b-e3f1a179d967" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212849 1074 "An unbiased sample is one in which every member of the population has an equal chance of being included. To achieve this, we must select individual units at random from a complete list of the entire population.\n\nLet's examine each sampling method in turn.\n\n- **Method I:** Randomly selecting from the hospital’s complete employee database gives every nurse an equal chance of being chosen, so this method can produce unbiased results.\n- **Method II:** Randomly selecting from the emergency department list is biased because it excludes nurses working in other departments, leading to *undercoverage*. These nurses may have different work schedules.\n- **Method III:** Randomly selecting from recent training attendees is biased because it excludes nurses who did not attend that training, also leading to *undercoverage*. These nurses may have different workloads.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\nNote that Methods II and III are examples of *undercoverage*, where certain members of the population are systematically excluded from the sample." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212850 1069 "e-19017" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212850 1071 "A circle of radius $7 \\: \\text{cm}$ is inscribed into an equilateral triangle, as shown above. What is the area of the triangle?\n\n![](images/5d/5d825777b6613650f7afe511c58b21c0df41d65d842d18252fa5ec165e951dda.png)" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212850 1072 #uuid "2b44d18e-e42d-5ff5-a9c1-48b1a1ac15cb" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212850 1074 "To find the side length of the triangle, we draw line segments from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/be/be544e496d80cb95dc1c3260ea74f58a20b7db531f506871557c73c429871973.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- Let the longer leg, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, be $x$. Notice that it is half of our equilateral triangle's side length.\n- The shorter leg is the radius $r = 7 \\: \\text{cm}$ of the circle.\n\nNext, in the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the shorter leg:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n&= 7\\sqrt{3}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equilateral triangle's side length is\n\n$$\ns = 2 \\cdot 7 \\sqrt{3} = 14 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}s^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(14\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot 588 \\\\\n&= 147\\sqrt{3}cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212851 1069 "e-19018" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212851 1071 "A circle is inscribed into an equilateral triangle with a side length of $72$ feet, as shown above. What is the radius of the circle?\n\n![](images/da/da4eec9cfc4586722576b49ea51415dc626dad2850eceec56816580f4abe86a3.png)" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212851 1072 #uuid "2ab47dcc-c38e-5156-bd57-0c72e5c4102f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212851 1074 "To find the radius of the circle, we draw line segments from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35e2cda051f78f6ec01d4995552f893fa74eb61445645bc8f2f7b8c1858fe7eb.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$x = \\dfrac{ML}{2} = \\dfrac{72}{2} = 36$\n- The shorter leg is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the shorter leg:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n36 &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 12\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $12\\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212852 1069 "e-19019" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212852 1071 "A national library association wants to estimate how many books the average adult reads each year.\n\nWhich of the following sampling methods is most likely to produce unbiased results?\n\n1. Posting a survey on a popular book review website and asking visitors to respond.\n2. Asking people in the library café on a Saturday afternoon to complete a questionnaire.\n3. Randomly selecting adults from a national database of residents.\n4. Randomly choosing several groups of adults across the state, then asking for volunteers from each group to complete the survey." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212852 1072 #uuid "074fc4f4-97ba-5743-9e55-534b3a064df8" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212852 1074 "An unbiased sample is one in which every member of the population (here, all adults in the country) has an equal chance of being included. To achieve this, we must select individuals at random from a complete list of the population.\n\nLet's examine each method:\n\n- **Method I:** Posting on a book review site is *voluntary response sampling*, overrepresenting strong readers.\n- **Method II:** Asking people in the library café is *convenience sampling*, overrepresenting library users.\n- **Method III:** Randomly selecting from a national database of residents gives each registered resident an equal chance of being chosen, so it can produce **unbiased** results.\n- **Method IV:** Asking for volonteers from groups is *self-selection within groups*, it may attract people with strong opinions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212853 1069 "e-19020" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212853 1071 "A neighborhood has $320$ houses. A random sample of $40$ houses was taken, and among those surveyed, $25$ had a garden.\n\nBased on the sample data, which of the following is the best estimate of the total number of houses in the neighborhood that have a garden?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212853 1072 #uuid "d1c039c5-2dd0-5087-934e-c6cd738648f8" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212853 1074 "We are told there are $320$ houses in the neighborhood. In a sample of $40$ houses, $25$ had a garden.\n\nFirst, we find the proportion of houses in the sample that have a garden. Since $25$ houses out of a sample of $40$ have a garden, the required proportion is\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{40} = 0.625 = 62.5\\%\n$$\n\nSo, $62.5\\%$ of the houses in our sample have a garden.\n\nFinally, to estimate the number in the whole neighborhood that hava a garden, we multiply the total number of houses by $62.5\\%$:\n\n$$\n320 \\cdot 0.625 = 200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best estimate is that $200$ houses have a garden." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212854 1069 "e-19022" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212854 1071 "Factor the expression $17t^2-13$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212854 1072 #uuid "b08d537c-84f9-5e99-9d0c-2f1c0347b0c7" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212854 1074 "Notice that\n\n$$\n17 = \\big(\\sqrt{17}\\big)^2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 13 = \\big(\\sqrt{13}\\big)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17t^{2} - 13 &= (\\sqrt{17}t)^{2} - (\\sqrt{13})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{17}t)^{2} - (\\sqrt{13})^{2} = (\\sqrt{17}t - \\sqrt{13})(\\sqrt{17}t + \\sqrt{13})\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212855 1069 "e-19023" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212855 1071 "Factor the expression $\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212855 1072 #uuid "11d3b3ea-12ee-5d4a-b66b-0f312638e31f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212855 1074 "The terms of our expression have a common factor of $4$. So, we simplify the expression by factoring it out:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m^2}{5}-9\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{m^{2}}{5} - 9 &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2} - 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} - 3)(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} - 3 \\right)\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} + 3 \\right)\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212856 1069 "e-19024" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212856 1071 "Factor the expression $-5r^2+ 36s^2$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212856 1072 #uuid "c2ba3085-2a74-5128-83c2-77bbd9750aea" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212856 1074 "Notice that\n\n$$\n5 = \\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5r^{2} + 36s^{2} &=  \\\\\n36s^{2} - 5r^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (6s)^{2} - (\\sqrt{5}r)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(6s)^{2} - (\\sqrt{5}r)^{2} = (6s - \\sqrt{5}r)(6s + \\sqrt{5}r)\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212857 1069 "e-19025" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212857 1071 "A survey was conducted using a sample of truck drivers randomly selected from shipping companies in Georgia. The drivers surveyed were asked about the average number of miles they drive each week. What is the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized?\n\n1. All truck drivers in Georgia shipping companies\n2. All truck drivers in the United States\n3. All truck drivers in Georgia\n4. All transportation workers in the United States" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212857 1072 #uuid "174051e5-eaad-5e54-9be0-b80f8936dff6" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212857 1074 "Because the sample was *randomly selected* from *truck drivers in Georgia shipping companies*, the sampling frame only includes members of this group. Therefore, any inference should be limited to this same population.\n\n- We *cannot* generalize to all truck drivers in Georgia, because the survey only included those working for *shipping companies*.\n- We *cannot* generalize to all truck drivers in the United States, because the survey only included those in Georgia.\n- We *cannot* generalize to all transportation workers in the United States for the same reason.\n\nThe largest population to which the survey results can be generalized is, \"All truck drivers in Georgia shipping companies.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212858 1069 "e-19026" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212858 1071 "A transportation agency is studying car ownership in a suburban neighborhood. The neighborhood has $1{,}500$ households. Surveyors randomly select $75$ households and record the number of cars owned by each household.\n\nWhat are the *sampling units* in this study?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212858 1072 #uuid "c6dafb15-1a50-512c-8563-0caeee622a50" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212858 1074 "The *sampling units* are the *individual elements* of a population from which observations are taken and could be selected in a sample.\n\nIn this case, the sampling units are the *individual households* in the suburban neighborhood. Each household is the basic unit selected for the sample, and the number of cars is recorded for each one. Although the data involve the people in the households, the selection is at the household level." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212859 1069 "e-19027" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212859 1071 "A university IT department wants to estimate the proportion of computers on campus running the latest operating system. They obtain a complete list of the $3{,}200$ device inventory numbers from the campus asset database and randomly select $150$ computers to check.\n\nWhat is the *sampling frame* in this study?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212859 1072 #uuid "146706df-f6d7-5767-ba74-30fc44913895" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212859 1074 "A *sampling frame* is the list or set of *sampling units* from which a sample is drawn.\n\nIn this case, the sampling frame is the *list of all $3{,}200$ device inventory numbers* obtained from the campus asset database. This is the set of all possible computers (the sampling units) that could be selected for the check." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212860 1069 "e-19029" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212860 1071 "${-\\frac{1}{6}x + \\frac{1}{4}y = \\frac{5}{12}; \\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{t}{12}y =-\\frac{5}{6}$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $t$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212860 1072 #uuid "a7daa55a-4856-5bc4-80ab-3eef823e1de6" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212860 1074 "We start by eliminating fractions from both equations to make the comparison easier. To do that, we\n\n- multiply both sides of the first equation by $12$ (the least common multiple of $6$, $4$, and $12$), and\n- multiply both sides of the second equation by $12$ (the least common multiple of $2$, $6$, and $12$).\n\nThis gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{12 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}x + \\frac{1}{4}y) = 12 \\cdot \\frac{5}{12}; 12 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{t}{12}y) = 12 \\cdot (-\\frac{5}{6})\n$$\n\nA system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line.\nThe two equations represent the same line if one equation is a non-zero constant multiple of the other.\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all the corresponding coefficients and constants must be proportional:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-2}{4} = \\dfrac{3}{t} = \\dfrac{5}{-10}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-2}{4} = \\dfrac{5}{-10} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the value of $t$, we focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{t}\n$$\n\nSolving for $t$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{1}{2} &= \\frac{3}{t} \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot t &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n- t &= 6 \\\\\nt &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212861 1069 "e-19030" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212861 1071 "${-9 + 4y = tx; 3y + x =-5x + 8$\nConsider the system of equations above, where $t$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $t$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212861 1072 #uuid "b06fcc57-873a-5514-8c5b-c8b777f6f645" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212861 1074 "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent distinct parallel lines. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet's start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n$-9 + 4y|= tx; 4y|= tx + 9; y|= \\frac{t}{4}x + \\frac{9}{4}$.\nSo, the slope is $\\dfrac{t}{4}$ and the $y$-intercept is $\\dfrac{9}{4}$.\n- For the second equation, we have\n$3y + x|=-5x + 8; 3y|=-6x + 8; y|=-2x + \\frac{8}{3}$.\nSo, the slope is $-2$ and the $y$-intercept is $\\dfrac{8}{3}$.\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts\n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{4}\\ \\text{and}\\ \\dfrac{8}{3}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nthe slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, equating the slopes and solving for $t$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{t}{4} &= -2 \\\\\nt &= -2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\nt &= -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $t$ is $-8$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212862 1069 "e-19031" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212862 1071 "A meteorological research institute wants to estimate the average annual rainfall in remote villages across a large desert region. Why might the institute choose to use a sample instead of conducting a census?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212862 1072 #uuid "3079108f-c7c4-5d1e-953b-417520d59457" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212862 1074 "Advantages of sampling:\n\n- Sampling is generally cheaper and faster than taking a census.\n- Sample data is more readily available, especially when the population is large or widely dispersed.\n- Sampling is useful when testing items leads to their destruction (e.g., testing for the average lifetime of a battery).\n- Sampling can allow more intensive study of each unit (e.g., collecting more detailed measurements than would be feasible in a census).\n\nIn this case, traveling to every remote village in the desert to record rainfall data would be extremely time-consuming, costly, and logistically challenging. By selecting a representative sample of villages from different parts of the region, the institute can still estimate the average rainfall without needing to visit every single village.\n\nSampling is more practical for gathering data when the population is widely dispersed." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212863 1069 "e-19032" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212863 1071 "A large university wants to record the total number of publications authored by each faculty member in the current academic year.\n\nWhy might the university decide to conduct a census instead of using a sample to gather this information?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212863 1072 #uuid "fdb24964-3957-5b27-86a6-95f4b1ac23c9" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212863 1074 "Advantages of a census:\n\n- Provides data for every unit in the population.\n- Eliminates sampling error and bias, since all units are measured.\n- Allows detailed analysis of small subgroups within the population.\n\nIn this case, a census will gather information from every faculty member in the university. This approach eliminates sampling error and bias, because all units in the population are measured rather than a subset.\n\nConducting a census ensures data is collected from every faculty member, eliminating sampling error and bias." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212864 1069 "e-19033" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212864 1071 "${\\frac{5}{6}x - \\frac{1}{3}y = \\frac{7}{2}; rx - 6y = s$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $r$ and $s$ are constants. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $\\dfrac{r}{s}$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212864 1072 #uuid "00eb2c5e-e622-53e9-8c6f-9f95afd19a8c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212864 1074 "We start by eliminating fractions from both equations to make the comparison easier. To do that, we multiply both sides of the first equation by $6$ (the least common multiple of $2$, $3$, and $6$). This gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{6 \\cdot (\\frac{5}{6}x - \\frac{1}{3}y) = 6 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2}; rx - 6y = s\n$$\n\nA system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line.\nThe two equations represent the same line if one equation is a non-zero constant multiple of the other.\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all the corresponding coefficients and constants must be proportional:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{r} = \\dfrac{-2}{-6} = \\dfrac{21}{s}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nwe focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{r} = \\dfrac{21}{s}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nwe start by cross-multiplying, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5s &= 21r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides of the equation by $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5s}{s} &= \\frac{21r}{s} \\\\\n5 &= \\frac{21r}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides of the equation by $21$ yields the desired quantity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= \\frac{21r}{s} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{21} &= \\frac{21r}{21s} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{21} &= \\frac{r}{s} \\\\\n\\frac{r}{s} &= \\frac{5}{21}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212865 1069 "e-19035" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212865 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212865 1072 #uuid "9707f903-49f4-592f-a266-a7837f7f5542" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212865 1074 "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - 1) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212866 1069 "e-19036" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212866 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t$ as a power of $\\dfrac43$ only." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212866 1072 #uuid "ed802710-305b-5ee1-a5c4-02808862e7af" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212866 1074 "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{4}{3})^{3}(\\frac{4}{3})^{t} &= (\\frac{4}{3})^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212867 1069 "e-19037" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212867 1071 "Given that $15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x) = k(3^x)$, find the value of $k$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212867 1072 #uuid "e267bdeb-c900-591b-acfb-673ed2e3c454" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212867 1074 "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212868 1069 "e-19038" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212868 1071 "Express $5^{2k}\\,4^{k}$ in the form $n^k$, where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212868 1072 #uuid "e8e8010e-054c-57bd-af92-14e59be0a637" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212868 1074 "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n$5^{2k}\\,4^{k}$,\ninvolves the factor $5^{2k}$. However, using the power rule, we can express this as a power of $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n5^{2k} = \\left(5^{2}\\right)^k\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{2k}4^{k} &= (5^{2})^{k}4^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5^2 = 25$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(5^{2}\\right)^k \\, 4^k = 25^k \\, 4^k\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power of the product rule, we can write this as\n\n$$\n25^k \\, 4^k = \\left(100\\right)^k\n$$\n\nHence, $n = 100$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212869 1069 "e-19039" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212869 1071 "A local library surveyed a random sample of patrons about adding more weekend activities. All selected patrons responded. It was estimated that $31\\%$ of patrons supported the addition, with a margin of error of $5\\%$.\n\nFind the plausible interval for the percentage of patrons who supported the addition." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212869 1072 #uuid "8f399635-3f89-56d0-ac9a-107804f2de21" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212869 1074 "The estimate is $31\\%$ with a margin of error of $5\\%$, so we compute the plausible bounds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLower bound &= 31% - 5% = 26% \\\\\nUpper bound &= 31% + 5% = 36%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, plausible values of the percent of patrons who support the addition are between $26\\%$ and $36\\%$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212870 1069 "e-19040" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212870 1071 "A sports scientist measured the average sprint time for a random sample of soccer players. Based on the data, the estimated mean sprint time for $100$ meters was $11.4$ seconds, with a margin of error of $0.3$ seconds.\n\nGiven this information, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. It is plausible that the mean sprint time for all soccer players is between $11.1$ and $11.7$ seconds.\n2. It is possible that some players take less than $11$ seconds to complete $100$ meters.\n3. It is **NOT** possible that some players take $12$ seconds or more to complete $100$ meters." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212870 1072 #uuid "39873e7d-ab7e-5c21-815b-848c6c75b9e0" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212870 1074 "The estimated mean sprint time is $11.4$ seconds with a margin of error of $0.3$ seconds.\nWe calculate the plausible bounds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLower bound &= 11.4 - 0.3 = 11.1 \\\\\nUpper bound &= 11.4 + 0.3 = 11.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe margin of error bounds the *population mean*, not individual sprint times, so individual values can be below $11.1$ seconds or above $11.7$ seconds.\n\nWith that in mind, we have the following:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n- Statement II is true.\n- Statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212871 1069 "e-19041" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212871 1071 "A battery warehouse stores $7{,}200$ batteries. To estimate the percentage that are rechargeable, a random sample was taken. The percent of rechargeable batteries was estimated to be $68\\%$, with a margin of error of $5\\%$.\n\nFind the plausible interval for the actual number of rechargeable batteries in the warehouse." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212871 1072 #uuid "107ee8d8-9e87-5fcf-a38a-0d6aae3db1ad" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212871 1074 "The estimate is $68\\%$ with a margin of error of $5\\%$, so we compute the plausible bounds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLower percent &= 68% - 5% = 63% \\\\\nUpper percent &= 68% + 5% = 73%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert these percentages to totals using $7{,}200$ total batteries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLower total &= 63% \\cdot 7,200 \\\\\n&= 0.63 \\cdot 7,200 \\\\\n&= 4,536 \\\\\nUpper total &= 73% \\cdot 7,200 \\\\\n&= 0.73 \\cdot 7,200 \\\\\n&= 5,256\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a plausible range for the actual number of rechargeable batteries is between $4{,}536$ and $5{,}256$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212872 1069 "e-19042" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212872 1071 "Find the measure of the angle that's complementary to the angle of $4x - 18^\\circ$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212872 1072 #uuid "45744bb5-0025-5e45-b6a7-8615c70aa159" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212872 1074 "Two angles are complementary if the sum of their measures is equal to $90^\\circ$. So, the measure of the angle that's complementary to the angle of $4x - 18^\\circ$ must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90^{∘} - (4x - 18^{∘}) &= 90^{∘} - 4x + 18^{∘} \\\\\n&= 108^{∘} - 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212873 1069 "e-19047" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212873 1071 "Simplify $\\left(\\sqrt{x^5y^6} - \\sqrt{y^7}\\right)\\sqrt{x^3}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212873 1072 #uuid "4b56fbbc-24c8-577d-86d6-cef96471d77f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212873 1074 "First, we write radicals as fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x^{5}y^{6}} - \\sqrt{y^{7}})\\sqrt{x^{3}} &= ((x^{5}y^{6})^{1/2} - (y^{7})^{1/2})(x^{3})^{1/2} \\\\\n&= (x^{5/2}y^{3} - y^{7/2})x^{3/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the distributive law, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^{5/2} y^3 - y^{7/2} \\right)x^{3/2} = x^{5/2} y^3 \\cdot x^{3/2} - y^{7/2} \\cdot x^{3/2}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the terms by combining the rules of exponents with algebraic expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{5/2}y^{3} \\cdot x^{3/2} - y^{7/2} \\cdot x^{3/2} &= (x^{5/2} \\cdot x^{3/2})y^{3} - x^{3/2}y^{7/2} \\\\\n&= x^{5/2 + 3/2}y^{3} - x^{3/2}y^{7/2} \\\\\n&= x^{8/2}y^{3} - x^{3/2}y^{7/2} \\\\\n&= x^{4}y^{3} - x^{3/2}y^{7/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write fractional exponents as radicals:\n\n$$\nx^4 y^3 - x^{3/2} y^{7/2} = x^4 y^3 - \\sqrt{x^3 y^7}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212874 1069 "e-19048" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212874 1071 "${4(x - 2) + 3(2y + 1) = 39; - 4(x - 2) + 3(2y + 1) = 21$\n\nThe equations above form a system, and the solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $3(2y + 1)$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212874 1072 #uuid "cdd6a18b-de0d-5c38-a684-7f4982fc7d8d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212874 1074 "First, we let $w=x-2$ and\n\n$$\nz=2y+1\n$$\n\nso that the system may be written as follows:\n\n$$\n{4w + 3z = 39; - 4w + 3z = 21\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that when we add the two equations, the $4w$ terms will cancel:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4w + 3z &= 39 \\\\\n- 4w + 3z &= 21 \\\\\n(3z + 3z) &= (39 + 21) \\\\\n6z &= 60 \\\\\nz &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nz=2y+1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2y + 1) &= 3z \\\\\n&= 3(10) \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212875 1069 "e-19050" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212875 1071 "The function\n$v(t) = 2,250 \\cdot (1.09)^{(1/6)t}$\nmodels the number of video views $t$ seconds after a clip is uploaded.\nWhat function models the number of video views $m$ minutes after the clip is uploaded?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212875 1072 #uuid "7b8e51ec-649e-5fa4-b900-03daa2325633" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212875 1074 "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nv(t) = 2,250 \\cdot (1.09)^{(1/6)t}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the number of seconds after upload. We want to rewrite this model in terms of minutes rather than seconds.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=60m$ into the original equation.\n- We *multiply* by $60$ (not divide) because we want $m=1$ minute to correspond to $t=60$ seconds.\n\nSubstituting, we get the model\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(m) = v(60m) &= 2, 250 \\cdot (1.09)^{(1/6) \\cdot (60m)} \\\\\n&= 2, 250 \\cdot (1.09)^{10m}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis models the number of video views as a function of time in minutes after the clip is uploaded." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212876 1069 "e-19051" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212876 1071 "${x + y = 10; 4(x + y) + 2y = 52$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212876 1072 #uuid "6dab0ec3-03d2-582b-b763-7c5a0df4fe72" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212876 1074 "Notice that the expression $(x+y)$ occurs in both equations.\n\nFrom the first equation, we have that\n\n$$\nx+y = 10\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute this value into the second equation to solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x + y) + 2y &= 52 \\\\\n4(10) + 2y &= 52 \\\\\n40 + 2y &= 52 \\\\\n2y &= 52 - 40 \\\\\n2y &= 12 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{12}{2} \\\\\ny &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $6$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212877 1069 "e-19052" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212877 1071 "${6x - 13y = 80; 13x - 6y = 72$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(x, y)$, what is the value of $x - y$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212877 1072 #uuid "8f8a1bc1-513b-5f6f-b893-5f5d81953d29" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212877 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation differ by a factor of $-1$, and vice versa.\n\n$$\n{6x- 13y = 80; 13x- 6y = 72\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we add the equations, the variables will have the same coefficient.\n\nFirst, we add the two equations and group like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 13y &= 80 \\\\\n13x - 6y &= 72 \\\\\n(6x + 13x) + (-13y - 6y) &= (80 + 72) \\\\\n19x - 19y &= 152\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the resulting equation for $x - y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n19x - 19y &= 152 \\\\\n19(x - y) &= 152 \\\\\nx - y &= \\frac{152}{19} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of $x-y$ is $8$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212878 1069 "e-19053" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212878 1071 "The function\n$C(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}$\nmodels the number of data packets processed, in thousands, by a server $t$ minutes after a throughput upgrade. According to the model, in how many seconds is the number of packets expected to increase by $5\\%$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212878 1072 #uuid "e1521bf1-21d2-513a-842b-d2187c64e34d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212878 1074 "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nC(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the number of data packets processed, in thousands, $t$ minutes after the upgrade. We want to rewrite this model in terms of seconds rather than minutes.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{s}{60}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $60$ (not multiply) because we want $s=60$ seconds to correspond to $t=1$ minute.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $c(s)$ for the number of packets as a function of time in seconds $s$ after the upgrade:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc(s) = C(\\frac{s}{60}) &= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2) \\cdot (s/60)} \\\\\n&= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{s/24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe base $1.05$ means that the number of packets grows by $5\\%$ each time the exponent increases by $1$, i.e., once every growth period.\n\nThe exponent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{s}{24}\n$$\n\nshows that for every increase in $s=24$ seconds, the exponent increases by $1$.\n\nTherefore, the number of packets is expected to increase by $5\\%$ every ${24}$ seconds." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212879 1069 "e-19054" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212879 1071 "The function\n$k(t) = 650 \\cdot (1.04)^{(2/5)t}$\nmodels the number of messages sent $t$ seconds after a chat stream begins. According to the model, what percentage are messages predicted to increase by every $1$ minute? Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212879 1072 #uuid "3a54592b-b5f5-518c-a932-d8d923e5c990" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212879 1074 "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nk(t) = 650 \\cdot (1.04)^{(2/5)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents messages $t$ seconds after the stream begins. We want to rewrite this model in terms of minutes instead of seconds.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=60m$ into the original equation.\n- We *multiply* by $60$ (not divide) because we want $m=1$ minute to correspond to $t=60$ seconds.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $K(m)$ for messages as a function of time in minutes $m$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK(m) = k(60m) &= 650 \\cdot (1.04)^{(2/5) \\cdot (60m)} \\\\\n&= 650 \\cdot (1.04)^{24m}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the percentage increase over $1$ minute. Inspecting the model in terms of minutes $K(m)$, we see that the growth factor over $m$ minutes is\n$(1.04)^{24m}$.\n\nSo, the growth factor over $1$ minute is\n\n$$\n(1.04)^{24} \\approx 2.563\\,304\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an increase of approximately\n\n$$\n2.563\\,304 - 1 = 1.563\\,304 \\approx 156.33\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, messages are predicted to increase by $156.33\\%$ every $1$ minute." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212880 1069 "e-19056" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212880 1071 "${2x - 2y =-6; (y - x)(x + y) = 15$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x+y$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212880 1072 #uuid "880b785e-aeac-5607-8974-30d06c9fbc03" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212880 1074 "First, we write both equations of the given system in factored form:\n\n$$\n{-2(y - x) =-6; (y - x)(x + y) = 15\n$$\n\nNotice that $(y-x)$ is a common factor in both equations.\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $(y-x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(y - x) &= -6 \\\\\ny - x &= \\frac{-6}{-2} \\\\\ny - x &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the result in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - x)(x + y) &= 15 \\\\\n(3) \\cdot (x + y) &= 15 \\\\\nx + y &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\\nx + y &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x+y$ is $5$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212881 1069 "e-19057" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212881 1071 "${5x - \\frac{7}{11} = \\sqrt{3}; y =-2(\\frac{7}{11} - 5x)^{2}$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212881 1072 #uuid "cb100771-08ec-5284-bbda-3a7f09c3b496" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212881 1074 "From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n5x-\\dfrac{7}{11}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply this equation by $-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{11} - 5x = -\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the expression on the left-hand side features in the second equation.\n\nSo next, we substitute $-\\sqrt{3}$ for\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{11}-5x\\right)\n$$\n\nin the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2(\\frac{7}{11} - 5x)^{2} \\\\\n&=-2(-\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $-6$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212882 1069 "e-19058" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212882 1071 "${4x + 8y = 32; 4y^{2} - x^{2} =-32$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212882 1072 #uuid "6a267aba-0b27-5ad8-b1aa-0dee05bc7ab2" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212882 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n4y^2-x^2 = (2y)^2-x^2\n$$\n\nis a difference of squares, so we apply the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(2y)^2-x^2=(2y+x)(2y-x)\n$$\n\nThus, we write both equations of the given system in factored form:\n\n$$\n{4(2y + x) = 32; (2y + x)(2y - x) =-32\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2y+x)$ is a common factor in both equations.\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $(2y+x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(2y + x) &= 32 \\\\\n2y + x &= \\frac{32}{4} \\\\\n2y + x &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the result in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2y + x)(2y - x) &= -32 \\\\\n8 \\cdot (2y - x) &= -32 \\\\\n2y - x &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we get the following system with linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2y + x = 8; 2y - x =-4\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation,\n\n$$\nx = 2y+4\n$$\n\nwhich we substitute into the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y + (2y + 4) &= 8 \\\\\n4y + 4 &= 8 \\\\\n4y &= 4 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $1$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212883 1069 "e-19059" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212883 1071 "| Online Order Value $(\\[math])$ | Number of Orders |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0-25$ | $8$ |\n| $25-50$ | $12$ |\n| $50-75$ | $15$ |\n| $75-100$ | $9$ |\n| $100-150$ | $6$ |\n\nThe table above shows the distribution of online order values. If an order between $\\[/math]25$ and $\\[math]100$ is selected at random, what is the probability that the order is between $\\[/math]50$ and $\\[math]75$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212883 1072 #uuid "f6dd94ec-61dc-5189-a199-2acf75af44fb" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212883 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of orders between $\\[math]50$ and $\\[/math]75$ among those between $\\[math]25$ and $\\[/math]100$:\n\n$$\nP(50 \\le Value \\le 75 \\mid 25 \\mid \\le Value \\le 100); = \\frac{# orders between \\[math]25 and \\[/math]100 and between \\[math]50 and \\[/math]75}{# orders between \\[math]25 and \\[/math]100}; = \\frac{# orders between \\[math]50 and \\[/math]75}{# orders between \\[math]25 and \\[/math]100}\n$$\n\nThe total number of orders between $\\[math]25$ and $\\[/math]100$ is the sum of the orders in the $\\[math]25 - 50$, $\\[/math]50 - 75$, and $\\[math]75 - 100$ groups:\n\n$$\n12 + 15 + 9 = 36\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the number of orders between $\\[/math]50$ and $\\[math]75$ is the number of orders in the $\\[/math]50 - 75$ group:\n$15$\n\nTherefore, the fraction of orders between $\\[math]50$ and $\\[/math]75$ out of those between $\\[math]25$ and $\\[/math]100$ is\n\n$$\nP(50 \\leq \\text{Value} \\leq 75 \\: \\mid \\: 25 \\leq \\text{Value} \\leq 100) = \\dfrac{15}{36} = \\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212884 1069 "e-19060" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212884 1071 "|  | Full-Time | Part-Time | Contract | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Managers | $21$ | $10$ | $9$ | $40$ |\n| Assistants | $14$ | $11$ | $12$ | $37$ |\n| Totals | $35$ | $21$ | $21$ | $77$ |\n\nThe table above shows the employment types of different groups of employees. If an employee from the Managers group is selected at random, what is the probability that they are either full-time or contract?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212884 1072 #uuid "0787edbc-66f5-5164-a790-eba64489df16" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212884 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of employees who are full-time or contract among those who are managers:\n\n$$\n\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! P(\\text{Full-Time or Contract} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Managers}) = \\dfrac{\\text{# Managers working full-time or contract}}{\\text{# Managers}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of $\\color{red}{40}$ managers are listed. Of these,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{21}} + {\\color{blue}{9}} = 30\n$$\n\nare full-time or contract employees.\n\n|  | Full-Time | Part-Time | Contract | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Managers | $\\color{blue}{21}$ | $10$ | $\\color{blue}{9}$ | $\\color{red}{40}$ |\n| Assistants | $14$ | $11$ | $12$ | $37$ |\n| Totals | $35$ | $21$ | $21$ | $77$ |\n\nTherefore, the fraction of employees who are full-time or contract among those who are managers is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Full-Time or Contract} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Managers}) = \\dfrac{30}{40} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212885 1069 "e-19061" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212885 1071 "The function\n$P(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}$\nmodels the population of bacteria in a sealed container $t$ weeks after a chemical is introduced. According to the model, what percentage is the population predicted to decrease by every $2$ days? Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212885 1072 #uuid "5adc477e-3e18-5c72-83c4-696d8427b979" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212885 1074 "Explanation:\n\nWe are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nP(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the population $t$ weeks after the chemical is introduced. We want to rewrite this model in terms of days instead of weeks.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $7$ days in a week, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{d}{7}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $7$ (not multiply) because we want $d=7$ days to correspond to $t=1$ week.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $p(d)$ for the population as a function of time in days $d$ after the chemical is introduced:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(d) = P(\\frac{d}{7}) &= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4) \\cdot (d/7)} \\\\\n&= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{3d/28}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the percentage decrease over $2$ days. Inspecting the model in terms of days $p(d)$, we see that the decay factor over $d$ days is\n$(0.95)^{3d/28}$.\n\nSo, the decay factor over $2$ days is\n\n$$\n(0.95)^{3\\cdot 2/28} = (0.95)^{3/14} \\approx 0.989\\,069\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to a decrease of approximately\n\n$$\n1 - 0.989\\,069 = 0.010\\,931 \\approx 1.09\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the population is predicted to decrease by $1.09\\%$ every $2$ days." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212886 1069 "e-19062" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212886 1071 "A survey records whether people are vegetarians and whether they prefer organic food. The data is summarized in the table below.\n\n|  | Prefers Organic | Prefers Conventional |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Vegetarians | $24$ | $x$ |\n| Non-Vegetarians | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nComplete the frequency table given that, if a person who prefers conventional food is selected at random, the probability that the person is a vegetarian is $\\dfrac{3}{8}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212886 1072 #uuid "fcf6307d-e076-5c9b-ad5f-55bb871f4d3d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212886 1074 "We are told that\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Vegetarian} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Conventional}) = \\dfrac38\n$$\n\nThis probability is the fraction of vegetarians among those who prefer conventional food:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Vegetarian} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Conventional}) = \\dfrac{\\text{# Vegetarians who prefer conventional food}}{\\text{# People who prefer conventional food}}\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the number of vegetarians who prefer conventional food. From the table, we can see that a total of\n$\\color{red}20 + x$\npeople prefer conventional food. Of these, $\\color{blue}x$ are vegetarians.\n\n|  | Prefers Organic | Prefers Conventional |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Vegetarians | $24$ | $\\color{blue}{x}$ |\n| Non-Vegetarians | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the fraction of vegetarians among those who prefer conventional food is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Vegetarian} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Conventional}) = \\dfrac{x}{20+x}\n$$\n\nSince this probability is equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac38\n$$\n\nwe can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{8} &= \\frac{x}{20 + x} \\\\\n3(20 + x) &= 8x \\\\\n60 + 3x &= 8x \\\\\n5x &= 60 \\\\\nx &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is shown below.\n\n|  | Prefers Organic | Prefers Conventional |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Vegetarians | $24$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{12}$ |\n| Non-Vegetarians | $16$ | $20$ |" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212887 1069 "e-19066" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212887 1071 "$5w + 3j = 90$\nLucas filled $5$ water tanks that held $w$ liters each and $3$ jugs that held $j$ liters each. If the given equation represents this situation, which of the following is the best interpretation of $90$ in this context?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212887 1072 #uuid "c657325b-a234-5a1f-a429-cc7c775c03d3" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212887 1074 "In the given equation,\n\n- the term $5w$ represents the total water in the tanks, wherethe coefficient $5$ represents the number of tanks, and the variable $w$ denotes the capacity of each tank;\n- the term $3j$ represents the total water in the jugs, wherethe coefficient $3$ represents the number of jugs, and the variable $j$ denotes the capacity of each jug.\n\nSo, the left-hand side, $5w + 3j$, gives the total water Lucas filled, in liters.\n\nTherefore, the right-hand side, $90$, also represents *the total water filled, in liters, in all tanks and jugs.*" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212888 1069 "e-19067" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212888 1071 "A reading club completed a total of $3{,}900$ pages last week. The equation\n$210a + 120b = 3{,}900$\nrepresents this situation, where $a$ is the number of long sessions and $b$ is the number of short sessions held. How many more pages does each long session take than each short session?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212888 1072 #uuid "54326c2a-12b5-584e-ab13-92ded92f07b5" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212888 1074 "In the given equation,\n\n- the term $210a$ represents the total number of pages read in long sessions, and\n- the term $120b$ represents the total number of pages read in short sessions.\n\nWe know that there were $a$ long sessions and $b$ short sessions last week.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the coefficient $210$ gives the number of pages in each long session, and\n- the coefficient $120$ gives the number of pages in each short session.\n\nTo find how many more pages each long session has than each short session, we subtract $120$ from $210{:}$\n\n$$\n210 - 120 = 90\n$$\n\nTherefore, each long session has $90$ more pages than each short session." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212889 1069 "e-19068" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212889 1071 "Two friends are reading books. Alice read $5$ books, and Bob read $15$ books. Together they read $1{,}200$ pages. The equation\n$5p + 15q = 1{,}200$\nrepresents this situation. What is the best interpretation of $q$ in this context?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212889 1072 #uuid "233dea1d-146e-5bf1-88b0-8541ce14f226" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212889 1074 "In the given equation,\n\n- the term $5p$ represents the total pages read by Alice, and\n- the term $15q$ represents the total pages read by Bob.\n\nWe know that Bob read $15$ books.\n\nTherefore, the variable $q$ gives *the* ***average*** *number of pages per book read by Bob.*" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212890 1069 "e-19070" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212890 1071 "What value of $x$ is the solution to the equation $5x + 2(x - 3) = 29$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212890 1072 #uuid "7ab11967-df9d-5af4-a318-edab728a6c15" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212890 1074 "First, we expand the expression on the left-hand side and simplify by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 2(x - 3) &= 5x + 2x - 6 \\\\\n&= 7x - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x - 6 &= 29\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $x$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x - 6 &= 29 \\\\\n7x - 6 + 6 &= 29 + 6 \\\\\n7x &= 35 \\\\\n\\frac{7x}{7} &= \\frac{35}{7} \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $x=5$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212891 1069 "e-19072" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212891 1071 "If $\\dfrac{5}{8}y - \\dfrac{2}{8}y = 6$, what is the value of $y$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212891 1072 #uuid "c5d5bdf0-e898-500f-9205-c98bf56a4d98" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212891 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8}y - \\dfrac{2}{8}y = 6\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $y$.\n\nFirst, we simplify the left-hand side by combining like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{8}y - \\frac{2}{8}y &= (\\frac{5}{8} - \\frac{2}{8})y \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{8}y &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ by multiplying both sides of the equation by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\n:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 6 \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $y$ that satisfies the equation is $16$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212892 1069 "e-19073" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212892 1071 "What value of $m$ is the solution to the equation $8m = 72 - m$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212892 1072 #uuid "59160848-e355-5fe4-9d62-3af3b8fade90" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212892 1074 "We are given the equation $8m = 72 - m$ and are asked to find the value of $m$ that satisfies it.\n\nFirst, we isolate the variable on one side of the equation by adding $m$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8m + m &= 72 - m + m \\\\\n9m &= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides of the equation by $9$ to solve for $m$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212893 1069 "e-19075" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212893 1071 "What value of $b$ satisfies the equation $9b - 81 = 108$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212893 1072 #uuid "e1d18e5e-3192-5cb9-b5af-9c99cda8c35b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212893 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n9b - 81 = 108\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $b$.\n\nTo isolate the term $9b$, we add $81$ to both sides of the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9b &= 108 + 81 \\\\\n&= 189\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $9$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= \\frac{189}{9} \\\\\n&= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $b$ that satisfies the equation is $21$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212894 1069 "e-19077" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212894 1071 "$2.1 + z = 5.0$\nWhat value of $z$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212894 1072 #uuid "bb9372e9-694d-5f12-bfff-34d7bbd6efe2" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212894 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n2.1 + z = 5.0\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $z$.\n\nTo isolate the variable $z$, we subtract $2.1$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= 5.0 - 2.1 \\\\\n&= 2.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212895 1069 "e-19079" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212895 1071 "If $6p + 8 = 26$, what is the value of $2p - 3$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212895 1072 #uuid "4ba07b42-665b-5fb4-b968-6fc905373ba6" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212895 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n6p + 8 = 26\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $2p - 3$.\n\nFirst, we solve the equation for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6p + 8 &= 26 \\\\\n6p &= 18 \\\\\np &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $p = 3$ into $2p - 3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2p - 3 &= 2(3) - 3 \\\\\n&= 6 - 3 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $2p - 3$ is $3$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212896 1069 "e-19080" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212896 1071 "If $11k = 77$, what is the value of $6k$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212896 1072 #uuid "7a32502e-c5d3-522b-9643-ed368fa77035" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212896 1074 "We are given the equation $11k = 77$ and are asked to find the value of $6k$.\n\nFirst, we solve the equation for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11k &= 77 \\\\\nk &= \\frac{77}{11} \\\\\nk &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $k = 7$ into the expression $6k$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6k &= 6 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $6k$ is $42$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212897 1069 "e-19081" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212897 1071 "Calculate $m + 4$ given that $8m = 64$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212897 1072 #uuid "023ccb68-14cb-524a-aefe-9b5454277c31" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212897 1074 "We are given the equation $8m = 64$ and are asked to find the value of $m + 4$.\n\nTo isolate the variable $m$, we divide both sides of the equation by $8$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{64}{8} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $m = 8$ into $m + 4$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm + 4 &= 8 + 4 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212898 1069 "e-19082" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212898 1071 "$t + 32 = 90$\nWhat value of $t$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212898 1072 #uuid "f53dc545-c4c8-5ef9-9850-c2802e192d80" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212898 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nt + 32 = 90\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $t$.\n\nLet's solve the equation for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt + 32 &= 90 \\\\\nt &= 90 - 32 \\\\\nt &= 58\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212899 1069 "e-19083" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212899 1071 "$2v = 38$\nWhat value of $v$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212899 1072 #uuid "21dc849f-a06c-5f95-8282-eb988ba5e87b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212899 1074 "We are given the equation $2v = 38$ and are asked to solve for $v$.\n\nLet's solve the equation for $v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2v &= 38 \\\\\nv &= \\frac{38}{2} \\\\\nv &= 19\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $v$ that satisfies the equation is $19$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212900 1069 "e-19084" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212900 1071 "$\\dfrac{2v}{5} = 14$\nIn the equation above, what is the value of $v$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212900 1072 #uuid "64f0b30b-5498-5df7-b70d-cb1c0c8a0e2f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212900 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2v}{5} = 14\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $v$.\n\nLet's solve the equation for $v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2v}{5} &= 14 \\\\\n2v &= 14 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n2v &= 70 \\\\\nv &= \\frac{70}{2} \\\\\nv &= 35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $v$ that satisfies the equation is $35$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212901 1069 "e-19085" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212901 1071 "$6r - 18 = 180$\nWhat is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212901 1072 #uuid "2f38fbea-f229-5e19-b809-73549547021d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212901 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n6r - 18 = 180\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $r$.\n\nLet's solve the equation for $r{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6r - 18 &= 180 \\\\\n6r &= 180 + 18 \\\\\n6r &= 198 \\\\\nr &= \\frac{198}{6} \\\\\nr &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212902 1069 "e-19087" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212902 1071 "$b + 23 = 510$\nWhat value of $b$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212902 1072 #uuid "072950f2-3fb6-59ef-b81f-d19ad626e5d4" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212902 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nb + 23 = 510\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $b$.\n\nLet's solve the equation for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb + 23 &= 510 \\\\\nb &= 510 - 23 \\\\\nb &= 487\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution to the given equation is $487$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212903 1069 "e-19088" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212903 1071 "$6v - 2v + 18 = 66$\nWhat value of $v$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212903 1072 #uuid "ca1eb7d9-074e-5124-af35-b36146297406" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212903 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n6v - 2v + 18 = 66\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $v$.\n\nFirst, we simplify the left-hand side by combining like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6v - 2v + 18 &= 66 \\\\\n4v + 18 &= 66\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation for $v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4v + 18 &= 66 \\\\\n4v &= 48 \\\\\nv &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212904 1069 "e-19090" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212904 1071 "Calculate $2d - 7$ given that $4d + 20 = 0$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212904 1072 #uuid "6368fb4c-07d4-5c9b-a1ce-cfa902ba4684" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212904 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n4d + 20 = 0\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $2d - 7$.\n\nFirst, we solve the equation for $d{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4d + 20 &= 0 \\\\\n4d &= -20 \\\\\nd &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $d = -5$, we substitute this value into the expression $2d - 7$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2d - 7 &= 2(-5) - 7 \\\\\n&=-10 - 7 \\\\\n&=-17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212905 1069 "e-19091" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212905 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the equation $9z + 15 = 24$?\n\n1. $z + 5 = 6$\n2. $3z + 5 = 8$\n3. $z + 12 = 8$\n4. $3z + 12 = 16$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212905 1072 #uuid "7aea2f7e-552e-5bda-9372-dbf4eb70497c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212905 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n9z + 15 = 24\n$$\n\nand are asked to determine which equation is equivalent to it.\n\nThe greatest common factor of all terms is $3$, so we divide both sides of the equation by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{9z + 15}{3} &= \\frac{24}{3} \\\\\n3z + 5 &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n3z + 5 = 8\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the original equation.\n\nIt can be verified that none of the other answer choices are equivalent to\n\n$$\n9z + 15 = 24\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212906 1069 "e-19095" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212906 1071 "If $6y + \\dfrac{2}{3} = 4$, what is the value of $12y + 1$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212906 1072 #uuid "5534ff55-2e7f-5fb8-9574-6a54473d40a8" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212906 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n6y + \\dfrac{2}{3} = 4\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $12y + 1$.\n\nTo find $12y + 1$ from the given expression, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and solve for $12y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(6y + \\frac{2}{3}) &= 2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n12y + \\frac{4}{3} &= 8 \\\\\n12y &= 8 - \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n12y &= \\frac{20}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain $12y + 1$, we add $1$ to both sides and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12y &= \\frac{20}{3} \\\\\n12y + 1 &= \\frac{20}{3} + 1 \\\\\n12y + 1 &= \\frac{23}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $12y + 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{23}{3}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212907 1069 "e-19097" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212907 1071 "If $2x + 1 = 9$, then what is the value of $8x + 4$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212907 1072 #uuid "cd93d6b3-3647-5c13-a3a7-3233c2aa6a47" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212907 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 9\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $8x + 4$.\n\nSince we are already told that\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 9\n$$\n\nwe can multiply both sides of the equation by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 1 &= 9 \\\\\n4 \\cdot (2x) + 4 \\cdot 1 &= 4 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n8x + 4 &= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212908 1069 "e-19101" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212908 1071 "Which of the following has the same solutions as $5m - 35 = 90$?\n\n1. $5m=25$\n2. $5m=55$\n3. $5m=125$\n4. $5m=450$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212908 1072 #uuid "db46756f-d616-56f3-b7ef-bef993d133d9" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212908 1074 "Notice that the given answer choices are of the form\n$5m = k$\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nTo write our equation in this form, we apply the addition principle, adding $35$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5m - 35 &= 90 \\\\\n5m - 35 + 35 &= 90 + 35 \\\\\n5m + 0 &= 125 \\\\\n5m &= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $5m=125$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212909 1069 "e-19103" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212909 1071 "What value of $x$ is the solution to the equation\n$5(2x + 1) - 2 = 9 - 3(2x - 5) - 1$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212909 1072 #uuid "2fdc9716-cca3-518f-89a6-f8fa72ea1588" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212909 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of the binomial on the right-hand side is negative, so we need to be extra careful when expanding this binomial.\n\nWe begin by expanding both sides of the equation and simplifying by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(2x + 1) - 2 &= 9 - 3(2x - 5) - 1 \\\\\n10x + 5 - 2 &= 9 - 6x + 15 - 1 \\\\\n10x + 3 &= 23 - 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation for $x$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10x + 3 &= 23 - 6x \\\\\n10x + 3 - 3 &= 23 - 6x - 3 \\\\\n10x &= 20 - 6x \\\\\n10x + 6x &= 20 - 6x + 6x \\\\\n16x &= 20 \\\\\n\\frac{16x}{16} &= \\frac{20}{16} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212910 1069 "e-19104" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212910 1071 "For what value of $w$ does $w + 7 = 4(w - 2)$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212910 1072 #uuid "45d32ca0-3add-5121-9d27-3fde07b813cb" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212910 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nw + 7 = 4(w - 2)\n$$\n\nand are asked to determine the value of $w$ that satisfies the equation.\n\nFirst, we expand the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw + 7 &= 4(w - 2) \\\\\n&= 4w - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the variable $w$ by subtracting $w$ from both sides and then adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw + 7 &= 4w - 8 \\\\\nw + 7 - w &= 4w - 8 - w \\\\\n7 &= 3w - 8 \\\\\n15 &= 3w \\\\\n\\frac{15}{3} &= w \\\\\n5 &= w\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $w$ that satisfies the equation is $5$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212911 1069 "e-19105" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212911 1071 "What value of $y$ is the solution to the equation $9(y + 4) + 2(2y - 7) = 7y$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212911 1072 #uuid "253ab3d4-f529-58b5-9acc-bdb5357263b7" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212911 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n9(y + 4) + 2(2y - 7) = 7y\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $y$ that satisfies it.\n\nWe begin by distributing on the left-hand side and combining like terms\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9(y + 4) + 2(2y - 7) &= 7y \\\\\n9y + 36 + 4y - 14 &= 7y \\\\\n13y + 22 &= 7y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we combine like terms on both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n13y - 7y + 22 &= 7y - 7y \\\\\n6y + 22 &= 0 \\\\\n6y &= -22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $6$ to isolate $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{-22}{6} =-11 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212912 1069 "e-19106" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212912 1071 "If $3(2s + 2) - 1 = 2(5s + 1) + 3$, what is the value of $s + 9$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212912 1072 #uuid "582393d8-dafe-5ca2-a834-3db6f2d34841" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212912 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n3(2s + 2) - 1 = 2(5s + 1) + 3\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $s + 9$.\n\nFirst, we begin by expanding both sides of the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2s + 2) - 1 &= 2(5s + 1) + 3 \\\\\n6s + 6 - 1 &= 10s + 2 + 3 \\\\\n6s + 5 &= 10s + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation for $s{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6s - 10s + 5 &= 10s - 10s + 5 \\\\\n- 4s + 5 &= 5 \\\\\n- 4s &= 5 - 5 \\\\\n- 4s &= 0 \\\\\ns &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $s = 0$ into $s + 9$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 9 &= 0 + 9 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $s + 9$ is $9$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212913 1069 "e-19108" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212913 1071 "If $9 - 7(3b + 2) = 6 - 5(3b + 2)$, then find $3b+2$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212913 1072 #uuid "5d075985-c091-5e4f-869c-08b581dbe00b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212913 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n9 - 7(3b + 2) = 6 - 5(3b + 2)\n$$\n\nNotice that both sides contain the same binomial expression $(3b + 2)$. We solve by moving all terms involving $(3b + 2)$ to one side and constants to the other:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 - 7(3b + 2) &= 6 - 5(3b + 2) \\\\\n- 7(3b + 2) + 5(3b + 2) &= 6 - 9 \\\\\n(-7 + 5)(3b + 2) &= -3 \\\\\n(-2)(3b + 2) &= -3 \\\\\n3b + 2 &= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212914 1069 "e-19109" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212914 1071 "If $2(2z + 5) + 8(2z + 5) = 120$, what is the value of $2z + 5$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212914 1072 #uuid "b048da64-f9a2-5132-8340-c8806138240b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212914 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n2(2z + 5) + 8(2z + 5) = 120\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $2z + 5$.\n\nWe begin by combining like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(2z + 5) + 8(2z + 5) &= 120 \\\\\n(2 + 8)(2z + 5) &= 120 \\\\\n10(2z + 5) &= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we divide both sides by $10$ to isolate $2z + 5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2z + 5 &= \\frac{120}{10} \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $2z + 5$ is $12$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212915 1069 "e-19110" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212915 1071 "If $7(2q - 3) = 5(2q - 3) + 18$, what is the value of $2q - 3$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212915 1072 #uuid "8cad3217-cc60-5ebc-bfce-89157729270d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212915 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n7(2q - 3) = 5(2q - 3) + 18\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $2q - 3$.\n\nWe begin by combining like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7(2q - 3) &= 5(2q - 3) + 18 \\\\\n7(2q - 3) - 5(2q - 3) &= 18 \\\\\n(7 - 5)(2q - 3) &= 18 \\\\\n2(2q - 3) &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we divide both sides by $2$ to isolate $2q - 3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2q - 3 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $2q - 3$ is $9$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212916 1069 "e-19112" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212916 1071 "If $5(6b+2) - 8(6b+2) = 9$, then what is the value of $b$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212916 1072 #uuid "c244f47c-1642-56d9-9a1f-f67a89947916" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212916 1074 "Notice that $(6b+2)$ is a common factor in both terms on the left-hand side.\n\nWe begin by factoring out $(6b+2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(6b + 2) - 8(6b + 2) &= 9 \\\\\n(5 - 8)(6b + 2) &= 9 \\\\\n- 3(6b + 2) &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $-3$ to isolate $(6b+2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3(6b + 2) &= 9 \\\\\n(-3(6b + 2))/(-3) &= \\frac{9}{-3} \\\\\n6b + 2 &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the obtained linear equation for $b$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6b + 2 &= -3 \\\\\n6b + 2 - 2 &= -3 - 2 \\\\\n6b &= -5 \\\\\n\\frac{6b}{6} &= \\frac{-5}{6} \\\\\nb &= -\\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nb=-\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212917 1069 "e-19113" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212917 1071 "If $5 - 2(x - 1) = 25 - 6(x - 1)$, then what is the value of $x$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212917 1072 #uuid "995fd4ec-544a-5466-86d0-e21a24fc0340" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212917 1074 "Notice that $(x-1)$ is a common factor on both sides of the equation.\n\nWe must isolate $(x-1)$ on one side of the equation. We start by adding $6(x-1)$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 - 2(x - 1) &= 25 - 6(x - 1) \\\\\n5 - 2(x - 1) + 6(x - 1) &= 25 - 6(x - 1) + 6(x - 1) \\\\\n5 - 2(x - 1) + 6(x - 1) &= 25 - 6(x - 1) + 6(x - 1) \\\\\n5 - 2(x - 1) + 6(x - 1) &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $5$ from both sides, and factor $(x-1)$ on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 - 2(x - 1) + 6(x - 1) - 5 &= 25 - 5 \\\\\n- 2(x - 1) + 6(x - 1) &= 20 \\\\\n(-2 + 6)(x - 1) &= 20 \\\\\n4(x - 1) &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $4$ and isolate $(x - 1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x - 1) &= 20 \\\\\n(4(x - 1))/(4) &= \\frac{20}{4} \\\\\nx - 1 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the obtained linear equation for $x$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 1 &= 5 \\\\\nx - 1 + 1 &= 5 + 1 \\\\\nx &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=6$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212918 1069 "e-19114" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212918 1071 "$\\dfrac{5}{6}(9w - 1) - \\dfrac{1}{3}(9w - 1) = 9$\nWhat value of $w$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212918 1072 #uuid "16fe85c3-d01d-5f61-9ccd-0e9c1c22d72d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212918 1074 "We are given\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}(9w - 1) - \\dfrac{1}{3}(9w - 1) = 9\n$$\n\nNotice that both terms on the left-hand side involve the same binomial expression $(9w - 1)$. So we factor it out and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{1}{3})(9w - 1) &= 9 \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{2}{6})(9w - 1) &= 9 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(9w - 1) &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, multiply both sides by $2$ and solve for $w{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9w - 1 &= 18 \\\\\n9w &= 19 \\\\\nw &= \\frac{19}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{19}{9}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212919 1069 "e-19115" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212919 1071 "$0.6(3d + 1) - 0.2(3d + 1) = 4.0$\nWhat value of $3d+1$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212919 1072 #uuid "5a1405ca-1d67-5ec4-8686-7d015541f994" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212919 1074 "Notice that $(3d+1)$ is a common factor in both terms on the left-hand side.\n\nWe begin by factoring out $(3d+1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.6(3d + 1) - 0.2(3d + 1) &= 4.0 \\\\\n(0.6 - 0.2)(3d + 1) &= 4.0 \\\\\n0.4(3d + 1) &= 4.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides by $10$ to remove the decimals. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot 0.4(3d + 1) &= 10 \\cdot 4.0 \\\\\n4(3d + 1) &= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $4$ to isolate $(3d+1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(3d + 1) &= 40 \\\\\n(4(3d + 1))/(4) &= \\frac{40}{4} \\\\\n3d + 1 &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $3d+1$ is $10$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212920 1069 "e-19116" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212920 1071 "If $\\dfrac{8k + 1}{5} = \\dfrac{8k + 1}{9}$, then, find the value of $8k + 2$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212920 1072 #uuid "dfe256e2-93e1-5c91-9df2-b197fd24ca51" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212920 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8k + 1}{5} = \\dfrac{8k + 1}{9}\n$$\n\nThe numerators on both sides are the same, but the denominators are different. The only way this equation can be true is if the numerator is $0$.\n\nFirst, we set\n\n$$\n8k + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8k + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n8k &= -1 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute\n\n$$\nk = -\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\ninto the expression $8k + 2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8k + 2 &= 8 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}) + 2 \\\\\n&=-1 + 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212921 1069 "e-19117" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212921 1071 "$6(j - 2) - 3(j - 2) = 27$\nWhat value of $j$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212921 1072 #uuid "2b1e1745-a90b-55d1-8f77-39d6a1aa6b87" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212921 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n6(j - 2) - 3(j - 2) = 27\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $j$ that satisfies it.\n\nWe begin by simplifying the left-hand side of the equation. Notice that both terms involve the same binomial $(j - 2)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6(j - 2) - 3(j - 2) &= 27 \\\\\n(6 - 3)(j - 2) &= 27 \\\\\n3(j - 2) &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we divide both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(j - 2) &= 27 \\\\\nj - 2 &= \\frac{27}{3} \\\\\nj - 2 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nj &= 9 + 2 \\\\\nj &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212922 1069 "e-19119" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212922 1071 "What value of $x$ is the solution to the equation $\\dfrac{1}{3}x + \\dfrac{1}{2}(x + 6) = 7$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212922 1072 #uuid "81a7e6df-b3dc-5333-b37e-3862433f9022" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212922 1074 "First, we expand the expression on the left-hand side and simplify by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{1}{2}(x + 6) &= \\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{1}{2}x + 3 \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{2})x + 3 \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2}{6} + \\frac{3}{6})x + 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6}x + 3 &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides by $6$ to get rid of the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\cdot (\\frac{5}{6}x + 3) &= 6 \\cdot (7) \\\\\n5x + 18 &= 42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 18 &= 42 \\\\\n5x + 18 - 18 &= 42 - 18 \\\\\n5x &= 24 \\\\\n\\frac{5x}{5} &= \\frac{24}{5} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{24}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\n$$\nx=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{24}{5}}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212923 1069 "e-19120" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212923 1071 "$2(5b - 6) = 28$\nFind the solution of the above equation." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212923 1072 #uuid "3ef136cb-46af-5ca7-91ef-6c43c146c04f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212923 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n2(5b - 6) = 28\n$$\n\nand are asked to determine which equation has the same solution.\n\nTo simplify, we divide both sides of the equation by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5b - 6 &= \\frac{28}{2} \\\\\n5b - 6 + 6 &= 14 + 6 \\\\\n5b &= 20 \\\\\n\\frac{5b}{5} &= \\frac{20}{5} \\\\\nb &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212924 1069 "e-19121" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212924 1071 "$(w + 11) + 7 = 29$\nWhat value of $w$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212924 1072 #uuid "8b1a7e13-0b49-5a0b-9454-badb764eea77" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212924 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n(w + 11) + 7 = 29\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $w$.\n\nLet's simplify and solve the equation for $w{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(w + 11) + 7 &= 29 \\\\\nw + 18 &= 29 \\\\\nw &= 29 - 18 \\\\\nw &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution to the given equation is $w=11$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212925 1069 "e-19123" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212925 1071 "What value of $q$ is the solution to the equation $5q + 4(q + 3) = 84$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212925 1072 #uuid "0fd62b1c-50cb-50c6-8715-8e865e009d99" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212925 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n5q + 4(q + 3) = 84\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $q$ that satisfies it.\n\nFirst, we expand the expression on the left-hand side by distributing the $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5q + 4(q + 3) &= 5q + 4q + 12 \\\\\n&= 9q + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9q + 12 &= 84\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $12$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9q &= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq &= \\frac{72}{9} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $q$ that satisfies the equation is $8$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212926 1069 "e-19124" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212926 1071 "|  | Concerts | Festivals | Sports |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Friends | $18$ | $22$ | $20$ |\n| Families | $16$ | $19$ | $21$ |\n| Couples | $20$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nThe table above shows the attendance at different events by groups of people. If a couple is selected at random, what is the probability that they attended either concerts or sports?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212926 1072 #uuid "38fa3a60-3416-5a6e-b131-4dbcb3269926" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212926 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of couples who attended either concerts or sports:\n\n$$\n\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! P(\\text{Concerts or Sports} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Couples}) = \\dfrac{\\text{# Couples attending concerts or sports}}{\\text{# Couples}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of\n\n$$\n20 + 18 + 24 = {\\color{red}62}\n$$\n\ncouples were surveyed. Of these,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{20}} + {\\color{blue}{24}} = 44\n$$\n\nattended either concerts or sports.\n\n|  | Concerts | Festivals | Sports |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Friends | $18$ | $22$ | $20$ |\n| Families | $16$ | $19$ | $21$ |\n| Couples | $\\color{blue}{20}$ | $18$ | $\\color{blue}{24}$ |\n\nTherefore, the fraction of couples who attended either concerts or sports is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Concerts or Sports} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Couples}) = \\dfrac{44}{62} = \\dfrac{22}{31}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212927 1069 "e-19125" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212927 1071 "$0.85(2v + 3) - 0.35(2v + 3) = 2.5$\nWhat value of $v$ is the solution to the given equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212927 1072 #uuid "de0e41ec-7d5d-54b3-a6f8-3d5a8fb3561e" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212927 1074 "Notice that $(2v+3)$ is a common factor in both terms on the left-hand side.\n\nWe begin by factoring out $(2v+3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.85(2v + 3) - 0.35(2v + 3) &= 2.5 \\\\\n(0.85 - 0.35)(2v + 3) &= 2.5 \\\\\n0.50(2v + 3) &= 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides by $10$ to remove the decimals. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot 0.50(2v + 3) &= 10 \\cdot 2.5 \\\\\n5(2v + 3) &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, divide both sides by $5$ to isolate $(2v+3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2v + 3 &= \\frac{25}{5} \\\\\n2v + 3 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solve for $v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2v + 3 &= 5 \\\\\n2v &= 2 \\\\\nv &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $v=1$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212928 1069 "e-19126" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212928 1071 "A sugar cube has a mass of $216$ grams and each edge has a length of $6$ centimeters. What is the density, in grams per cubic centimeter, of the cube?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212928 1072 #uuid "64582e83-a255-54a7-b66a-7bb05a681f4d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212928 1074 "Density is defined as the ratio of mass to volume:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{m}{V}\n$$\n\nSince the object is a cube, its volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = s^3\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of an edge.\n\nIn this problem,\n\n- the mass is $m = 216$ grams, and\n- the edge length is $s = 6$ centimeters, so the volume is\n$V = (6)^3 = 216 \\ \\text{cm}^3$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\frac{216}{216} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the density is $1$ gram per cubic centimeter." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212929 1069 "e-19127" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212929 1071 "A concrete block has a density of $8$ pounds per cubic foot and a volume of $7$ cubic feet. What is the mass, in pounds, of the block?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212929 1072 #uuid "f6d78982-ffb5-5d78-8707-69d6042fa016" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212929 1074 "Density is defined as the ratio of mass to volume:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{m}{V}\n$$\n\nHere, we want to find the mass $m{:}$\n\n$$\nm = \\rho \\cdot V\n$$\n\nIn this problem,\n\n- the density is $\\rho = 8$ pounds per cubic foot, and\n- the volume is $V = 7$ cubic feet.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nm = 8 \\cdot 7 = 56\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass is $56$ pounds." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212930 1069 "e-19128" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212930 1071 "A large tank has a mass of $200$ pounds and a density of $25$ pounds per cubic foot. What is the volume, in cubic feet, of the tank?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212930 1072 #uuid "83d20122-f1d8-569f-ba7f-ae62e625b2d6" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212930 1074 "Density is defined as the ratio of mass to volume:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{m}{V}\n$$\n\nHere, we want to find the volume $V{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{m}{\\rho}\n$$\n\nIn this problem,\n\n- the mass is $m = 200$ pounds, and\n- the density is $\\rho = 25$ pounds per cubic foot.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{200}{25} = 8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is $8$ cubic feet." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212931 1069 "e-19130" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212931 1071 "Find the solution of the equation $3x - 6(x + 4) = -48$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212931 1072 #uuid "7e6d6413-e9eb-5060-bd59-94acc1ba8ee5" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212931 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of the binomial on the left-hand side is negative, so we need to be extra careful when expanding this binomial.\n\nFirst, we expand the expression on the left-hand side and simplify by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 6(x + 4) &= 3x - 6x - 24 \\\\\n&=-3x - 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - 24 &= -48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $x$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - 24 &= -48 \\\\\n- 3x - 24 + 24 &= -48 + 24 \\\\\n- 3x &= -24 \\\\\n\\frac{-3x}{-3} &= \\frac{-24}{-3} \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $x=8$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212932 1069 "e-19131" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212932 1071 "The table below shows the daily water usage (in liters) for five households.\n\n| Household | Water (L) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| A | $18$ |\n| B | $22$ |\n| C | $20$ |\n| D | $19$ |\n| E | $21$ |\n\nA sixth household uses $12$ liters, and this value is added to the original dataset of five households to create a new dataset. Which dataset has a greater mean?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212932 1072 #uuid "2d8f8955-1d02-5369-b21b-083381c5db02" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212932 1074 "When a new data point is added, the mean of the dataset moves *toward* that new data point:\n\n- If the new value is *less* than the current mean, the overall mean decreases.\n- If the new value is *greater* than the current mean, the overall mean increases.\n\nThe values in the original dataset range from $18$ to $22$. So, the mean of the original dataset lies somewhere in this range, around $20$ (calculating, the mean is actually $20$).\n\nThe new value of $12$ must be less than the original mean, as it lies below the range. As a result, the overall mean will *decrease* when this data point is included.\n\nTherefore, the mean of the original dataset is *greater* because the new value is less than the mean of the original dataset." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212933 1069 "e-19132" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212933 1071 "A battery test involved $18$ devices. The mean battery life measured per device was $9.5$ hours. After one device is removed from the dataset, the mean for the remaining devices drops to $9.2$ hours. How many hours of battery life did the removed device have?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212933 1072 #uuid "2b8c94c1-e8fe-5888-9c08-a3d9ebb59cb0" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212933 1074 "The sum of the values in a dataset is the product of the mean and the number of values.\n\nLet $S_{\\text{orig}}$ be the total hours for all $18$ devices, and let $S_{\\text{new}}$ be the total for the remaining $17$ devices. So, the sums are:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{orig} &= 18 \\cdot 9.5 = 171.0 \\\\\nS_{new} &= 17 \\cdot 9.2 = 156.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe removed device’s battery life is the difference between these totals:\n\n$$\nS_{\\text{orig}} - S_{\\text{new}} = 171.0 - 156.4 = 14.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the removed device had $14.6$ hours of battery life." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212934 1069 "e-19137" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212934 1071 "Calculate $8x$ given that $\\dfrac{5}{8}x - 1 = 9$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212934 1072 #uuid "ce1c87ab-bff9-504f-b487-2847d99fac8e" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212934 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8}x - 1 = 9\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $8x$.\n\nFirst, we solve the equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{8}x - 1 &= 9 \\\\\n\\frac{5}{8}x &= 9 + 1 \\\\\n\\frac{5}{8}x &= 10 \\\\\n5x &= 80 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{80}{5} \\\\\nx &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x = 16$ into $8x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x &= 8 \\cdot 16 \\\\\n&= 128\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212935 1069 "e-19138" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212935 1071 "A tutoring company charges a one-time registration fee of $\\[math]42$ and a monthly tuition fee of $\\[/math]15$. If there are no additional charges, after how many months will a student have paid a total of $\\[math]175$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212935 1072 #uuid "2605836e-ae61-5948-b728-c8d1730461b8" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212935 1074 "Let the number of months be $m$. The total cost includes a one-time fee of $\\[math]42$ and a monthly cost of $\\[/math]15$ for each of the $m$ months, so we write this as the following equation:\n\n$$\n175 = 15m + 42\n$$\n\nTo solve for $m$, we first subtract $42$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n175 - 42 &= 15m \\\\\n133 &= 15m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $15{:}$\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{133}{15} = 8.86\\ldots\n$$\n\nSince the cost after exactly $8$ months would not yet reach $\\[math]175$, we round up to the next whole number.\n\nSo it will take *$9$ months* to reach or exceed $\\[/math]175$ in charges." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212936 1069 "e-19140" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212936 1071 "Nine more than twelve times a number $a$ is equal to $81$. Find the equation representing this situation." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212936 1072 #uuid "7c282e18-41a7-5ab4-8dc5-4ddf70f05181" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212936 1074 "The phrase “twelve times a number $a$” translates to $12a$, and “nine more than” means we add $9$ to that expression. So the entire expression is $12a + 9$.\n\nSince this is equal to $81$, the full equation is\n\n$$\n12a + 9 = 81\n$$\n\nThis equation represents the situation described in the problem." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212937 1069 "e-19142" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212937 1071 "Jared bought a flash drive for $\\[math]6.75$ and several styluses that cost $\\[/math]3.50$ each. If he spent a total of $\\[math]17.25$, which equation can be used to find $s$, the number of styluses he purchased?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212937 1072 #uuid "bea4f395-c373-5457-ab39-5783cab4f0d0" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212937 1074 "Jared’s total cost includes $\\[math]6.75$ for the flash drive and $s$ styluses at $\\[/math]3.50$ each. So the total spent on styluses is $3.50s$, and the total purchase cost was $\\[math]17.25$.\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\n3.50s + 6.75 = 17.25\n$$\n\nThis equation models the total cost in terms of the number of styluses purchased." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212938 1069 "e-19143" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212938 1071 "Bella has $y$ pens. Ryan has $10$ more than six times the number of pens that Bella has. In terms of $y$, how many pens does Ryan have?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212938 1072 #uuid "bc50d1ac-8ede-5b6e-b1c1-9ac9c003d2ea" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212938 1074 "Six times the number of pens that Bella has is $6y$. Since Ryan has $10$ more than that, we add $10{:}$\n$6y + 10$\n\nSo, Ryan has $6y + 10$ pens." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212939 1069 "e-19144" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212939 1071 "Tara has $\\[math]66.00$ at a gaming arcade. She buys $6$ accessories that each cost $\\[/math]3.00$. She then uses the rest of her money to buy video game tokens that cost $\\[math]3.00$ each. How many tokens can Tara buy?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212939 1072 #uuid "8f461d91-038c-50d4-83f4-d2c29fa3bef5" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212939 1074 "First, we calculate how much Tara spent on accessories:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 3.00 = 18.00\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract that amount from the total to find the remaining money:\n\n$$\n66.00 - 18.00 = 48.00\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide the remaining amount by the cost per token:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48.00}{3.00} = 16\n$$\n\nSo, Tara can buy *$16$ tokens* with the rest of her money." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212940 1069 "e-19145" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212940 1071 "A city park begins a renovation project with $6,900$ gallons of paint. If about $84$ gallons are used per day, based on this rate, after how many days will there be $1,236$ gallons left?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212940 1072 #uuid "ea23a471-2382-54c5-a05a-78b589f52da7" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212940 1074 "First, we subtract the target number of gallons from the starting amount to find how much paint is used:\n\n$$\n6,900 - 1,236 = 5,664\n$$\n\nNext, we divide that amount by $84$ to find how many days it takes:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5,664}{84} = 67.42\\ldots\n$$\n\nSince the number of days must be a whole number and the supply doesn’t reach the target until the full day is completed, we round up:\n$68$\n\nSo, it will take *$68$ days* for the paint supply to reach $1,236$ gallons." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212941 1069 "e-19152" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212941 1071 "A painting crew is assigned to complete $7$ walls in a gallery. Each wall will be painted using either latex paint or oil paint, but not both. A latex-painted wall requires $2$ gallons of paint, and an oil-painted wall requires $5$ gallons. If $l$ walls will use latex paint and the remaining walls will use oil paint, determine the expression representing the total number of gallons of paint needed." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212941 1072 #uuid "754367ba-ac19-58f4-9444-ff16041a9b68" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212941 1074 "Let $l$ be the number of walls that will use latex paint.\n\nThere are $7$ total walls. If $l$ walls use latex paint, then the remaining $7 - l$ walls use oil paint.\n\nEach latex-painted wall requires $2$ gallons of paint, so all latex-painted walls require\n$2l \\text{gallons.}$\n\nEach oil-painted wall requires $5$ gallons of paint, so all oil-painted walls require\n$5(7 - l) \\text{gallons.}$\n\nTherefore, the total number of gallons of paint needed is\n$2l + 5(7 - l)$.\n\nThis expression represents the total number of gallons of paint needed." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212942 1069 "e-19153" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212942 1071 "A designer toy company spends $\\[math]38$ to manufacture a collectible figure. A reseller earns a commission of $22\\%$ of the sale price of each figure. The company’s profit on each figure is the sale price minus both the manufacturing cost and the commission. Given that the sale price is $\\[/math]75$, find the equation representing the number of figures, $n$, the company must sell to earn a profit of $\\[math]10{,}494$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212942 1072 #uuid "9b9184f6-d485-56f3-a596-64657ccc7f93" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212942 1074 "Let $n$ be the number of figures sold.\n\nWe are told the production cost per unit is $\\[math]38$, and the reseller earns $22\\%$ of the sales price. The sales price is $\\[/math]75$, so the commission per unit is\n$0.22 \\times 75$.\n\nTo find the profit per unit, we subtract both the manufacturing cost and the commission from the sales price:\n$75 - 38 - (0.22 \\times 75)$\n\nThis simplifies to\n$(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)$.\n\nTo find the total profit, we multiply the profit per unit (given above) by the number of units $n{:}$\n$(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)n$\n\nSince the company wishes to earn a profit of $\\[math]10{,}494$, this gives the equation\n\n$$\n(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)n = 10{,}494\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is *$(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)n = 10{,}494$*." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212943 1069 "e-19155" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212943 1071 "A jewelry box contains $120$ pieces that are either rings, necklaces, or bracelets. The number of rings is $2$ times the number $n$ of necklaces. There are $40$ bracelets. Determine the equation that can be used to find the number of necklaces, $n$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212943 1072 #uuid "39f74de6-a874-58e3-a788-f82d074022e7" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212943 1074 "Let $n$ represent the number of necklaces.\n\nThe number of rings is $2n$, and there are $40$ bracelets.\n\nTo find the total number of pieces, we add together the necklaces, rings, and bracelets:\n\n$$\nn + 2n + 40 = 120\n$$\n\nThis equation represents the relationship between the number of each type of jewelry and the total." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212944 1069 "e-19163" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212944 1071 "The equation $x + y = 2,400$ represents the number of calories from protein, $x$, and the number of calories from other sources, $y$, in a daily diet totaling $2,400$ calories. If the diet includes $680$ calories from protein, how many calories are from other sources?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212944 1072 #uuid "e27f9078-e63c-5548-a21f-bca0899a9768" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212944 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nx + y = 2,400\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ represents calories from protein and $y$ represents calories from other sources.\n\nWe are told that the diet includes $x = 680$ calories from protein. So, we substitute this value into the equation:\n\n$$\n680 + y = 2,400\n$$\n\nTo isolate $y$, we subtract $680$ from both sides:\n$y = 1,720$\n\nTherefore, the number of calories from other sources is $1,720$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212945 1069 "e-19164" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212945 1071 "The equation $d = 0.09t$ models the relationship between the distance $d$, in kilometers, that a person travels from the stable on horseback, and the time $t$, in minutes, spent riding. How far did the person travel from the stable if the ride lasted $50$ minutes?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212945 1072 #uuid "340aa861-77a9-5ace-923f-04c490c591fc" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212945 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nd = 0.09t\n$$\n\nwhich shows that the distance $d$ in kilometers is equal to $0.09$ times the number of minutes $t$.\n\nWe are told that $t = 50$, so we substitute:\n\n$$\nd = 0.09(50)\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify:\n\n$$\nd = 4.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the person traveled $4.5$ kilometers from the stable." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212946 1069 "e-19165" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212946 1071 "The number $p$ is smaller than $q$ by $14$. Find an equation representing the relationship between $p$ and $q$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212946 1072 #uuid "6634931b-aca0-59f9-a391-4d4fa3523f0b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212946 1074 "The problem tells us that $p$ is smaller than $q$ by $14$. This means we take $q$ and subtract $14$ to get $p$.\n\nTherefore, the equation that represents this relationship is\n\n$$\np = q - 14\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212947 1069 "e-19170" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212947 1071 "A catering service calculates the total cost $C$ (in dollars) of preparing $t$ trays of food and using $h$ hours of staff labor using the following equation:\n$7t + 8h = 118$\nIf the service used $6$ hours of staff labor for an event, how many trays of food did it prepare?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212947 1072 #uuid "50b99947-1b63-5409-b77b-8b7e4e1c0815" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212947 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n7t + 8h = 118\n$$\n\nand told that $h = 6$.\n\nFirst, we substitute $6$ for $h$ in the equation:\n\n$$\n7t + 8(6) = 118\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n7t + 48 = 118\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $48$ from both sides:\n$7t = 70$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $7{:}$\n\n$$\nt = \\frac{70}{7} = 10\n$$\n\nTherefore, the service prepared $10$ trays of food." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212948 1069 "e-19173" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212948 1071 "The width of a rectangular patio is $a$ feet. The length is $8$ feet less than the width. Determine the expression representing the perimeter, in feet, of the patio in terms of $a$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212948 1072 #uuid "52b7058b-61b8-59b6-a900-91571dae9a14" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212948 1074 "We are told that the width is $a$ feet, and the length is $8$ feet less than that, so the length is $a - 8$.\n\nThe formula for the perimeter of a rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\text{Perimeter} = 2(\\text{length}) + 2(\\text{width})\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions gives\n$2(a - 8) + 2(a)$.\n\nNow we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(a - 8) + 2a &= 2a - 16 + 2a \\\\\n&= 4a - 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the perimeter is *$4a - 16$ feet*." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212949 1069 "e-19174" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212949 1071 "A manufacturer produces $7$ octagonal frames, each with side length $x$, and $3$ metal trays, each with a length of $4y$ and a width of $y$. None of these objects share any sides. The total perimeter of all the frames and trays is $476$. Determine the equation representing this situation." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212949 1072 #uuid "386c80d6-859f-517b-929e-446954d24185" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212949 1074 "To model this situation, we will add together the perimeters of all the shapes.\n\n- Each *regular octagon* has $8$ sides of length $x$, so its perimeter is $8x$.\nWith $7$ octagons, the total perimeter from the frames is\n$7 \\cdot 8x = 56x$.\n- Each *rectangle* has perimeter $2(\\text{length} + \\text{width}) = 2(4y + y) = 10y$.\nWith $3$ trays, the total perimeter from the rectangles is\n$3 \\cdot 10y = 30y$.\n\nAdding both contributions together, we get the total perimeter:\n\n$$\n56x + 30y = 476\n$$\n\nThus, the correct equation is\n\n$$\n56x + 30y = 476\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212950 1069 "e-19176" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212950 1071 "Two walkways in a park are lined with decorative stones. Walkway $M$ has $m$ stones and Walkway $N$ has $n$ stones. The total length of both walkways is $1{,}140$ inches. The equation $8m + 15n = 1{,}140$ represents this situation. What is the interpretation of $15$ in this context?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212950 1072 #uuid "115df962-a8df-59b6-84e7-a658b35370c5" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212950 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n8m + 15n = 1{,}140\n$$\n\nmodels the total length of the walkways. The terms $8m$ and $15n$ represent the length of Walkways $M$ and $N$, respectively.\n\nThe factor of $15$ in $15n$ shows that each stone in Walkway $N$ is $15$ inches long." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212951 1069 "e-19178" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212951 1071 "$6a + 3b = 45$\n\nNoah bought $6$ apples that cost $a$ dollars each and $3$ bananas that cost $b$ dollars each. If the given equation represents this situation, what is the interpretation of $45$ in this context?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212951 1072 #uuid "947b752c-4d5c-52b1-9c56-bed1f7cbee49" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212951 1074 "In the equation\n\n$$\n6a + 3b = 45\n$$\n\nthe term $6a$ represents the total cost of the apples, and the term $3b$ represents the total cost of the bananas. Adding them gives the total amount Noah spent.\n\nTherefore, the number $45$ represents the total cost, in dollars, for all apples and bananas purchased." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212952 1069 "e-19180" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212952 1071 "The equation $2.85m + 4.15n = 44.85$ represents the number of muffins, $m$, and sandwiches, $n$, a café sells to total exactly $\\[math]44.85$. The price of each muffin is the same and the price of each sandwich is the same. What is the price of one muffin?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212952 1072 #uuid "d32fdd86-c2c4-5e32-9ff0-3498a3242b59" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212952 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n2.85m + 4.15n = 44.85\n$$\n\nmodels the total cost of buying muffins and sandwiches.\n\nThe term $2.85m$ shows the total cost of the muffins, so the coefficient $2.85$ represents the price, in dollars, of one muffin.\n\nTherefore, one muffin costs $\\[math]\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{2.85}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212953 1069 "e-19183" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212953 1071 "During a science fair, students earned points for completing experiments and presenting findings. One student completed $x$ experiments and gave $y$ presentations, earning a total of $360$ points. The equation $12x + 24y = 360$ represents this situation. How many fewer points is an experiment worth than a presentation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212953 1072 #uuid "eba8095a-5836-524a-bd01-09419625a926" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212953 1074 "In the equation\n\n$$\n12x + 24y = 360\n$$\n\n, the coefficient $12$ means each experiment is worth $12$ points, and the coefficient $24$ means each presentation is worth $24$ points.\n\nTo find how many fewer points an experiment is worth than a presentation, subtract $12$ from $24{:}$\n\n$$\n24 - 12 = 12\n$$\n\nTherefore, each experiment is worth $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{12}$ fewer points than each presentation." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212954 1069 "e-19184" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212954 1071 "Two teams at a bakery decorated cakes for a large event. Team 1 decorated $5$ cakes per day, and Team 2 decorated $8$ cakes per day. Over the course of several days, the total number of cakes decorated was $2{,}900$. The equation $5x + 8y = 2{,}900$ represents this situation. What is the interpretation of $y$ in this context?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212954 1072 #uuid "6a5b2587-a253-5dd9-aba0-65195619317d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212954 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n5x + 8y = 2{,}900\n$$\n\nmodels the total number of cakes decorated. The terms $5x$ and $8y$ represent the number of cakes decorated by Team 1 and Team 2, respectively.\n\nSince the factor of $8$ in $8y$ corresponds to Team 2’s rate, $y$ represents the number of days Team 2 worked." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212955 1069 "e-19186" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212955 1071 "$T = 8.60n + 3.90s$\nIn the equation above, $T$ represents the total amount of money, in dollars, a pet grooming service charges for $n$ nail trims and $s$ shampoo treatments. The price, in dollars, of each nail trim is the same, and the price, in dollars, of each shampoo treatment is the same. What is the interpretation of the number $3.90$ in this context?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212955 1072 #uuid "9c165133-9430-58d0-ab6c-64d4de2dc11d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212955 1074 "In the equation\n\n$$\nT = 8.60n + 3.90s\n$$\n\n, the term $3.90s$ represents the total cost of the shampoo treatments.\n\nTherefore, the coefficient $3.90$ represents the price, in dollars, of one shampoo treatment." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212956 1069 "e-19189" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212956 1071 "Lena delivered $60$ liters of milk. She used $x$ small jugs and $y$ large jugs. The equation $4x + 6y = 60$ represents this situation. Which is the interpretation of $6y$ in this context?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212956 1072 #uuid "39ae7c30-1576-5b8f-a7c8-4f2ff14c64c6" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212956 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n4x + 6y = 60\n$$\n\nmodels the total amount of milk Lena delivered, in liters.\n\nIn this equation, $x$ is the number of small jugs and $y$ is the number of large jugs. Correspondingly, $4$ and $6$ are the capacities, in liters, of the small and large jugs, respectively.\n\nConsequently, the term $6y$ represents the total number of liters of milk in the large jugs." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212957 1069 "e-19191" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212957 1071 "In quadrilateral $EFGH$, sides $EF$ and $FG$ each have a length of $k$ meters, and side $GH$ has a length of $\\ell$ meters. The equation $2k + \\ell = 96$ represents this situation. What is the interpretation of $96$ in this context?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212957 1072 #uuid "fff20d50-3089-5570-9368-9005a966e870" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212957 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n2k + \\ell = 96\n$$\n\nmodels the total length of the three sides described. The terms $2k$ and $\\ell$ represent the total combined length of sides $EF$, $FG$ and the length of side $GH$, respectively.\n\nTherefore, the constant $96$ represents the sum of the lengths, in meters, of sides $EF$, $FG$, and $GH$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212958 1069 "e-19192" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212958 1071 "The linear function $h$ is defined by\n$h(x) = k - 3x$,\nwhere $k$ is a constant. If $h(m + 2) = \\dfrac{m}{2}$, where $m$ is a constant, write an expressions that represents the value of $k$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212958 1072 #uuid "04378bce-0fc7-5990-bd1e-34bf7c5ac30b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212958 1074 "We must find an expression for $k$ in terms of $m$.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nh(m + 2) = \\dfrac{m}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\nx = m+2\n$$\n\ninto the function definition and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(x) &= k - 3x \\\\\nh(m + 2) &= k - 3(m + 2) \\\\\n\\frac{m}{2} &= k - 3m - 6 \\\\\nk &= \\frac{m}{2} + 3m + 6 \\\\\nk &= \\frac{m + 6m}{2} + 6 \\\\\nk &= \\frac{7m}{2} + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212959 1069 "e-19193" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212959 1071 "For the function $f$, if $f(4x) = x - 2$ for all values of $x$, what is the value of $f(8)$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212959 1072 #uuid "9ff5dc37-b50f-578d-bacb-d8a32c4d244e" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212959 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n8 = 4 \\cdot 2\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x = 2$ into the function definition and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(4x) &= x - 2 \\\\\nf(4 \\cdot 2) &= 2 - 2 \\\\\nf(8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212960 1069 "e-19198" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212960 1071 "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 6x - 5$. For what value of $x$ does $g(x) = 49$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212960 1072 #uuid "96653a17-6834-5cd9-a752-5de62dd7d029" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212960 1074 "We want to find the value of $x$ such that $g(x) = 49$.\n\nSubstituting the expression for the function $g$ into the equation above and solving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= 49 \\\\\n6x - 5 &= 49 \\\\\n6x &= 54 \\\\\nx &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212961 1069 "e-19199" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212961 1071 "The function $p$ is defined by $p(x) = 6x$. For what value of $x$ does $p(x) = 42$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212961 1072 #uuid "f57f8980-8dc6-536a-bfe1-792e8fcf8fa9" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212961 1074 "We want to find the value of $x$ such that $p(x) = 42$.\n\nSubstituting the expression for the function $p$ into the equation above and solving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(x) &= 42 \\\\\n6x &= 42 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{42}{6} \\\\\nx &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212962 1069 "e-19216" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212962 1071 "The function $f$ is defined by\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{x + 2}{2}$,\nand $f(b) = 6$, where $b$ is a constant. What is the value of $b$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212962 1072 #uuid "de351940-68c9-5e44-a4a9-4407329927d7" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212962 1074 "Substituting $x=b$ into the expression for $f(x)$ and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf(b) = 6\n$$\n\nwe can solve for $b$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x + 2}{2} \\\\\nf(b) &= \\frac{b + 2}{2} \\\\\n6 &= \\frac{b + 2}{2} \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2 &= b + 2 \\\\\n12 &= b + 2 \\\\\n12 - 2 &= b \\\\\nb &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212963 1069 "e-19219" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212963 1071 "The function $g$ is defined by\n$g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{3}x + \\dfrac{7}{3}$.\nFor what value of $x$ does $g(x) = 5$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212963 1072 #uuid "84e56f18-c123-514f-9c2a-04e3cddfcb96" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212963 1074 "We want to find the value of $x$ such that $g(x) = 5$.\n\nSubstituting the expression for the function $g$ into the equation above and solving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= 5 \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{7}{3} &= 5 \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}x &= 5 - \\frac{7}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}x &= \\frac{15}{3} - \\frac{7}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}x &= \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{8}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{8 \\cdot 3}{3 \\cdot 2} \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212964 1069 "e-19223" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212964 1071 "The function $m(x) = 2x + 1{,}200$ gives the perimeter, in millimeters ($\\text{mm}$), of an isosceles triangle with two equal sides of length $x$ mm and a fixed base. What is the length of the base of the triangle?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212964 1072 #uuid "b34e4c46-6b93-520c-9121-a5733409636a" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212964 1074 "For an isosceles triangle with two equal sides of length $x\\text{mm}$ and a base of length $b\\text{mm}$, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\nP = x + x + b = 2x + b\n$$\n\nFrom the problem, we know the perimeter function is\n\n$$\nm(x) = 2x + 1{,}200\n$$\n\nBy comparing, we get\n\n$$\n2x + b = 2x + 1{,}200\n$$\n\nTherefore, after subtracting $2x$ from both sides, the length of the base of the triangle is\n\n$$\nb = 2x + 1{,}200 - 2x = 1{,}200 \\text{mm}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212965 1069 "e-19233" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212965 1071 "One liter of varnish will cover $150$ square feet of a surface. A wooden patio has a total area of $P$ square feet. Find an equation representing the total amount of varnish $S$, in liters, needed to varnish the patio three times." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212965 1072 #uuid "d5c04f16-958c-5130-b6eb-e11acdd4159f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212965 1074 "Since one liter covers $150$ square feet, varnishing a patio of area $P$ square feet once would require\n\n$$\n\\frac{P}{150} \\,\\, \\text{liters.}\n$$\n\nTo varnish the patio three times, we simply triple this amount, so we get\n\n$$\n3 \\times \\frac{P}{150} = \\frac{P}{50}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that represents the total amount of varnish $S$, in liters, needed to varnish the patio of area $P$ three times, is\n\n$$\nS = \\frac{P}{50}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212966 1069 "e-19250" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212966 1071 "A submarine is initially $50$ feet below the surface. It begins descending at a constant rate of $7$ feet per second. What equation gives the depth $D$, in feet, of the submarine below the surface $t$ seconds after it starts descending?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212966 1072 #uuid "a395127a-a498-5edb-9e93-ed32318f8129" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212966 1074 "The depth of the submarine below the surface is made up of two parts.\n\n- At the beginning, the submarine is already $50$ feet below the surface.\n- As it descends, it gains an additional $7$ feet for every second. This means for $t$ seconds, it increases its depth by\n\n$$\n7 \\times t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that models the depth $D$, in feet, of the submarine below the surface $t$ seconds after it starts descending is\n\n$$\nD = 7t + 50\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212967 1069 "e-19258" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212967 1071 "An electric scooter’s battery is charged at $60\\%$. For every hour of riding, the battery charge decreases by $1.2\\%$. What equation can be used to find the battery charge $C$, in percent, after $h$ hours of use?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212967 1072 #uuid "882ac09f-e1f3-5683-a62a-dd2da33a6336" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212967 1074 "The battery starts at $60\\%$ charge.\n\nFor each hour of riding, it loses $1.2\\%$. So after $h$ hours, the total charge decrease is $1.2h$.\n\nTherefore, after subtracting this from the original charge, the equation that models the battery charge after $h$ hours is\n\n$$\nC = 60 - 1.2h\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212968 1069 "e-19262" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212968 1071 "During her marathon training, Mia runs $\\dfrac{11}{20}$ of the $200$ kilometers in her plan each week. If Mia keeps up this training pace, how many kilometers will she run in $3$ weeks?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212968 1072 #uuid "2a1e673f-f6d4-56ce-a6b8-fc77c06e633b" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212968 1074 "First, we find how many kilometers Mia runs each week by taking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{20}\n$$\n\nof $200$ kilometers. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{20} \\times 200 = 110\n$$\n\nSo each week, Mia runs $110$ kilometers.\n\nWe can now find the total distance run in $3$ weeks to be\n\n$$\n110 \\times 3 = 330\n$$\n\nConsequently, Mia will run $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{330}$ kilometers in $3$ weeks." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212969 1069 "e-19263" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212969 1071 "Factor the following expression:\n$\\dfrac{1}{2}(5y-3) - \\dfrac{1}{5}(5y-3)$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212969 1072 #uuid "c74a39cf-7bb8-57aa-a1a5-653e761a2acf" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212969 1074 "We are asked to factor the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}(5y-3) - \\dfrac{1}{5}(5y-3)\n$$\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(5y-3)$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{5})(5y - 3)\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract the numbers inside the first brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{5})(5y - 3) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{10} - \\frac{2}{10})(5y - 3) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5 - 2}{10}(5y - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10}(5y - 3) \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212970 1069 "e-19656" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212970 1071 "The following equation relates the distinct positive numbers $m$, $n$, and $p{:}$\n$m = \\dfrac{n}{\\,4+5p\\,}$\nExpress $n$ in terms of $m$ and $p$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212970 1072 #uuid "df650044-8495-5518-b6e1-0cc936a868eb" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212970 1074 "To eliminate the denominator, we multiply *both sides* of the equation by $(4+5p)$ to remove the fractions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{n}{4 + 5p} \\\\\nm \\cdot (4 + 5p) &= \\frac{n}{4 + 5p} \\cdot (4 + 5p) \\\\\nm \\cdot (4 + 5p) &= \\frac{n}{4 + 5p} \\cdot (4 + 5p) \\\\\nm(4 + 5p) &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nn = m(4+5p)\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212971 1069 "e-19657" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212971 1071 "The variables $x$ and $y$, such that $x \\neq 11$ and $y \\neq 8$, are related by the equation\n$x-11 = \\dfrac{55}{y-8}$.\nExpress $y$ in terms of $x$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212971 1072 #uuid "3eb3fa33-81b1-5ac2-8c72-9d75c6742f04" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212971 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the given equation by $(y-8)$ to remove the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 11 &= \\frac{55}{y - 8} \\\\\n(x - 11) \\cdot (y - 8) &= \\frac{55}{y - 8} \\cdot (y - 8) \\\\\n(x - 11) \\cdot (y - 8) &= \\frac{55}{y - 8} \\cdot (y - 8) \\\\\n(x - 11)(y - 8) &= 55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $(x-11)$ to isolate $(y-8){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 11)(y - 8))/(x - 11) &= \\frac{55}{x - 11} \\\\\ny - 8 &= \\frac{55}{x - 11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add $8$ to both sides and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 8 + 8 &= \\frac{55}{x - 11} + 8 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{55}{x - 11} + (8(x - 11))/(x - 11) \\\\\ny &= (55 + 8(x - 11))/(x - 11) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{55 + 8x - 88}{x - 11} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{8x - 33}{x - 11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{8x-33}{x-11}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212972 1069 "e-19658" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212972 1071 "The following equation relates the positive numbers $r$, $s$, and $t{:}$\n$\\dfrac{1}{6r} = \\dfrac{7s}{t}$\nExpress $s$ in terms of $t$ and $r$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212972 1072 #uuid "ae032a40-7578-5773-ba48-71fb61dc195d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212972 1074 "To eliminate the denominators, we use *cross multiplication*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{6r} &= \\frac{7s}{t} \\\\\n1 \\cdot t &= 7s \\cdot 6r \\\\\nt &= 42rs\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $42r$ to isolate $s{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{t}{42r} &= \\frac{42rs}{42r} \\\\\n\\frac{t}{42r} &= s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ns = \\dfrac{t}{42r}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212973 1069 "e-19659" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212973 1071 "The variables $r$ and $s$ are related by the equation\n$r = \\dfrac{3s + 16}{s}$.\nExpress $s$ in terms of $r$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212973 1072 #uuid "4370e17b-b5dc-5424-87c9-1eefd2dbf53d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212973 1074 "To get rid of fractions, we multiply both sides of the equation by $s{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\frac{3s + 16}{s} \\\\\nr \\cdot s &= \\frac{3s + 16}{s} \\cdot s \\\\\nr \\cdot s &= \\frac{3s + 16}{s} \\cdot s \\\\\nrs &= 3s + 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move all terms with $s$ to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrs &= 3s + 16 \\\\\nrs - 3s &= 3s + 16 - 3s \\\\\nrs - 3s &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $s$ out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(r - 3) &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $s{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(r - 3) &= 16 \\\\\n(s(r - 3))/(r - 3) &= \\frac{16}{r - 3} \\\\\n(s(r - 3))/(r - 3) &= \\frac{16}{r - 3} \\\\\ns &= \\frac{16}{r - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ns = \\dfrac{16}{r - 3}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212974 1069 "e-19666" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212974 1071 "Write an equivalent expression for the following\n$(\\sin 22^\\circ)(\\cos 68^\\circ) + (\\cos 22^\\circ)(\\sin 68^\\circ)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212974 1072 #uuid "4625562c-7c38-5091-bb5f-2e90d038975d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212974 1074 "First, notice that all the answer choices only involve trigonometric functions of $22^\\circ$. So, we need to rewrite the original expression entirely in terms of $22^\\circ$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n68^\\circ = 90^\\circ - 22^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe can do this with the cofunction identities\n\n$$\n\\sin(90^\\circ - \\theta) = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad \\cos(90^\\circ - \\theta) = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nReplacing every instance of $68^\\circ$ with $90^\\circ - 22^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n(\\sin 22^\\circ)(\\cos (90^\\circ - 22^\\circ)) + (\\cos 22^\\circ)(\\sin (90^\\circ - 22^\\circ))\n$$\n\nApplying the cofunction identities, the expression becomes\n\n$(\\sin 22^\\circ)(\\sin 22^\\circ) + (\\cos 22^\\circ)(\\cos 22^\\circ)$\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n(\\sin 22^\\circ)^2 + (\\cos 22^\\circ)^2\n$$\n\nwhich further simplifies to $1$ by the Pythagorean identity." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212975 1069 "e-19742" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212975 1071 "The table below shows the distribution of **running lap times (in minutes)** recorded at a track meet. Estimate the mean lap time.\n\n| Lap Time $(x)$ | Number of Runners $(f)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $8-10$ | $4$ |\n| $10-12$ | $5$ |\n| $12-14$ | $6$ |\n| $14-16$ | $3$ |\n| $16-18$ | $2$ |" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212975 1072 #uuid "72e9195b-8255-5f1e-94d5-7ccdcff205a7" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212975 1074 "To estimate the mean from grouped data, we first use the frequency table.\n\nSince the data is grouped, we record $x_{\\textrm{mid}}$, the middle value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{mid}}$ for each row.\n\n| Lap Time $(x)$ | Number of Runners $(f)$ | $x_{\\textrm{mid}}$ | $x_{\\textrm{mid}}\\cdot f$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8-10$ | $4$ | $9$ | $4\\cdot 9 = 36$ |\n| $10-12$ | $5$ | $11$ | $5\\cdot 11 = 55$ |\n| $12-14$ | $6$ | $13$ | $6\\cdot 13 = 78$ |\n| $14-16$ | $3$ | $15$ | $3\\cdot 15 = 45$ |\n| $16-18$ | $2$ | $17$ | $2\\cdot 17 = 34$ |\n\nTherefore, our estimate of the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{36 + 55 + 78 + 45 + 34}{4 + 5 + 6 + 3 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{248}{20} \\\\\n&= 12.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean lap time is approximately $12.4$ minutes." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212976 1069 "e-19743" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212976 1071 "| Group | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $24-27$ | $4$ |\n| $27-30$ | $5$ |\n| $30-33$ | $4$ |\n| $33-36$ | $7$ |\n| $36-39$ | $6$ |\n\nThe frequency table above shows how many values from a dataset fall into each group. Which of the following values could be the median of the dataset?\n\n1. $25$\n2. $31$\n3. $34$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212976 1072 #uuid "1f7d5a80-69d8-5481-b45f-b60884b2507d" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212976 1074 "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add a cumulative frequency column and fill in the values.\n\n| Group | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $24-27$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $27-30$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n| $30-33$ | $4$ | $13$ |\n| $33-36$ | $7$ | $20$ |\n| $36-39$ | $6$ | $26$ |\n\nThere are $26$ total values in the dataset. Since this is an even number, the median is the average of the values in the following positions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{26}{2} = 13\\textrm{th} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad 13 + 1 = 14\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $13$th and $14$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $13$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $20$ positions in total.\n\nSo, we have that\n\n- the $13$th value falls in the third row corresponding to the group $30-33$, while\n- the $14$th value falls in the fourth row corresponding to the group $33-36$.\n\nThe median could fall in either of these groups. Of the given options:\n\n- Option I, $25$, doesn't fall within either group $30-33$ or $33-36$, so cannot be the median.\n- Option II, $31$, falls within the group $30-33$, so could be the median.\n- Option III, $34$, falls within the group $33-36$, so could be the median.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212977 1069 "e-19755" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212977 1071 "A hospital studied a random sample of patients and found that the average waiting time in the emergency department was $47$ minutes, with a margin of error of $6$ minutes. The study was then repeated with a much larger sample size, and the new sample’s mean and margin of error were calculated in the same way as in the original study.\n\nWhich of the following statements is most likely true?\n\n1. The margin of error from the new study is smaller than the margin of error from the original study.\n2. The mean from the new study is larger than the mean from the original study.\n3. The mean from the new study is $47$ minutes." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212977 1072 #uuid "b15b5c8e-1e95-568a-aa87-24277283ae8c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212977 1074 "More information is gathered in larger samples, which reduces the amount of random fluctuation in the sample estimate. As a result, the estimate is more stable and precise, with a smaller margin of error.\n\nIn our case, the new study has a much *larger sample size* than the original study. So, the margin of error from the new study is *smaller* than that from the original study. Therefore, statement I is likely true.\n\nOn the other hand, the new sample mean could be higher, lower, or essentially the same as the original mean; the direction is not determined by sample size alone. Therefore, statements II and III might not be true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212978 1069 "e-19807" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212978 1071 "The acceleration of a train is measured as $5.1$ meters per second squared. What is this rate in miles per minute squared, rounding to the nearest tenth?\n\n*Hint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} \\approx 1,609\\,\\textrm{m}$*" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212978 1072 #uuid "97a91ee6-d302-5452-bd82-b9ee7a08df30" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212978 1074 "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe'll need two conversion factors: one to convert the distance unit ($\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$), and one to convert the squared time unit ($\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $5.1\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- There are $1,609$ meters in a mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{1,609}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}}$\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$60\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of square seconds in one square minute, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$60(\\frac{s}{min})^{2}|= 60^{2}\\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}}; = 3, 600\\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5.1\\frac{m}{s^{2}} &= 5.1\\frac{m}{s^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1, 609}\\frac{mi}{m} \\cdot 3, 600\\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}} \\\\\n&= (5.1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{1, 609} \\cdot 3, 600)\\frac{m}{s^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{mi}{m} \\cdot \\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5.1 \\cdot 3, 600}{1, 609}\\frac{mi}{min^{2}} \\\\\n&\\approx 11.4\\frac{mi}{min^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the train is accelerating at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n11.4\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212979 1069 "e-19837" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212979 1071 "![](images/7b/7b93681ec027d67e445027465f4b4099b61551a8bb9173ba551536c19eac89d5.png)\n\nThe two dot plots above show the distributions of datasets $P$ and $Q$. Compare the means and medians of both data sets." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212979 1072 #uuid "8c4f5293-d217-52ed-8ae6-085bc90b5836" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212979 1074 "First, notice that dataset $Q$ is the result of changing the value of several data points from dataset $P$.\n\nWhen some data points are changed, the mean of the dataset moves *in the same direction* as the net change in the values:\n\n- If there is a net increase, the overall mean increases.\n- If there is a net decrease, the overall mean decreases.\n- If there is no net change, the overall mean stays the same.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The data points in dataset $P$ but not in dataset $Q$ (old data points) have values ${\\color{red}22}$ and ${\\color{red}24}$.\n- Similarly, the data points in dataset $Q$ but not in dataset $P$ (new data points) have values ${\\color{blue}28}$ and ${\\color{blue}30}$.\n\n![](images/d1/d191d44b2b09b634f3442f2b8d3d2d7b5a569d2cfcb0f03de02541f6706315a8.png)\n\nHence, the net change in the values is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsum of new values - sum of old values &= (28 + 30) - (22 + 24) \\\\\n&= 58 - 46 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the net change is positive, there is an overall net increase. As a result, the overall mean will *increase* when the data points' values are changed.\n\nTherefore, the mean of dataset P is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\textrm{less than}}$ the mean of dataset $Q$ because there is a $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\textrm{net increase}}$ in the values from dataset $P$ to dataset $Q$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212980 1069 "e-19838" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212980 1071 "![](images/79/79a1beb08224ad6b63f66b2a3425901d2188bbb5d91451ca51a9e78c21ddbb13.png)\n\nThe two dot plots above show the distributions of datasets $U$ and $V$, where dataset $V$ is formed by removing a single data point from dataset $U$. Which dataset has the greater mean?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212980 1072 #uuid "faa5b576-0d21-5e47-a904-add06a54b2a0" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212980 1074 "First, notice that dataset $V$ is the result of removing a data point with a value of $70$ from dataset $U$.\n\nWhen a new data point is removed, the mean of the dataset moves *away from* that data point:\n\n- If the removed value is *less* than the current mean, the overall mean *increases.*\n- If the removed value is *greater* than the current mean, the overall mean *decreases.*\n\nBy examining the dot plot, we estimate that the mean of dataset $U$ is *approximately* $82.5$.\n\nThe removed value of $70$ is less than the approximate mean of dataset $U$. As a result, the overall mean will *increase* when this data point is removed.\n\nTherefore, the mean of dataset $U$ is less than the mean of dataset $V$ because the data point with a value of $70$ in dataset $U$ is less than the mean of dataset $U$.\n\nTherefore, dataset $V$ has the greater mean." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212981 1069 "e-20485" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212981 1071 "In the $xy$-plane, line $g$ passes through the point $(0, 2)$ and is parallel to the line represented by the equation $y = -4x + 1$. If line $g$ also passes through the point $(3, d)$, what is the value of $d$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212981 1072 #uuid "cf7dc667-1493-5d50-8b37-ebe1aba0f8c1" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212981 1074 "Because line $g$ is parallel to\n\n$$\ny = -4x + 1\n$$\n\nit must have the same slope, which is $-4$.\n\nSince line $g$ passes through the point $(0, 2)$, its equation is\n\n$$\ny = -4x + 2\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x = 3$ to find $d{:}$\n\n$$\nd = -4(3) + 2 = -12 + 2 = -10\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $d$ is $-10$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212982 1069 "e-20488" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212982 1071 "![](images/04/04bf5a4e4afcedbf2f936f2150e9d85108f6700406a9233817a98464cc69ca3e.png)\n\nLine $f$ is shown in the $xy$-plane. If line $g$ is parallel to line $f$, what is the slope $m$ of line $g$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212982 1072 #uuid "0850df56-86b5-54bc-a01b-9d6d5fe0352c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212982 1074 "To find the slope of line $g$, we first determine the slope of line $f$, since parallel lines share the same slope.\n\nFrom the graph, we identify two clearly marked points, $(0, -3)$ and $(4, 3)$, on line $f$ and use them to calculate the slope.\n\nThe slope formula is\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the coordinates, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (3 - (-3))/(4 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of line $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 2\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212983 1069 "e-20492" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212983 1071 "Consider the linear function $g(x) = -\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 1$. If the graph of $y = g(x)$ is parallel to line $k$ in the coordinate plane, what is the slope of line $k$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212983 1072 #uuid "d6c6ea1d-c12c-516e-ac27-96288a319b82" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212983 1074 "First, observe that the function $g$ is written in slope-intercept form, which is\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ represents the slope.\n\nSo, the slope of the graph of $y = g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSince parallel lines have the same slope, the slope of line $k$ is also\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212984 1069 "e-20838" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212984 1071 "A farmer bought some hens and goats. The hens cost $\\[math]3.75$ each, and the goats cost $\\[/math]4.80$ each. If the farmer spent $\\[math]26.70$ in total and bought twice as many goats as hens, how many goats did he buy?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212984 1072 #uuid "3c9a2d34-ca6e-5aed-b679-a0b82f27adab" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212984 1074 "We are told that hens cost $\\[math]3.75$ each, goats cost $\\[/math]4.80$ each, and the farmer spent $\\[math]26.70$ in total.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of hens. Since he bought twice as many goats as hens, the number of goats is $2x$.\n\nThe total cost of the hens is $\\[/math]3.75x$, and the total cost of the goats is\n\n$$\n\\[math]4.80(2x) = \\[/math]9.60x\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the equation:\n\n$$\n3.75x + 9.60x = 26.70\n$$\n\nNow we combine like terms and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n13.35x &= 26.70 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the number of goats is $2x$, the number of goats is $2(2) = 4$.\n\nTherefore, the farmer bought $4$ goats." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212985 1069 "e-20839" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212985 1071 "A snack company ships boxes of chips in two sizes. Each small box contains $7$ bags of chips, and each large box contains $12$ bags. A total of $213$ bags of chips were shipped. If $9$ of the boxes contained $12$ bags each, and the remaining are $7$-bag boxes, how many $7$-bag boxes were shipped?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212985 1072 #uuid "af41d986-87e1-536f-9cdf-685ba544f697" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212985 1074 "Let $x$ represent the number of $7$-bag boxes shipped.\n\nWe are told there are $9$ boxes that each contain $12$ bags, so their total number of bags is\n\n$$\n12(9) = 108\n$$\n\nThe total number of bags shipped is $213$. So, the number of bags in the $7$-bag boxes plus the bags in the $12$-bag boxes must equal $213{:}$\n\n$$\n7x + 108 = 213\n$$\n\nNow we solve for $x$. Subtracting $108$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n7x = 105\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $7{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 15\n$$\n\nTherefore, $15$ seven-bag boxes were shipped." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212986 1069 "e-20841" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212986 1071 "A language learning app charges a one-time registration fee of $\\[math]28$ and a monthly fee of $\\[/math]14$. If there are no additional charges, after how many months will a customer have paid a total of $\\[math]196$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212986 1072 #uuid "35be1756-938c-5a8f-837f-c87749dfb34c" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212986 1074 "Let the number of months be $m$. The total cost includes a one-time fee of $\\[math]28$ and a monthly cost of $\\[/math]14$ for each of the $m$ months, so we write this as the following equation:\n\n$$\n196 = 14m + 28\n$$\n\nTo solve for $m$, we first subtract $28$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n196 - 28 &= 14m \\\\\n168 &= 14m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $14{:}$\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{168}{14} = 12\n$$\n\nSo it will take *$12$ months* to reach exactly $\\[math]196$ in charges." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212987 1069 "e-20904" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212987 1071 "Molly makes homemade jewelry. She sells gold bracelets for $\\[math]80$ each and silver bracelets for $\\[/math]40$ each. Let $x$ be the number of gold bracelets and $y$ be the number of silver bracelets she makes this month. Find the feasible region if she wants to make at least $\\[math]1\\,000$ this month, yet she only has the time to make at most $20$ bracelets." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212987 1072 #uuid "a48fb414-3607-53f1-b9d6-715e282394f7" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212987 1074 "We start by writing down the inequalities and plotting the corresponding regions in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- The profit from selling the bracelets is $80x+40y$. Since Molly wishes to make at least $\\[math] 1\\,000$, we have\n$80x+40y \\geq 1\\,000$.\nPlotting this in the $xy$-plane gives the following shaded region. We have labeled the line $80x+40y \\geq 1\\,000$.\n\n![](images/14/1469324179861178cde6f5026a64936723aecbd882cb5d71a78e186096775bfb.png)\n\n- Also, Molly can make a maximum of $20$ bracelets. So, we get\n$x + y \\leq 20$.\nAdding this to our plot, we get the following. We have labeled the line $x + y = 20$.\n\n![](images/04/04a5ff11701d4024c9616eabf46f72e1dce34d48f25a72e6289390592bd538f4.png)\n\n- Lastly, since $x$ and $y$ cannot be negative, we restrict our shading to the first quadrant:\n$x, y \\geq 0$\n\nCombining all constraints, we obtain the following region:\n\n![](images/08/0811bb106b4b6afc427fda647a972f1f07813401d3de863c34b095528883b3c3.png)" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212988 1069 "e-20916" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212988 1071 "A facilities team has less than $\\[math]414$ to prepare a cleaning solution. To qualify for a bulk shipping rate, it must mix more than $170$ liters in total. Base solvent costs $\\[/math]0.90$ per liter and disinfectant concentrate costs $\\[math]6.30$ per liter. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the maximum volume of disinfectant concentrate the team can include while staying within the budget and keeping the shipping rate?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212988 1072 #uuid "51b900a9-9207-5301-94af-c07e55b06c58" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212988 1074 "We start by writing down the inequalities that model the situation. Let $x$ denote the volume of base solvent and $y$ denote the volume of disinfectant concentrate the team purchases.\n\n- The total cost of the solution is $0.90x+6.30y$. Since the team wants to spend less than $\\[math]414$, we have\n$0.90x+6.30y < 414$.\n- The total volume of the solution is $x+y$. Since the team needs to mix more than $170$ liters to maintain the shipping rate, we have\n$x+y > 170$.\n\nAlso, notice that\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 0\n$$\n\nas the volumes must be non-negative.\nSo, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{0.90x + 6.30y < 414; x + y > 170; x \\ge 0; y \\ge 0\n$$\n\nLet's now plot the corresponding regions in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/23/23a62cc94d24c625d809a3c70faa3137775b317363529a870156af836b9be5ec.png)\n\nNotice that the corner point on the top of the feasible region has the coordinates approximately $(121.667,48.333)$, rounded to three decimal places. So, rounded to one decimal place, the maximum volume of disinfectant concentrate that potentially could satisfy the given constraints is $y=48.3$.\n\nNow, if $y=48.3$, then\n\n$$\nx \\mid > 170 - y; = 170 - 48.3; = 121.7\n$$\n\nSince the inequality is strict we take $x=121.8$.\n\nSubstituting $x=121.8$ and $y=48.3$ into the first inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.90x + 6.30y &= 0.90(121.8) + 6.30(48.3) \\\\\n&= 413.91 \\\\\n&< 414✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, all the constraints are indeed satisfied. Therefore, the team can include a maximum of $48.3$ liters of disinfectant concentrate." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212989 1069 "e-20936" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212989 1071 "![](images/a9/a9c4ca29ebdcbe26f1724796dd4bef7e93fc613a8718fb1a3fd842186277c93b.png)\n\nA dispatcher recorded the delivery times, in minutes, for two courier routes. This information is displayed using the dot plots shown above. Which group has the larger mean absolute deviation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212989 1072 #uuid "10e00b4b-4747-575f-896c-1f7183a67d78" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212989 1074 "Mean absolute deviation (MAD) is a statistical measure that indicates how much the values in a dataset deviate from the mean.\n\nA larger MAD means the data points are more spread out, while a smaller MAD indicates that the data points are clustered more closely around the mean.\n\n![](images/da/daa15c0219b5f7e8a6f9f04f0442244890ac48d4df6fffdb4c3c28298c127daf.png)\n\nTherefore, since the dataset corresponding to the first group is more spread out, the mean absolute deviation (MAD) of the first group is greater than the MAD of the second group." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212990 1069 "e-20937" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212990 1071 "A streaming service analyzed the episode lengths, in minutes, for two different genres. This information is displayed using the histograms shown below. Compare the range, shape, and mean absolute deviation of the two distributions.\n\n![](images/6a/6ae175e26bf7b40bd8ed40e9ea9cc990a92d2ec126ef5c3bf81c96dfa0bacbc3.png)" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212990 1072 #uuid "565a7cf8-f384-512f-9c8a-f30bced77372" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212990 1074 "Mean absolute deviation (MAD) is a statistical measure that indicates how much the values in a dataset deviate from the mean.\n\nA larger MAD means the data points are more spread out, while a smaller MAD indicates that the data points are clustered more closely around the mean.\n\n![](images/16/16a51a3631de6d542689fb7664c43b7e800149dcbdada63fa733f4847743ed28.png)\n\nIn our case, the ranges of the datasets are the same, but the histograms have different shapes.\n\nNotice that the data points corresponding to the second group are concentrated further from the mean than the data points of the first group.\n\nTherefore, the mean absolute deviation (MAD) of the first group is smaller than the MAD of the second group." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212991 1069 "e-20941" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212991 1071 "![](images/de/de9389e29ea6dc21f53cd496a15321eebe8773f2968a42a2b03ada8021327091.png)\n\nA school recorded the number of books read by students over the summer, using a $1{-}7$ scale to indicate the number of books. This information is displayed using the dot plots shown above. Which class has a smaller mean absolute deviation (MAD)?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212991 1072 #uuid "5aa77e88-7131-567d-aa17-472d53f2445e" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212991 1074 "Mean absolute deviation (MAD) is a statistical measure that indicates how much the values in a dataset deviate from the mean.\n\nA larger MAD means the data points are more spread out, while a smaller MAD indicates that the data points are clustered more closely around the mean.\n\n![](images/84/845854d1c3a29ca6e82ae57816cb34fe15c240b7083c6d8abc2194be9699d783.png)\n\nIn our case, the ranges of the datasets are the same, but the dot plots have different shapes.\n\nNotice that the data points corresponding to Class B concentrate further from the mean than those of Class A, which are spread uniformly.\n\nTherefore, the mean absolute deviation (MAD) of Class A is *smaller than* the MAD of Class B." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212992 1069 "e-20942" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212992 1071 "A call center supervisor recorded the duration (in minutes) of customer support calls for two teams. This information is displayed using the dot plots shown below. Which team has a smaller standard deviation?\n\n![](images/03/03970e3741b6c5f155f3fa6f8689b8ead1ad02f4efd1f9dc93a3fd24a44caefd.png)" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212992 1072 #uuid "f6c0a9a2-0546-5a01-b81d-f11eef5397f6" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212992 1074 "Standard deviation is a statistical measure that indicates how much the values in a dataset deviate from the mean.\n\nA larger standard deviation means the data points are more spread out, while a smaller standard deviation indicates that the data points are clustered more closely around the mean.\n\n![](images/ab/ab2824f65ffbe38bc48601df7dcd315f99aae6a17f8518c9886993a947375365.png)\n\nIn our case, the ranges of the datasets are the same, but the dot plots have different shapes.\n\nNotice that the data points corresponding to Team $\\text{B}$ are concentrated further from the mean than the data points of Team $\\text{A}$.\n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of Team $\\text{A}$ is smaller than the standard deviation of Team $\\text{B}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212993 1069 "e-20987" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212993 1071 "$4(x + 3) + 2 = 7 - 3(2x - 5) - 1$\nWhat value of $x$ is a solution of the above equation?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212993 1072 #uuid "38eb4f46-df5b-5780-9b07-0cbdc0a3a8e8" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212993 1074 "First, we begin by expanding both sides of the equation and simplifying by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x + 3) + 2 &= 7 - 3(2x - 5) - 1 \\\\\n4x + 12 + 2 &= 7 - 6x + 15 - 1 \\\\\n4x + 14 &= 21 - 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation for $x$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 14 &= 21 - 6x \\\\\n4x + 14 - 14 &= 21 - 6x - 14 \\\\\n4x &= 7 - 6x \\\\\n4x + 6x &= 7 - 6x + 6x \\\\\n10x &= 7 \\\\\n\\frac{10x}{10} &= \\frac{7}{10} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{7}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\n$$\nx=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{7}{10}}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212994 1069 "e-20988" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212994 1071 "What value of $x$ is the solution to the equation $3.3x + 0.7(x + 4) = 22.8$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212994 1072 #uuid "c13ceed6-d84d-5ef6-a28d-3c071dca159a" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212994 1074 "First, we expand the expression on the left-hand side and simplify by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.3x + 0.7(x + 4) &= 3.3x + 0.7x + 2.8 \\\\\n&= 4.0x + 2.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4.0x + 2.8 &= 22.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $10$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot (4.0x + 2.8) &= 10 \\cdot (22.8) \\\\\n40x + 28 &= 228\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $x$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40x + 28 &= 228 \\\\\n40x + 28 - 28 &= 228 - 28 \\\\\n40x &= 200 \\\\\n\\frac{40x}{40} &= \\frac{200}{40} \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $x=5$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212995 1069 "e-20990" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212995 1071 "Solve for $p$ given that $14 - 6(p - 4) = 2 - 4(p - 4)$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212995 1072 #uuid "5b6bf557-6109-5cac-a604-f79e29636924" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212995 1074 "Notice that $(p-4)$ is a common factor on both sides of the equation.\n\nWe must isolate $(p-4)$ on one side of the equation. We start by adding $4(p-4)$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 - 6(p - 4) &= 2 - 4(p - 4) \\\\\n14 - 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) &= 2 - 4(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) \\\\\n14 - 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $14$ from both sides, and factor $(p-4)$ on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14 - 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) - 14 &= 2 - 14 \\\\\n- 6(p - 4) + 4(p - 4) &= -12 \\\\\n(-6 + 4)(p - 4) &= -12 \\\\\n- 2(p - 4) &= -12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $-2$ and isolate $(p - 4){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(p - 4) &= -12 \\\\\n(-2(p - 4))/(-2) &= \\frac{-12}{-2} \\\\\np - 4 &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the obtained linear equation for $p$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np - 4 &= 6 \\\\\np - 4 + 4 &= 6 + 4 \\\\\np &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\n$$\np=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{10}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212996 1069 "e-20994" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212996 1071 "A bookstore is assembling gift bundles for two genres. Each fiction bundle contains $12$ items, and each non-fiction bundle contains $5$ items. Altogether, $795$ items were packed. The equation\n\n$12f + 5n = 795$\n\nrepresents this situation, where $f$ is the number of fiction bundles and $n$ is the number of non-fiction bundles. What is the interpretation of $(f, n) = (55, 27)$ in this context?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212996 1072 #uuid "dd4d3210-6f8c-5777-8874-a382180e3cf0" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212996 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n12f + 5n = 795\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is the number of fiction bundles and $n$ is the number of non-fiction bundles. This equation represents a total of $795$ items packed. We are told that\n\n$$\n(f, n) = (55, 27)\n$$\n\nis a solution, so we interpret these values accordingly:\n\n- The number $f = 55$ refers to the number of fiction bundles.\n- The number $n = 27$ refers to the number of non-fiction bundles.\n\nTo verify this solution, we substitute the values into the equation:\n\n$$\n12(55) + 5(27) = 660 + 135 = 795 \\quad \\color{green}{\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nSo, this ordered pair means that if $55$ fiction bundles were assembled, then $27$ non-fiction bundles were assembled, and together they used exactly $795$ items.\n\nTherefore, the point\n\n$$\n(f, n) = (55, 27)\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the scenario in which $55$ fiction bundles and $27$ non-fiction bundles were assembled." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212997 1069 "e-21028" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212997 1071 "$10x + 5kx = 0$\n\nIn the equation above, $k$ is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212997 1072 #uuid "e4158443-c995-5f18-a845-180f083851b8" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212997 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n10x + 5kx = 0\n$$\n\nand told that it has infinitely many solutions. This means it must be true for all values of $x$.\n\nFactoring out an $x$ on the left-hand side, we write our equation as\n\n$$\n(5k + 10)x = 0\n$$\n\nNow, applying the zero product rule, we have that this equation has infinitely many solutions whenever\n\n$$\n5k + 10 = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5k &= -10 \\\\\nk &= \\frac{-10}{5} \\\\\nk &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212998 1069 "e-21029" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212998 1071 "Consider the equation\n$2x - 10 = \\frac{f(x - 5)}{4}$\nwhere $f$ is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $f$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212998 1072 #uuid "c4804a9d-3c7d-5c1e-aea0-478a9ba4c1f2" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212998 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n2x - 10 = \\frac{f(x - 5)}{4}\n$$\n\nand told that it has infinitely many solutions. This means it must be true for all values of $x$.\n\nWe begin by expanding the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n2x - 10 = \\frac{fx - 5f}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we eliminate the denominator by multiplying both sides of the equation by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(2x - 10) &= fx - 5f \\\\\n8x - 40 &= fx - 5f\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the variable terms. Subtracting $fx$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n8x - fx - 40 = -5f\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n(8 - f)x - 40 = -5f\n$$\n\nFor the equation to be true for all values of $x$, the coefficients of corresponding terms on both sides must be equal:\n\n$$\n8 - f = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -40 = -5f\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n$f = 8$.\n\nWe check this satisfies the second equation:\n\n$$\n-5(8) = -40 \\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nSo, both conditions are satisfied. Therefore, the value of $f$ is $8$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212999 1069 "e-21036" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212999 1071 "$8v - 7 = \\dfrac{32v + m}{4}$\n\nIn the equation above, $m$ is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212999 1072 #uuid "e72f5611-5811-57ec-9a77-1a36b25e9a60" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186212999 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n8v - 7 = \\dfrac{32v + m}{4}\n$$\n\nand told that it has infinitely many solutions. This means it must be true for all values of $v$.\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the equation by $4$ to clear the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 \\cdot (8v - 7) &= 32v + m \\\\\n32v - 28 &= 32v + m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the addition principle to subtract $32v$ from both sides, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n32v - 28 - 32v &= 32v + m - 32v \\\\\n(32v - 32v) - 28 &= (32v - 32v) + m \\\\\n- 28 &= m \\\\\nm &= -28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, for $m = -28$, the equation is satisfied for any value of $v$, which means it has infinitely many solutions." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213000 1069 "e-21040" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213000 1071 "$11x = 3x - 2x(6p + 2)$\n\nIn the equation above, $p$ is a constant. What is the value of $p$ such that this equation has infinitely many solutions?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213000 1072 #uuid "ff533622-5531-5db0-bc8d-d4d91f6d3020" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213000 1074 "Since each term has a factor of $x$, we put our equation in the form $ax = 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11x - 3x + 2(6p + 2)x &= 0 \\\\\n(11 - 3 + 2(6p + 2))x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we require our equation to have infinitely many solutions, we need $a = 0$. So, we solve the following for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 - 3 + 2(6p + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n8 + 12p + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n12p + 12 &= 0 \\\\\n12p &= -12 \\\\\np &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213001 1069 "e-21042" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213001 1071 "$\\dfrac{5}{6}ax - 4 = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 8b$\n\nIn the equation above, $a$ and $b$ are constants. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213001 1072 #uuid "38209cf8-6cef-55b4-93f7-ab986aa62599" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213001 1074 "For our equation to have infinitely many solutions, both sides of our equation must be equivalent expressions. So, we must match our coefficients and constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6}ax + (-4) &= \\frac{5}{2}x + (8b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis yields the two equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{6}a &= \\frac{5}{2} & and - 4|= 8b \\\\ a|= \\frac{6}{5} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} & andb|=-\\frac{4}{8} \\\\ a|= 3 & andb|=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\displaystyle{\\dfrac{3}{-1/2}=3\\cdot(-2)=-6}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213002 1069 "e-21227" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213002 1071 "If $3x^2 - 6x - 24 = 0$, what is the value of $x^2 - 2x$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213002 1072 #uuid "164fc4a2-9bae-58a7-8aea-32024f87328e" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213002 1074 "Notice that all the coefficients in the given equation are multiples of $3$. Therefore, multiplying both sides by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\ndistributing and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 6x - 24 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (3x^{2} - 6x - 24) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3x^{2} - \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 6x - \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 24 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed to isolate the expression $(x^2 - 2x)$. Adding $8$ to both sides in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 + 8 &= 0 + 8 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x^2 - 2x$ is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{8}$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213003 1069 "e-21228" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213003 1071 "Which of the following is a solution to the equation\n$(x + 2)(x + 3) = (x - 2)(x - 3) + 10$?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213003 1072 #uuid "d518291a-1dd4-5358-847a-8e9c29443dbc" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213003 1074 "By expanding the products on both the left-hand side and the right-hand side, and then simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)(x + 3) &= (x - 2)(x - 3) + 10 \\\\\nx^{2} + 5x + 6 &= x^{2} - 5x + 6 + 10 \\\\\nx^{2} + 5x + 6 - x^{2} - 6 &= x^{2} - 5x + 6 + 10 - x^{2} - 6 \\\\\n5x &= -5x + 10 \\\\\n5x + 5x &= -5x + 10 + 5x \\\\\n10x &= 10 \\\\\n\\frac{10x}{10} &= \\frac{10}{10} \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the given equation is $x = 1$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213004 1069 "e-22707" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213004 1071 "Calculate $x$, given that $\\angle{A}$ and $\\angle{B}$ are complementary, and that\n$m\\angle{A} = 3x, \\qquad m\\angle{B} = 4x-8^\\circ\\,$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213004 1072 #uuid "08284a5a-ce0a-5fad-af15-dfefde39faea" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213004 1074 "Since angles $\\angle{A}$ and $\\angle{B}$ are complementary, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle A + m \\angle B = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n3x + (4x - 8^{∘}) &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n7x - 8^{∘} &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n7x &= 98^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 14^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213005 1069 "e-22708" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213005 1071 "Let $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ be two complementary angles. If $m \\angle B = 2m \\angle A$, find $m \\angle B$." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213005 1072 #uuid "e6658f35-3049-5609-b4a9-ce058c396e2f" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213005 1074 "Since $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are complementary angles, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle A + m \\angle B = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubs