ue] [17592186213716 1071 "At a community center, $250$ members were selected at random and were asked in a survey about a proposal to extend weekend hours. All $250$ members responded to the survey. It was estimated that $42%$ of the members supported the proposal, with a margin of error of $4%$.\n\nWhich of the following is the best interpretation of the survey results?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213716 1072 #uuid "b483dfe7-7917-5663-a072-331e08ffdd22" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186212135 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213717 1069 "q-301812" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213717 1071 "A research team measured the average daily screen time of a random sample of teenagers and found it to be $28$ hours per week, with a margin of error of $2$ hours.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate conclusion that can be made about the population mean?" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213717 1072 #uuid "1a922675-c92e-5016-9218-a06c580d0082" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186212141 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213718 1069 "q-301816" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213718 1071 "A transportation researcher recorded the commuting distance of a random sample of employees at a company. Based on the data, the estimated mean commuting distance was $15.2$ miles, with a margin of error of $1.4$ miles.\n\nGiven this information, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. It is plausible that the mean commuting distance for all employees is between $13.8$ miles and $16.6$ miles.\n2. It is possible that some employees commute less than $12$ miles.\n3. It is **NOT** possible that some employees commute $17$ miles or more." 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213718 1072 #uuid "f61c72da-5d9f-5c0b-99d8-ab5af11d8f05" 13194139533377 17592186045585 true] [17592186213719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186212147 131